window.AADS_KNOWLEDGE_INDEX=[{"title": "Welcome to the AADS Blog", "url": "./blog-aads-blog.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "First, we’d like to thank you for being a part of the AADS community! In an effort to communicate better with our clients and share important news and analysis from our industry, A", "text": "First, we’d like to thank you for being a part of the AADS community! In an effort to communicate better with our clients and share important news and analysis from our industry, AADS will be releasing a newsletter and a blog from this point forward. In this blog, we’ll give you a brief introduction to the services, advantages, and company culture that have led to our success. AADS Services At AADS, we have over 40 years of combined experience in the field of public records. We support the real estate industry, lenders, attorneys, and businesses nationwide with document abstracting, document searches, document delivery, and process service. We deal with time-sensitive projects, and our key to success has been our rapid turnaround time—usually between 24 to 48 hours. Our services include: Searches AADS helps clients mitigate risk with state and federal searches, including bankruptcy, criminal records, civil records, and court judgments. We also perform thorough lien searches on individuals, corporations, and properties. UCC, Corporate and registered agent services AADS’ serves as a registered agent, using our research team to perform UCC and lien searches. We handle document delivery and process of service as well. Document abstracting We protect lenders, corporations, and real estate professionals with thorough examination and abstraction of legal, financial, and public records. Our document abstraction services increase client efficiency and productivity, all while providing peace of mind. Process servicing and skip tracing We have a licensed team of process servers and skip tracers who work diligently, making sure court summons and other legal documents are delivered quickly and legally to the correct individuals. Our Place in the Industry Technological advances in the last 15 years have altered courthouse services to the very core, and we’ve taken advantage of every opportunity to decrease turnaround time and increase efficiency. Another aspect of our business we constantly strive to improve is our customer service. Our research team provides the same dedication and thoroughness on every project and shows the same respect to every client. New AADS Content and a New Website AADS has a brand-new, user-friendly website, thanks to our digital agency The Rebel Unicorn. Future blogs will feature news and analysis that are pertinent to all of our clientele—real estate agents, brokers, lenders, and attorneys. We’re also launching a newsletter where you can read more about our trade. Find out more information on our homepage, or call 866-318-8638 to speak with a representative."}, {"title": "Holy Cow! Agricultural Liens vs UCC Secured Livestock", "url": "./blog-agricultural-liens.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "If you’ve ever asked yourself the question “Is it possible to secure a loan with livestock?” then you’re in the right place. The relevant place to look is under “Farm products” in ", "text": "If you’ve ever asked yourself the question “Is it possible to secure a loan with livestock?” then you’re in the right place. The relevant place to look is under “Farm products” in Article 9 of the UCC, which governs security interests and financing for everything from unprocessed livestock to raw peas and carrots. The statutory framework that guides security interests for agricultural financing is distinct and therefore quite confusing. In order to maintain priority position, lenders and other secured parties must understand the difference between a UCC security interest in farm products and agricultural liens. Agricultural Liens vs. UCC Secured Livestock The goal of both agricultural liens and UCC security interests in farm products is to create an obligation for payment, but the context is different and they accomplish this goal in different ways. A security interest in farm products is created under the UCC; it emerges as a result of an agreement between parties, typically banks/commercial lenders and farmers. Agricultural liens, like PACA liens, are generally used by suppliers. Like other types of liens, they are triggered by state statute, with or without the consent of the debtor. They do not require possession of the farm products themselves. UCC Security Interests in Farm Products Banks and other secured parties generally perfect their security interests in farm products with a UCC-1 filing. The collateral description in these financing statements may describe the farm products: specifically, by type, or with a generic catch-all phrase like “all assets.” Unique Livestock Rules: Purchase Money Security Interest Now, if the loan is to provide purchase money for livestock—a Purchase Money Security Interest (PMSI)— the secured party is required to file its financing statement before the debtor receives possession of the livestock, similar to PMSI in inventory. The one difference is a PMSI notice for livestock has a much shorter effective period: 6 months compared to five years for inventory PMSI. Contact AADS for UCC Lien Litigation & Corporate Services AADS makes it easy to view all relevant risk information about any individual or company, either on an ongoing basis or on demand. We monitor everything from UCC liens to litigation and perform detailed searches, whether you need a Bankruptcy Search, UCC Lien Search, Patriot Act Search or Background check. Learn more about what makes us different from other due diligence providers with The AADS Advantage. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Everything you never wanted to know about aircraft liens, plus Autism Awareness Month", "url": "./blog-aircraft-liens-autism-awareness.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "News flash: Airplanes are expensive. Many are purchased with financing, which leads to the question: Is there a register for aircraft mortgages and encumbrances? We are glad you as", "text": "News flash: Airplanes are expensive. Many are purchased with financing, which leads to the question: Is there a register for aircraft mortgages and encumbrances? We are glad you asked. There is something called the FAA Registry, which provides a central system for the recording of conveyances, bills of sale (related to airframes only), contracts of conditional sale, leases, equipment trusts, notices of tax liens (other than federal), as well as any other lien or mortgage for: Any aircraft registered with the FAA Any aircraft engine with 550-plus rated take-off power Any propeller capable of absorbing 750 or more rated take-off shaft horsepower Liens and encumbrances also recorded in the FAA register include any aircraft engines, propellers, or appliances maintained by certificated US air carriers for installation or use in an aircraft, engine or propeller, as well as spare parts for the above so long as they are maintained by or for an air carrier at the location designated in the filing. Other interesting facts about aircraft liens? There is no requirement for US citizenship for an aircraft mortgagee. To be recorded, all aircraft liens must include the US registration number (in the case of aircraft), as well as the serial number, model, and make (in the case of propellers and engines). More on aircraft liens can be found in this excellent article published by Holland & Knight LLP AADS Raises Money for Autism Society of America It’s Autism Awareness Month, and we want to do our part. Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) is a complex developmental disability; signs typically appear during early childhood and affect a person’s ability to communicate, and interact with others. It can be debilitating, but (contrary to popular belief) autism can be treated, and early diagnosis and intervention lead to significantly better outcomes. Another important aspect of raising awareness for autism, unfortunately, is highlighting groups and organizations that are using the autism cause for selfish purposes. The Autism Society of America (ASA), the nation’s leading grassroots autism organization for over 50 years, recently reported that groups calling themselves the “Autistic Children of America” and “Autism Awareness Foundation” have embarked on aggressive telemarketing campaigns, supposedly to raise money in partnership with the ASA—despite the fact that that organization has no affiliation with either group and does not does not use tele-marketing as a means of requesting support. To make sure you are supporting a legitimate organization, whether for autism or any other cause, check out www.guidestar.com and www.charitynavigator.com both of which rate charities based on how they use the money they raise. In honor of Autism Awareness Month, we are donating 5% of profits from all new-client orders in the month of April to the Autism Society of America. If you are a new client, we invite you to Create Your Order Here. Thank you for reading!"}, {"title": "Background Checks for Invoice Factoring: Due Diligence & More", "url": "./blog-background-checks-factoring.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Are you looking for a reliable vendor of background checks? Factoring companies often are. Due diligence is a critical component of many types of transactions. Doing your homework ", "text": "Are you looking for a reliable vendor of background checks? Factoring companies often are. Due diligence is a critical component of many types of transactions. Doing your homework keeps you informed and shines a light on potential risks and opportunities. One such operation that requires precise due diligence is invoice factoring – the process of a business selling accounts receivable invoices to a third party, or factor, at a discount. In the due diligence process, a background check is an essential element to securing approval, used by many companies to verify the identity of a potential employee or client. In the case of factoring, the factor (the company deciding whether or not to buy the invoices) will submit a prospect’s relevant information to a third party that runs a background check. The ensuing report plays a significant role in the factor’s verification process. What goes into background checks performed for factoring companies? A concept called “trust and verify” is core to the success of invoice factoring. While a company’s customers’ credit history is the top concern for factoring companies when approving and establishing fund limits, it is also meaningful to know who exactly is collecting the advances. Enter the background check. It provides the factor with the necessary information about its prospect: current address and any previous addresses, known or suspected associates, and legal history including civil, criminal, and financial. Factors are most focused on any material that implies potential complications within the factoring relationship. For example, imagine a background check reveals that a prospective client has a criminal history, say check fraud. That presents a potential obstacle to funding as the factor could be reluctant to place trust in the prospect. For a better idea of what exactly a factory company looks for in a background check, the best way to find out is to ask. What issues in a background check might keep me from getting approved? Unless linked to an extreme situation like the check fraud example, prior bankruptcy typically does not automatically disqualify a prospective client from getting approved. Such issues may, however, cause the factor to investigate a bit further to understand the context of the circumstances. As you may expect, honesty is the best policy. Alerting a factor of any potential issues they could find in a background check builds greater trust between a prospect and a factor. The information you pass along will not have an effect on the final decision but will show the factor that you have a commitment to a transparent relationship. AADS is a national public records research company that is the top choice for due diligence work including background checks for factoring companies. We offer a vast portfolio of search products and delivery methods to suit any client’s needs. For more information on our searches and services, please visit our homepage today!"}, {"title": "Busy with Bankruptcy Searches? You will be after December Rule Change", "url": "./blog-bankruptcy-searches.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general", "courts"], "summary": "In any bankruptcy case, a secured creditor can file a proof of claim in an attempt to collect on a debt. Whether the creditor is a bank, leasing company, finance company, they may ", "text": "In any bankruptcy case, a secured creditor can file a proof of claim in an attempt to collect on a debt. Whether the creditor is a bank, leasing company, finance company, they may or may not be able to collect on their debt. Secured creditors currently have at least 3 months to file a proof of claim. But that is going to change on December 1, 2017. In addition to other amended forms, an amendment to Rule 3002(c) will reduce the number of days after the order for relief in which a secured creditor must file a proof of claim to 70 days — just over 2 months. With less time to file, many financial institutions stand to lose out on debts if they do not increase their volume and frequency of their borrower bankruptcy searches. Need for More Bankruptcy Searches, More Frequently This amendment will complicate things significantly for financial institutions that run bankruptcy searches for their borrowers on a monthly basis. In a worse case scenario, an institution could only have just over 30 days to prepare and file a proof of claim. The most obvious result of this change will be an increased demand for bankruptcy searches, as financial institutions that fail to file their proof of claim will lose out to other creditors and lienholders. This amendment applies a voluntary Chapter 7, 12 or 13 cases, and certain exceptions apply. A link at the bottom of this page will take you to the actual amendments published by the Judicial Conference. AADS is Your Partner for Economic, High-Volume Bankruptcy Searches AADS is a trusted partner to secured creditors, providing rapid and accurate Bankruptcy Searches and superior customer service. Our employees have worked in several different bankruptcy clerk’s offices and are experienced with the nuances of numerous search systems. We can obtain bankruptcy records quickly and provide them in the format you require. Visit our homepage to place your order. Check out the Pending Changes in the Bankruptcy Forms in their entirety here: http://www.uscourts.gov/rules-policies/pending-rules-and-forms-amendments/pending-changes-bankruptcy-forms"}, {"title": "Blockchain for Secured Lenders and Factors: It’s Happening", "url": "./blog-blockchain-secured-lenders.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Blockchain: secured lenders and factors better get to know what it is and how it can help their businesses. This blog from AADS will give you a primer.", "text": "A revolution in secured lending is underway, but it’s still in its early stages. The revolution is blockchain technology. The potential is massive, but at the moment it’s still in dial-up phase — think of the internet in 1992. Blockchain technology, the ledger system that makes Bitcoin possible, turns records into time-stamped blocks and doesn’t rely on banks or any central parties to verify transactions. Blockchain’s ledger is decentralized, distributed to all participants in the network who verify transactions by consensus. Soon after it was made public in 2009, Blockchain began capturing the imagination of the financial services industry — ironic because the technology itself was designed to remove them (middlemen) from the equation. Specifically, there was considerable appeal in having all parties to a transaction work from a consensus-driven ledger that no one party controlled. Settlement times would drop from days to seconds. All parties would have the same view of the data, leading to more transparency and efficiency. Parties could leave and join a transaction at will without tampering with the data. Six in ten large financial services firms now plan to employ blockchain or are seriously considering it, according to Juniper Research. Need a primer on what Blockchain is, and how it works? Check out this video Challenges Blockchain Could Solve for Secured Lenders The need to document a security interest in collateral Any and all changes to collateral, like shipment of a product or the payment of an invoice, must be recorded as soon as possible. Fraud must be avoided, and at least detected very quickly There is a widespread need for greater efficiency Blockchain technology, in one way or another, could go a long way in ameliorating, or outright solving, every one of these concerns. For instance, Blockchain could be used to document inventory collateral. The lender’s security interest would be tracked along with the asset no matter the asset’s status, whether it is moved from one place to the next or whether it is sold to another entity. Challenged Blockchain Could Solve for Factors In the factoring industry, Blockchain could be used to seamlessly record invoices and and document subsequent actions related to those invoices. Blockchain could also eliminate the need for a factor to match payments to invoices because the payer could easily link their payment directly to the invoice over the Blockchain. The transaction itself becomes the receipt. Fraud becomes much more difficult to achieve, and accounting becomes much more difficult to screw up — a good thing for companies in any industry, but especially financial services. Is Blockchain Right You? This is a complicated question to ask, but here are two questions to get you started: What could my company and all the other companies we interact with accomplish if we had access to a single, shared, secure ledger? Could whatever problem you have be solved by more conventional (i.e., inexpensive) means? To learn more about how Blockchain is disrupting the financial services industry, check out this article on The Secured Lender. Also on our blog: Can You Perfect a Security Interest in Cryptocurrency? Minimize Legal and Financial Risk with AADS AADS is a public records research company that helps lenders, financial institutions, and attorneys and their clients minimize legal and financial risk. We are the experts in boots-on-the-ground transactional due diligence. Visit our homepage to learn more about The AADS Advantage, browse our extensive UCC Lien Litigation & Corporate Services, or Place Your Order!"}, {"title": "Investing in a Business? Due Diligence is Your First Investment", "url": "./blog-business-due-diligence.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "In the world of business due diligence is a must. The most obvious reason for performing due diligence is to avoid bad business transactions. What many investors don’t know is that", "text": "In the world of business due diligence is a must. The most obvious reason for performing due diligence is to avoid bad business transactions. What many investors don’t know is that due diligence can also protect them from numerous unexpected legal liabilities. AADS performs thorough research covering financial, legal, and property related liabilities. Business Due Diligence Failure to perform due diligence can have severe consequences in business acquisitions, business investments, and business lending. By performing comprehensive due diligence prior to buying a business, the purchaser is: Evaluating the strengths and weaknesses of the target business to determine if it is worth the investment Identifying critical obligations and liabilities that must be discussed prior to acquiring the business Bringing unidentified and unseen risks (like environmental violations, court judgments, etc.) to light Indemnification vs. Due Diligence Purchasers and investors should acquire carefully drafted purchase or loan agreements that include favorable warranties and indemnifications, but it’s important to remember that these provisions do not take the place of proper due diligence. Indemnification is only worth as much as the value of the indemnifying party. If they lack a valid title to certain assets involved in the transaction—including real property—then indemnification will fall far short of being a curative solution. Complete and Total Due Diligence AADS helps lenders, investors, and business owners perform comprehensive due diligence on businesses, individuals, and properties. If you’re involved in a business transaction, AADS can give you a complete picture of the asset side and the legal liability side of the seller. We ensure due diligence with accurate and timely on-site searches and retrieval of business, real property, tax, and legal documents. Our searches include current owner searches, commercial searches, bankruptcy, civil and criminal records, UCC judgments, and mechanics liens. Visit our homepage today for more information."}, {"title": "Corporate Due Diligence Guide: Plain versus Certified Florida Corporate Documents", "url": "./blog-certified-florida-corporate-documents.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Whether you’re filing with the state to conduct a merger or acquisition or to incorporate an LLC in the state of Florida, you need to know whether your particular filing requires p", "text": "Whether you’re filing with the state to conduct a merger or acquisition or to incorporate an LLC in the state of Florida, you need to know whether your particular filing requires plain or certified Florida corporate documents. This blog explains the different kinds of copies required for different filing purposes. If you still have any questions about filing certified Florida corporate documents, don’t hesitate to contact us. What are Certified Documents? Certified corporate documents are records that include a filing office signature and state seal or similar identification mark. The identification mark on the document tells the reader that the document has been compared with the original and is considered an accurate reproduction of the document. What are Plain Documents? If you order a plain copy of business documents, you will receive a photocopy of the document that the filing office has on their files. The filing office will use a copy machine to directly copy the document and then send you their copied document via an email attachment or via standard mail. Many states have large electronic image libraries and can simply print out plain documents from their database. How Do I Know Whether I Need Plain or Certified Documents? In the majority of cases, you will only need a plain document when you are submitting copies to your state filing office. For example, if you’re identifying your corporate structure or showing the result of an internal review of your business, you will only require a plain document for your file with the state. The plain document format can also be used for establishing business timelines and for keeping the names of those within your business on file with the recordkeepers at the state filing office. You will require certified documents when records are to be used in an official legal capacity. For example, if you ever have to prove you legally own a commercial property, you will have to establish your ownership details via a certified document and then have the document on file with the state office. Certified documents lend credibility to the information contained within the file and help safeguard your business in various legal matters. Why Choose AADS? AADS has decades of experience in the production and sourcing of certified documents for clients across numerous industries. We are trusted by law firms, corporate clients and real estate services providers to provide commercial and transactional due diligence, commercial and residential title services and corporate due diligence. To learn more about what makes us different from other Florida due diligence providers, check out our AADS Advantage page. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "The Two Magic Words that can Save your Security Interest", "url": "./blog-collateral-description.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "In life and in lending, it’s the little things that end up being most consequential. A recent case out of New York showed just how important the language is in a UCC financing stat", "text": "In life and in lending, it’s the little things that end up being most consequential. A recent case out of New York showed just how important the language is in a UCC financing statement’s collateral description. Collateral Description Saves Security Interest A large bank makes a $1 loan that is secured by all the assets of the borrower. In listing the collateral, the bank includes the term all assets, but also goes on to list specifics, such as “any and all equipment, fixtures, inventory located at or related to the operation of the premises at 100 River Rock Road.” Seven years later, the borrower moves to a new address. While the bank amends box 2c of its financing statement to reflect the change of address, it forgets to change the address in collateral description. So the Debtor’s mailing address is now correct, but the financing statement’s collateral description is out of date. Shortly thereafter, the borrower files for bankruptcy. Within 90 days, the bank amends its financing statement and updates the collateral description to include “any and all equipment, fixtures, inventory located at or, etc.” at the borrower’s current address — but the bankruptcy trustee claims the security interest became unperfected after the debtor moved. What did the court decide? Although the address was incorrect on the financing statement after the borrower moved, it was irrelevant. By including the term “all assets of the debtor,” the collateral description was infallible; it included any and all equipment, fixtures, inventory located at or, etc.” regardless of what address they were located at, so long as they were owned by the borrower. The lesson for financial institutions and their counsel? Perfecting and maintaining your security interests can be a real challenge unless you have a cost-effective process in place. AADS provides that service as well as peace of mind. Perfect & Maintain Your Security Interest with AADS AADS provides UCC filing support and delivery to ensure our clients’ security interests are protected. We also provide UCC monitoring services, searching and verifying filing every single day to ensure our clients maintain their priority position. We have numerous packages, including our UCC, Lien & Litigation Due Diligence Package, and countless services to streamline the work of financial institutions, attorneys, paralegals and corporations. View our Services Place Your Order"}, {"title": "Corporate Filing Rejections: A Guide to the Most Common Errors", "url": "./blog-corporate-filing-rejections.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Corporate filing rejections not only waste time and money, they can also create legal and financial liability. Here's what you need to know.", "text": "Filing corporate documents takes time and involves key members of your team. Making errors in your corporate filing not only wastes time and limits the productivity of your staff, it can also lead to financial loss and legal liability. So, it’s important to limit corporate filing rejections as much as possible. Our team at AADS can help guide you in managing your corporate filings. In this new post, we’re highlighting the most common errors companies make when filing corporate documents. Corporate Name Not Available If you’re starting a new business and filing corporate documents with your local state, you may find that the corporate name you chose has already been taken. States will not allow multiple business entities to use the same name, and they will reject the document if the name has been taken. Make sure you work with your local state authorities to check your business name before filing the documents. Missing Signatures In some cases, companies file documents with key signatures missing. Each corporate document has specific signature requirements. For example, audit documents may require the signature of your accountant and the auditor. Make sure you communicate with the stakeholders for the document before filing. Most states will now accept electronic signatures, which can be provided via faxed and emailed documents. Poor Quality Documents When a document is considered to be of poor quality, it will be rejected. For example, if the text of the document is not legible and cannot be clearly understood, the state will reject the filing. The language around legibility is changing as technology advances. But most states will reject a document that is legible as a hard copy but cannot be scanned effectively for comprehension from their electronic databases. Failure to Pay the Required Fees When filing corporate documents, your company must pay the required fees to the state organization for document processing. If the state does not receive the fees at the time of filing, the document can be rejected. Failure to pay any of the following fees can cause document rejection: prepayment of taxes, special handling charges, certification fees, and counter fees. Tips for Ensuring Effective Processing of Corporate Documents So now that you have learned a few of the most common errors companies make when filing documents, let’s take a look at a few tips for effective corporate document processing: Have a legal professional review the document before it’s filed.Discuss the filing process with your state organization before filing.Ask all stakeholders to confirm the details in the documents when finalizing the process. To see how AADS can help you and your team clarify legal and financial risk, download our brochure. Why Choose AADS? Our team has over 40 years of experience as abstractors and public record search experts. AADS performs document delivery for a broad range of organizations, including law firms, title agents, businesses, and borrowers. We use state-of-the-art tools to increase legal compliance, reduce document backlogs, and streamline real estate deals. To learn more about what makes us different from other Florida due diligence providers, check out our AADS Advantage page. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Due Diligence Best Practices: UCC, Corporate, Legal, Public Records & Real Estate", "url": "./blog-due-diligence-best-practices.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "corporate", "property"], "summary": "Never before has it been more important to Know Your Customer. Federal and state regulations and their overwhelming compliance requirements have become the reality – the defining c", "text": "Never before has it been more important to Know Your Customer. Federal and state regulations and their overwhelming compliance requirements have become the reality – the defining characteristic of our era. In 2016 alone, the Treasury Department collected over $21.5 million in penalties and settlements for OFAC violations. Thorough due diligence and background investigations aren’t just prudent. They should be conducted without exception and include the use of onsite researchers. We understand the headaches involved in conducting due diligence. Paper trails seem to lead nowhere. Confusing abbreviations make performing accurate searches seem nearly impossible. Different filing practices complicate matters even further. We feel your pain, and we have a solution. Due Diligence Best Practices to Conduct in any Transactional Deal Research all jurisdictions in which the subject/ target has resided, owned property and has had business dealings. An investigation into public records (State and Federal Civil and Criminal litigation histories, UCC, State and Federal Tax Liens, Judgment Liens and Bankruptcies) Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC), lists known terrorists, money launderers and financiers of these activities A monitoring system Key Questions that need to be answered Who owns the business? Are they who they say they are? How long have they been in business? Are they licensed in the State/County/City? What is the corporate status of the target? Are they in good standing? Are there any UCC’s or Liens Filed? Are the receivables already pledged as collateral in an existing UCC? Are there any pending litigation cases? Is there a judgment against the business or principal? Does the borrower own any property? What is the assessed value? Do the business owners or partners have: A record of bankruptcy History of serial litigation & Liens Criminal problems Product safety issues Are they mentioned on official lists (OFAC) that indicate possible association with crime, money laundering or terrorism? We at AADS help simplify your workflow and mitigate risk in the due diligence process. For over a decade we have worked hard to offer solutions that address local jurisdictional search and filing challenges. Our services include: Legal / Corporate Due Diligence Services Apostille / Embassy Legalization UCC / Tax Lien / Judgment Services Bankruptcy Civil / Criminal Searches Monitoring Regulatory / Compliance Services Real Estate Ownership Verifications / Title Searches Courthouse Research and Retrievals OFAC / Patriot Act Searches Achieving Due Diligence Best Practices is Easy with AADS Contact AADS or see our Services to see how our comprehensive services make us a true one-stop solution for due diligence."}, {"title": "Due Diligence & Why the Right Documents Make All the Difference", "url": "./blog-due-diligence-documents.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Mistakes happen all the time. That's why the importance of due diligence can't be overstated. See why it pays to have the right document at the right time!", "text": "Ever heard of Murphy’s law, that if something can go wrong, it will? Mistakes happen all the time. That’s why the value of due diligence can’t be overstated. Some mistakes are easy to fix, while others are incredibly costly. The example that follows is one of the latter. Due Diligence Pays for Itself J.P. Morgan loaned a large sum to General Motors (GM) as part of a $300 million synthetic loan. In a separate agreement, the bank joined other lenders in loaning GM $1.5 billion. When GM finished paying off the first loan, it prepared the necessary documents to release J.P. Morgan’s interest in the GM property used to secure the $300 million. A Mayer Brown partner assigned the job to an associate and instructed him to assemble a closing checklist and drafts of documents needed to pay off the Synthetic Lease and terminate J.P. Morgan’s interest in GM property relating to that Synthetic Lease. In preparing that checklist, the Mayer Brown associate asked a paralegal, who was unfamiliar with the transaction or the purpose of a request form, to perform a search for UCC-1 financing statements recorded against GM in Delaware. According to the Second Circuit’s opinion, the paralegal’s search identified three UCC-1s. Neither the paralegal nor the associate realized that while the first two UCC-1s were related to the Synthetic Lease, the third was instead related to the Term Loan totaling $1.5 billion. You probably see where this is going. The associate and the partner approved the paralegal’s work and prepared a UCC-3 statement that released the interest that secured the $1.5 billion loan as well, which will be an issue if General Motors goes bankrupt and J.P. Morgan is told it’s in the unsecured line. So on the surface, it looks like Mayer Brown screwed up. Did anyone else see the documents? Of course they did. According to the Second Circuit, no one at General Motors, Mayer Brown, J.P. Morgan, or the bank’s counsel Simpson Thacher & Bartlett LLP, noticed the error, despite copies of the Closing Checklist and UCC-3 termination statements being sent to individuals at each organization. After signing off on the agreement to release its interest, J.P. Morgan requested the court reverse the deal on the grounds of ‘No Consequences.’ Incredibly, the bankruptcy court agreed with them. The Second Circuit disagreed. J.P. Morgan and Simpson Thacher reviewed all the documents and agreed to file them, so they assumed responsibility for their contents. What’s the point of making deals if the law doesn’t back them up? Lawyers have a duty to scrutinize what comes across their desks. At the end of the day, Mayer Brown is fortunate that it’s error worked out in its client’s favor. While embarrassing, GM can’t be too angry. Simpson Thacher’s inability to catch the mistake is far worse, especially considering the amount of money on the line. The moral of the story: remember the importance of due diligence in document research and retrieval. While a paralegal can easily make a mistake, a trusted document service company will not. Clarifying Legal & Financial Risk with AADS AADS is a national public records research company that has become a top choice for due diligence, document filing/retrieval, service of process, and background checks for a variety of clientele. We support the title insurance and lending industry with public records research and retrieval and provide title abstracts, legal due diligence, courthouse retrieval, and many different searches, including municipal lien searches, estoppel, UCC searches, and corporate searches for llaw firms, businesses, real estate agents, and private investigators. For more information on how we can help you reduce legal and financial risk, please visit our homepage or contact us today!"}, {"title": "Embassy Legalization and Apostille Services: Why Choose AADS?", "url": "./blog-embassy-legalization-apostille-services.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["corporate", "international"], "summary": "If you need Embassy Legalization and Apostille Services, same-day responses, and all around exemplary customer service, there’s no better place than AADS.", "text": "If you need Embassy Legalization and Apostille Services, same-day responses, status updates, and all around exemplary customer service, there’s no better place than AADS. Because let’s face it, if you are a CPA accountant, tax, corporate, probate, immigration or estate attorney, you have a constant need for the embassy legalization and apostille services. You don’t want to spend half your time on the phone with a vendor. You don’t want hand-holding. You don’t want to pay to do someone else’s job. So why pick an Embassy Legalization and Apostille Services provider with poor customer service? We Know Your Pain Points Are you sick of tacked-on fees? Delays? Being unable to get a real person on the phone? These are the kinds of minor annoyances that we don’t think you or any other attorney should have to live with. We provide simplicity and clarity on every order, along with prompt replies, frequent status updates, and consistent turnaround times. In our experience, this makes all the difference in the world! We Do it All In addition to being experienced processing any and all personal, legal, or commercial documents, AADS also provides Document Retrieval and Courier Services to expedite you or your clients’ foreign venture! We Don’t Skimp on Quality of Service It is what differentiates us from our peers. We invest in technological tools for everything from skip tracing to document retrieval and delivery and only hire people-focused customer service representatives. These investments have made us a top choice for law firms nationwide. Try Embassy Legalization and Apostille Services from AADS There is a better way forward, and it’s with AADS. To learn more about our accurate investigations, rapid results and competitive prices, please visit our homepage today!"}, {"title": "Due Diligence: Knowing Your Environmental Liability in a Property Deal", "url": "./blog-environmental-liability.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "If you are party to a property deal, don't count out the danger of environmental liability. The risk mitigation experts at AADS can help.", "text": "If you are party to a merger and acquisition or other property deal, don’t count out the danger of environmental liability. Inadvertently taking on a property that is riddled with environmental liabilities can cost you big time. The risk mitigation experts at AADS can help. All too often, buyers fail to perform their due diligence and are faced with unpleasant surprises. Under federal law, buyers can be held jointly or even entirely liable for damages, cleanup costs and the like when acquiring contaminated properties. In fact, they can even be held liable for the release of hazardous substances even if they were released long before the property transaction occurred. Quantifying Environmental Liability Given that the Environmental Protection Agency, or EPA, performed enforcement actions totaling more than $404 million in 2015 alone and that court-ordered environmental cleanup projects totaled more than $4 billion that same year, it is plain to see that environmental liability is a serious matter–and one that many parties to such deals fail to take seriously enough. Consider this: The average cost to clean up a contaminated site is around $26 million. Could your company or other business venture survive that? Even if so, would you want to be out that kind of money? Performing due diligence regarding potential environmental liabilities is therefore well worth it. Due Diligence Questions to Ask It is important to put as many tools to work as possible when performing due diligence under these circumstances. Come up with a detailed list of due diligence questions to pose to the other party, and get their answers in writing. Ask the other party to provide any documentation that they can to demonstrate that the property is environmentally sound. Most importantly of all, have an extensive public records search performed to ensure that something isn’t still lurking out there that the seller is either hiding or is completely unaware of for whatever reason. A lot is at stake in mergers and acquisitions and other property deals. Failure to properly investigate the matter can result in serious problems that can jeopardize your financial future. Make sure to seek help from qualified professionals so that you can move forward with confidence. Why Partner with AADS? AADS provides search and abstraction of a wide range of documents essential to the real estate, legal, corporate, and transaction processing industries. If you are concerned about environmental liability, look no further than AADS. Our accurate background checks, abstracts, and filings have helped to clarify legal and financial risk for thousands of companies and individuals since 2002."}, {"title": "EPA Ups Enforcement: Do You Know Your Environmental Liability?", "url": "./blog-epa-environmental-liability.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "With the Environmental Protection Agency stepping up fines and enforcement actions, the importance of performing due diligence is more important than ever for purchasers, lenders, ", "text": "With the Environmental Protection Agency stepping up fines and enforcement actions, the importance of performing due diligence is more important than ever for purchasers, lenders, CEOs, and other business people. In 2015 alone, Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) enforcement actions resulted in $404 million in penalties and criminal fines, $4 billion in court-ordered environmental projects, and 129 combined years of incarceration. Even if they’re eco-friendly, homebuyers and business purchasers could inherit the seller’s environmental violations—and the liability for those violations. Here’s how: Under state and federal environmental laws, the purchaser may be held jointly or completely liable for cleanup costs, damages, and other environmental response costs related to real property they have acquired. Average cost of cleanup for sites the EPA deems contaminated is $26 million. Real property acquired may decline significantly in value or become worthless due to environmental problems. According to the Comprehensive Environmental Response Compensation and Liability Act of 1980 (CERCLA), which has been amended numerous times, property owners and operators may be held liable for the release of hazardous substances like asbestos even if it occurred before they acquired the property. By performing due diligence prior to acquisition, the purchaser may properly structure the transaction to avoid certain costs and liabilities. AADS is a public records research company that provides exhaustive Universal Commercial Code (UCC) searches, Revised Article 9 (RA9) compliant searches, and searches at county recorder offices for Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) liens. Our accurate investigation, rapid turnaround time, and paperless delivery have helped to expedite thousands of real estate transactions since 2002. Visit our homepage today."}, {"title": "The Heavy Equipment Lien Search: Why You Need One", "url": "./blog-equipment-lien-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "It's essential to perform an equipment lien search before you invest in any heavy, expensive equipment. Learn why from the risk mitigation experts at AADS!", "text": "Title insurance companies typically provide research and assistance only for real estate properties. Heavy equipment and other vehicles are not covered under these types of policies. As a result, these valuable items may have undisclosed title issues that could create problems for purchasers at auction or in private sales. Heavy Equipment Lien Search: Why Does a Clear Title Matter? If liens are currently in place against the vehicle, they could threaten your ownership of the equipment. Performing a lien search is the only reliable way to determine if a vehicle currently has financial obligations attached to it. Without a clear title, you could be on the hook financially for debts incurred by a previous owner. What Are the Benefits of an Equipment Lien Search? A thorough lien search on any major equipment you are planning to purchase can help you identify serious issues, including the following: No lien history may indicate that the equipment was stolen or otherwise obtained in an unusual way. A lien search can sometimes provide information on the number of owners a particular piece of equipment has had. This can provide added information on its likely age and state of functionality. Any financial encumbrances on the equipment can be pointed out to the seller to reduce the price or to ensure that they are taken care of before the sale becomes final. By working with a reputable lien search firm, you can protect yourself financially and legally when purchasing used equipment at public auctions or through individual sales. Trust the Professionals at AADS The expert team at AADS can provide you with lien searches on homes, vehicles and heavy equipment. Our experience and knowledge can help you make the best possible choices in the competitive marketplace. Call us today at 866-318-8638 to discuss your lien search needs. We look forward to the chance to work with you!"}, {"title": "Are Your Company’s Background Checks FCRA Compliant?", "url": "./blog-fcra-compliant-background-checks.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Background checks are a company’s first step in evaluating an employee’s suitability for a position. With online checks the norm, are you FCRA compliant?", "text": "Background checks are a company’s first step in evaluating an employee’s suitability for a position. In recent years, instant online background checks have become more and more popular. The problem is that instant online background checks are convenient, but they’re not always reliable. As you know, one bad hiring decision can cause enormous financial and legal consequences. So what’s a company to do if their HR department doesn’t have the resources for an in-depth background check? Perils of Online Background Checks The main issue with the “instant” model of an online background check is that it, by definition, can only search records that are digitized and available online. While electronic record-keeping is becoming increasingly common, not all areas or jurisdictions have made the leap. Furthermore, many county courthouses that do keep digital records require that they be accessed via the in-house computer terminal. If a potential employee was arrested or convicted in one of these regions, the record won’t be shown on an online search. An online background check doesn’t just miss crucial information: it often throws up false red flags. Many online background check services rely on databases that were provided as-is that have not updated since the initial purchase. This means that expunged records or acquittals will be flagged as active cases. Much of the information you’re receiving could be months or even years behind! The Better Option: FCRA Compliant Background Checks The Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) governs pre-employment screening at the national level, but only when it’s conducted by third-party companies like AADS. If your HR department is performing the screening, FCRA rules are not mandatory. Interestingly, well-staffed HR departments are now performing pre-employment screenings under FCRA rules to ensure thoroughness, performing searches of criminal and civil court records and past employment driving records, and verifying education, professional licenses, and professional references. Working with AADS Most important records are available and accessible to the public, but not all records are easy to locate. For example, sealed files and purged reports are accessed only via a clerk for a fee. Our researchers go above and beyond, working at the county, statewide, and federal level to retrieve all pertinent information to provide: County, State and Federal Criminal Records Searches Commercial Drivers Record Searches Driving Records Reports from the DMV Education Verifications Previous Employment Verifications Personal & Business Reference Checks Professional License Verifications Social Security Traces For more information on how AADS gives employers, corporations, legal professionals and real estate professionals the power of rapid and accurate due diligence, please visit our homepage today! Image courtesy of Mark Van Scyoc/ Shutterstock.com"}, {"title": "Federal Tax Liens Unaffected By Local Foreclosures, Sometimes…", "url": "./blog-federal-tax-liens.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "In a September 2016 ruling, the North Carolina Court of Appeals has established that in the absence of any notice to the federal government, foreclosure for any unpaid local taxes ", "text": "In a September 2016 ruling, the North Carolina Court of Appeals has established that in the absence of any notice to the federal government, foreclosure for any unpaid local taxes has no bearing or ability to extinguish federal tax liens. Federal Tax Liens Unaffected by Local Foreclosures North Carolina is what is known as a “pure race” state, meaning that the first lien holder or lender to record their claim to a property has precedent over any other claimants. This encourages stakeholder parties to record their interest as quickly as possible in order to secure their claim in the event of a foreclosure or tax lien. However, the court’s ruling explicitly states that winning the race to be first recorded party “does not upset the rules of lien priority” as established by local and federal law — including cases where those rules conflict. In short, if a piece of property is obtained via foreclosure sale and the buyer does not notify the federal government, any pre-existing tax liens applied to the property are still in play. National Liens Generally speaking, liens — encumbrances on a piece of property that are incurred by unpaid taxes or other fees — go in a straightforward order of restitution. If the property is sold, the first (or senior) lien must be paid in full before any remaining funds can be applied to subsequent, or junior, ones; when the profit is exhausted, all remaining liens are extinguished. The NC ruling means that if a property has both local and federal liens applied to it, the fulfillment of a senior local lien does not extinguish a junior federal one. Going Forward The solution for foreclosure buyers? Clear communication. The NC ruling clearly states that notice to the federal government is enough to fulfill the buyer’s obligation, so a foreclosure purchase pursuant to delinquent state taxes (and with notification) stands a fair chance of extinguishing often-substantial federal liens. For everything from municipal lien searches and title searches to background checks and skip tracing, look no further than AADS. We have proudly served the real estate, legal and corporate worlds with rapid and accurate public records research since 2002. Find out more today at our homepage."}, {"title": "Financing Statement Errors Hurt: Here’s when they are still effective", "url": "./blog-financing-statement-errors.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Just how dangerous are financing statement errors? In short, some are worse than others. As a Secured Party, you know how much work it takes to bring in business, to approve credit", "text": "Just how dangerous are financing statement errors? In short, some are worse than others. As a Secured Party, you know how much work it takes to bring in business, to approve credit, to structure the debt, to negotiate and document the transaction. What you may not know is how little work is required to put your lien position in jeopardy. Though the UCC-1 Financing Statement is short, it is complicated. If it’s not properly completed, it may not be valid. Here’s what the Uniform Commercial Code (2010) has to say about it. U.C.C. – ARTICLE 9 – SECURED TRANSACTIONS (2010) › Part 5. Filing › § 9-506. EFFECT OF ERRORS OR OMISSIONS. (a) [Minor errors and omissions.] A financing statement substantially satisfying the requirements of this part is effective, even if it has minor errors or omissions, unless the errors or omissions make the financing statement seriously misleading. (b) [Financing statement seriously misleading.] Except as otherwise provided in subsection (c), a financing statement that fails sufficiently to provide the name of the debtor in accordance with Section 9-503(a) is seriously misleading. (c) [Financing statement not seriously misleading.] If a search of the records of the filing office under the debtor’s correct name, using the filing office’s standard search logic, if any, would disclose a financing statement that fails sufficiently to provide the name of the debtor in accordance with Section 9-503(a), the name provided does not make the financing statement seriously misleading. (d) [“Debtor’s correct name.”] For purposes of Section 9-508(b), the “debtor’s correct name” in subsection (c) means the correct name of the new debtor. Your Partner for UCC-1 Filing, UCC Monitoring and More Need help perfecting security interests? AADS can help. We specialize in UCC-1 Filing as well as UCC Search to Reflect and post-Filing Searches to make your you have maintained your priority position. Check out our homepage to place your order."}, {"title": "New Law would expand Florida FinCEN Real Estate Transparency Requirements Nationwide", "url": "./blog-fincen-real-estate.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["corporate", "property"], "summary": "A new bill would take the recent FinCEN geographic targeting orders affecting three Florida counties nationwide.", "text": "In January 2016, FinCEN, a bureau of the U.S. Department of the Treasury, issued a Geographic Targeting Order that required all title insurers to report the identity of the “beneficial owners” in all-cash real estate transactions in Miami-Dade County when the purchase price exceeded $1 million. The goal was to prevent criminals and sanctioned individuals from hiding their identities behind LLCs and laundering their money via real estate. In July of 2016, FinCEN expanded the Geographic Targeting Order to Broward and Palm Beach counties, also for all-cash purchases of $1 million or more. In September of 2017, the order was expanded once again to include wire purchases. The most dramatic escalation occured in November of 2018, when all-cash LLC purchases above $300K were declared subject to additional reporting requirements nationwide. The problem with Geographic Targeting Orders is they aren’t laws. They expire and must constantly be renewed. Meanwhile, criminals and sanctioned individuals have time to react and change their tactics. But now Congress is aiming to take the program nationwide once and for all. Proposed Transparency Requirements for Real Estate Purchases The proposed Corporate Transparency Act of 2019 would require all anonymous LLCs nationwide to disclose their beneficial owners — removing a prime money laundering tool used by criminals and sanctioned individuals and creating new compliance requirements for the real estate and title industries. As it stands, exactly who title companies would report the beneficial owners to is undecided. The bill in its current form has reporting going to FinCEN, who would then share the identities with financial institutions and law enforcement, but other proposals where reporting would be made to the IRS have also been proposed. If passed, this bill or one like it would have a huge impact on the real estate industry. We’ll keep you posted! Clarifying Legal & Financial Risk with AADS AADS provides search and abstraction of a wide range of property documents essential to the title, real estate, and mortgage industries. We also serve the corporate and legal world with research and retrieval of a variety of courthouse documents, lien searches, background checks, and pending litigation searches. Our accurate investigation, rapid turnaround time, and paperless delivery have helped to expedite thousands of business and real estate transactions since 2002. Visit our homepage today to find out more!"}, {"title": "Who Can Claim Title to Property Using Florida’s Adverse Possession Law?", "url": "./blog-florida-adverse-possession.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Few laws are as confusing and fraught with risk as the Florida adverse possession law. Here's a primer on how to protect you and your client's property.", "text": "Florida’s adverse possession law is a unique law that allows a trespasser to gain possession of the private property they have trespassed upon. Court cases have established several conditions the trespasser must meet in order to take legal ownership of the property. As a property owner, real estate agent, or title insurer, it’s wise to understand these conditions so that you can protect your property or help clients protect their properties and lower your liability. Who Can Claim Title to Property Under Florida’s Adverse Possession Law? There are five conditions a trespasser must meet in order to claim title under the Florida adverse possession law: Hostile: The trespasser must be acting without permission. For example, if you give a landscape maintenance company permission to mow your lawn, they are not acting with hostility when they do so. Therefore, they do not meet this condition for adverse possession. The same condition prevents your tenants from making a title claim, so long as you have an agreement with them. Actual: The trespasser must have some control over the property. One of the clearest examples of actual possession is adding a new building to the land, such as a shed or a gazebo. Open and notorious: The trespasser cannot hide their use of the property in the hopes of going unnoticed. If you don’t notice a trespasser, they may still meet this requirement unless they took some action to hide their use of the property. Continuous: Florida law requires the trespasser to meet the other four conditions, without a break, for seven years. That doesn’t mean your neighbor has to stay on the property for seven years; it means they have to exercise control over the property for seven years. For example, if they remove their shed before the seven-year mark, then they may void their claim. Exclusive: Only one person can make a claim under adverse possession. So, two people cannot combine their time spent on the property in order to meet the time condition. None of these limits are written into law. Instead, they were all created through case law, or judges making decisions about specific cases. A judge may add further conditions to the possession law at any time. Even if your trespasser meets the conditions, you may still try the case in court, depending on the advice of your lawyer. How to Protect Your Property from Adverse Possession Most cases of adverse possession can be prevented by politely asking the trespasser to withdraw from the property. Your neighbor may simply not understand where their property line exists. If asking the trespasser does not resolve the situation, it is critical that you retain the services of a real estate lawyer to take your next steps. Streamline Title Search with AADS AADS can help you minimize your legal risk for adverse possession or help you transfer your client’s property legally by giving you rapid access to the title documents you need. You may also be interested in our full title searches, owner verification, and chain of title searches. Most searches are completed within 24 hours, and we provide paperless delivery. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "A Brief Guide to Florida Construction Lien Law", "url": "./blog-florida-construction-lien-law.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Construction is core to the heart and soul of Florida. The Sunshine State ranked fifth in the nation in terms of commercial real estate development in 2017, when construction spend", "text": "Construction is core to the heart and soul of Florida. The Sunshine State ranked fifth in the nation in terms of commercial real estate development in 2017, when construction spending totalled $9.26 billion, contributed $19.3 billion to GDP, and created 156,000 jobs. Though development has slowed down over the previous year, construction remains a major industry in Florida. Much of this is thanks to Florida construction lien law. Florida Construction Lien Law: Making Sense of Chaos Construction projects are as complex as ecosystems. A general contractor leads the project, hiring subcontractors for plumbing, electrical work, roofing, HVAC, etc. The direct contract between the general contractor and the owner is underpinned by a series of “downstream” subcontracts. These subcontracts are between the contractor and its subcontractors, and between subcontractors and sub-subcontractors and suppliers. Invoicing is conducted on a monthly basis based on either costs incurred or on the percentage of the project completed. Each party sends its invoice its direct customer who then bills upstream to their customer. Suppliers bill sub-subcontractors and contractors, subcontractors bill the general contractor, the general contractor bills the owner. The owner, meanwhile, may submit an invoice to a lender to obtain funding. Once the funding is secured, the payments go downstream: The owner pays the general contractor, who pays the subcontractors, who pays the subcontractors and so on. It’s a remarkably cumbersome process, and it’s a wonder to think how well it works when project costs regularly exceed $100 million. To a large degree the reason this system doesn’t collapse is because of the existence of construction lien law. Florida Construction Lien Law Common law states that only parties to a contract can enforce it. So what are downstream parties to do if upstream parties fail to pay, or if money somehow gets lost along its downstream path? What’s a supplier to do if their supplies have already been furnished, but the owner fails to pay the contractor who hired them? Or what if the owner or lender dispute the numbers being billed by the general contractor? The answer is something called a construction lien, which gives an entity a vehicle to enforce its subcontract or purchase order directly against an owner even though they are not directly in contract with them. Essentially it allows a party to leapfrog intermediate contracts and assert a claim against an entity with no direct connection to them. Uncover Relevant Risk Information with AADS AADS makes it easy to get an accurate picture of borrowers, identify any liens encumbering their assets, validate key data against public record databases, and achieve peace of mind! Our turnaround time is 48-72 hours, but most searches are completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. Check out our UCC Corporate Services or learn more about our AADS Advantage. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today! The materials on this website are for informational purposes only and not for the purpose of providing legal advice."}, {"title": "Is Your Vendor Complying with the Florida HOA Estoppel Law?", "url": "./blog-florida-hoa-estoppel.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "HOA estoppel certificates are an essential part of title commitment and of real estate due diligence. That’s why title companies and real estate attorneys in Broward, Palm Beach an", "text": "HOA estoppel certificates are an essential part of title commitment and of real estate due diligence. That’s why title companies and real estate attorneys in Broward, Palm Beach and Miami-Dade counties trust AADS with this important task. Senate Bill 398 and the 2017 Florida HOA estoppel law was great for our clients because it reduced the sometimes exorbitant fees charged by community associations and streamlined the process of requesting estoppel letters from these same associations. But it also created risk because of new requirements on the vendor side. This blog will explain the need for the Florida HOA estoppel law, the risks it creates for lawyers and title companies, and how AADS ensures that all the requirements are being followed properly. Why the Florida HOA Estoppel Law was Needed The main thrust of Senate Bill 398 was to cap fees for dues letters/certification requests at $250. Previously, many management companies and community associations were tacking on mysterious fees ranging from $5 to $50 with dubious names like “online misc.” Now, when an account is delinquent, associations may only charge an additional $150. Fees for expedited requests up to $100 are allowed under the law. Total fees for expedited, delinquent estoppel certificates from one association may not exceed $500. While the law is curbing the bad behavior of some associations, it has had the unintended consequence of causing some management companies that hadn’t been price gouging to raise their prices to the maximum amount. Information Required on an HOA Estoppel Certificate Though most management companies and associations happily provide all required information, our public records research experts know exactly what to look for and how to deal with the associations when they’re not adhering to the law by providing: Scheduled assessments levied against the propertyThe date the scheduled assessment has been paid throughThe amount and the due date of the next installment Itemized list of any other fees that will become due, such as special assessments Whether or not there is a transfer fee, resale fee, or capital contribution fee, and if so the amounts Any open violationsIf buyer approval is required by the board of directors of the community association Whether there is the right of first refusal and if the association has exercised that rightContact information for all associations of which the property is a memberContact information for all insurance of the association AADS is Trusted for HOA Estoppel Letter Services in South Florida AADS is trusted by Florida title agents and attorneys to deliver HOA estoppel letters in Broward, Palm Beach and Miami-Dade counties. You may also be interested in our full title searches, owner verification, and chain of title searches. Most searches are completed within 24 hours, and we provide paperless delivery. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "Florida Permit Problems: Why Quality Matters in a Title Search", "url": "./blog-florida-permit-title-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The Risk of Substandard Title Search Services", "text": "A longtime resident of Clearwater is currently facing thousands of dollars in fines because of the failure of the original owner to obtain the required building permits for a pool built in 1980. Working with a trusted partner can help home buyers avoid issues like this and can ensure the best outcomes for real estate transactions nationwide. The Risk of Substandard Title Search Services Janet Martin purchased her home in 1998, almost 20 years ago. Unfortunately, the title search performed at the time of the sale did not identify the issue. Since that time, the title company in question has gone out of business. The original owners of the property can not be located, leaving Martin on the hook for what could potentially be thousands of dollars in repair costs and fines. An electrical inspection for the pool will be required. If any issues are identified, Martin will likely be responsible for the entire cost of remediating the problems found. A Persistent Problem If issues with permits and liens are not addressed promptly, they will not disappear on their own. Most buyers will not take on a property with significant liens or missing permits; if a buyer can be found, the perceived value of the home may be reduced to account for the added trouble and expense these ongoing issues can cause. Working with a Professional Team The experts at AADS can provide the expertise and knowledge home buyers need to ensure that their transactions go smoothly and that no unpleasant surprises await them in the future. The skilled professionals at AADS can find even the most obscure liens and encumbrances to protect buyers, real estate agents and other parties from financial loss. Give us a call at 866-318-8638 to learn more about our lineup of services and how they can help you achieve your real estate goals."}, {"title": "Why Switch to AADS for Florida Title and Lien Searches?", "url": "./blog-florida-title-and-lien-searches.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "There are three compelling reasons why you should choose AADS for all your Florida Title and Lien Searches. Two are common sense. One is for a limited time.", "text": "Three good reasons: We treat you better, we can help you clear files faster, and we have an extended Black Friday sale on Florida Title and Lien Searches. Just place your order between now and March 2020 to receive a 10% discount on our Florida services, including: Municipal Lien SearchesTax ReportsTitle SearchesUCC Filing and Searches Place your order on our website or through the Qualia Marketplace today! Florida Housing Market Outlook The latest report from the Miami Association of Realtors confirms what we already knew. Transaction volume in South Florida is fast and furious. In Miami-Dade County, single-family home sales rose 7.5 percent year-over-year and sales dollar volume rose by a whopping 13.4 percent to $2 billion. In Broward County, single family home sales rose 4 percent year-over-year and sales dollar volume reached $2 billion with a 7.7 percent jump year-over-year. In Palm Beach County, single-family home sales rose 8.3 percent year-over-year, and sales dollar volume skyrocketed 19.6 percent to $2.6 billion. These increases are thanks to low mortgage rates and migration to Florida from high-tax states. Volume is likely to stay high for 2020 thanks to stimulative fiscal policy and low interest rates. Your Trusted Source for Florida Title and Lien Searches Since 2002, AADS has been trusted by Florida title agents and attorneys to deliver title searches (current owner, two owner, full searches), municipal lien searches, tax reports and UCC filings and searches. We also provide HOA estoppel letters in Broward, Palm Beach and Miami-Dade counties. Most searches are completed within 24 hours, and we provide paperless delivery. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "Florida Title Industry Alert: Important Legislative Updates", "url": "./blog-florida-title-industry.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Important legislative changes have taken place that will impact the Florida title industry. Here's what you need to know.", "text": "Recent changes to Florida title laws could have an impact on your real estate transactions. Our team at AADS works to help you stay informed about the latest legislative changes. Within this post, we’ll explore several new updates to the Florida title laws and explain the impact they will have upon the local industry. SB 248 Public Records Exemption Expansion – Law Enforcement Home Address This legislation is designed to expand the current law prohibiting the publication of home addresses for law enforcement officials. The law removes the legal property description and the parcel identification number, as well as any other information that could be used to identify a person. However, it’s important to know that the person can waive their right to anonymity in cases where they wish to work with the Florida title industry. HB 447 Building Permits HB 447 requires local Florida governments to provide written notice to a property owner or contractor in no fewer than 30 days before a building permit is about to expire. It also allows the property owner to close a permit by assuming the role of owner-builder, upon follow-up approval from the local government. Another benefit of this update is that it sets out a clear process for property owners to close open or expired building permit by retaining the original contractor or choosing a new contractor to perform required work. HB 409 Electronic Legal Documents HB 409 has been designed to streamline the notarization of electronic legal documents in cases where two parties are able to communicate with one another, allowing the recording the transaction via electronic media. This has ramifications on the Florida real estate industry, where title changes require a witness to the signing of transaction documents. Beginning January 1, 2020, Florida title documents can be signed and witnessed via digital communications technology such as Skype or FaceTime. The technology allows both parties to agree verbally and share their signed documents with a notary. Online notarizations will be recorded in full and preserved for historical record with state officials. A key consideration within this legislation is the potential impact the new law will have on vulnerable people. In cases in which one party is deemed legally vulnerable or otherwise unable to consent to the signing of a legal document, there must be a witness physically present with the vulnerable person. This witness must be able to prove their relationship to the vulnerable person. Turn to AADS for Guidance Our experienced team can help you to streamline your real estate transactions and locate public records regarding local Florida properties. We have decades of experience in the industry and harness web-based tools as well as a nationally-based team to complete our work for clients. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "Warning! Florida Court Rules Some Warranty Deeds Can Bind Subsequent Purchasers", "url": "./blog-florida-warranty-deeds.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property", "courts"], "summary": "Let your buyers beware, if they buy a home in Florida the previous owner’s warranty deeds can bind subsequent purchasers to the terms laid out in those deeds. Take the time to expl", "text": "Let your buyers beware, if they buy a home in Florida the previous owner’s warranty deeds can bind subsequent purchasers to the terms laid out in those deeds. Take the time to explain this to your buyers and make sure they know what they are getting into! The Case Hayslip v. U.S. Home Corp., In 2010, the Hayslips purchased a home from the Kensingtons. The 2010 deed – which the Kensingtons did not sign – stipulated that should any disputes arise between the grantor (the Kensingtons) and the grantee (U.S. Home Corp.) they would first try to settle through mediation and if that weren’t successful it should be submitted to binding arbitration. Although unsigned by the Kensingtons, it further provides that the grantee, by accepting the deed, automatically agrees to its provisions on behalf of itself, its heirs, personal representatives, and successors. In 2017, the Hayslips filed a lawsuit against U.S. Home Corp. alleging that they had inadequately and improperly installed the home’s stucco system contrary to Florida’s Building Code Act. U.S. Home Corp. moved to stay the court proceedings and pursue arbitration in light of the preexisting arbitration clause in the original deed with the Kensingtons. The Hayslips argued that they shouldn’t be held to the terms of the deed. The Ruling Following a hearing, the general magistrate concluded that the arbitration provision in the original deed is a covenant running with the land, therefore binding the Hayslips, who knew of the original provisions in the first place. The general magistrate recommended mediation and/or arbitration, and the circuit court agreed. The Question Before The Courts Despite the favorable ruling for U.S. Home Corp., the court certified this question to the Florida Supreme Court: “Does a mandatory arbitration provision contained within a residential warranty deed conveying residential property from homebuilder to original purchaser run with the land such that it is binding on subsequent purchasers where the intended nature of the provision is clear and the party against whom enforcement is sought was on notice of the provision?” Streamline Title Search with AADS AADS can help you minimize your legal risk for adverse possession or help you transfer your client’s property legally by giving you rapid access to the title documents you need. You may also be interested in our full title searches, owner verification, and chain of title searches. Most searches are completed within 24 hours, and we provide paperless delivery. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "The Most Trusted Georgia Public Records Research Company", "url": "./blog-georgia-public-records-research-company.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "If obtaining quality public records research in Georgia has proved to be a challenge for your organization, you’re not alone. Georgia public records research companies have unfortu", "text": "If obtaining quality public records research in Georgia has proved to be a challenge for your organization, you’re not alone. Georgia public records research companies have unfortunately earned a reputation for failing to provide the caliber of information their clients require in a timely manner. That’s why our team of trusted research experts is filling the gap with cost-effective, boots-on-the-ground records research solutions. Our research process provides reliable, accurate records with predictable, dependable turnover times to meet the needs of your organization. You’ll have the information you need, regardless of your timelines or volume of work. Here’s how AADS’ boots on the ground researchers make public records research in Georgia easy. Full Compliance Our reports comply with the Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) as well as secure disposition of personal information laws. Our researchers perform primary research in-person visiting the proper clerk’s offices as well as the courts in Georgia in hand with state-of-the-art software. This provides you with the most accurate information. Turnaround times that usually take 48-72 hours are reduced to as little as 24. Dependable Background Checks Whether you require background checks for individuals, businesses, or assets in Georgia, you won’t run into the challenges research companies encounter using online systems or third-party databases. We avoid issues because our team uses screening solutions that are quicker. You’ll have the information you need to make informed decisions, using cost-effective background checks so you can avoid mistakes that will negatively impact your organization. We combine nationwide background checks with local searches, so you have the full story. Official Data Searches Our Public Records Searches in Georgia use official data sources to access public records. You can get quick reports based on records such as: Full NameAgeRecent AddressPhone NumbersAddress HistoryAKAs/Maiden NamePotential AssociatesPotential RelativesNeighborsPropertiesHome ValueAircraft / BoatsHunting / Fishing LicenseCriminal RecordsConcealed WeaponBankruptciesTax LiensJudgmentsAccident HistoryDeath RecordEmail Address Full Criminal History Checks Criminal histories come from different sources. We do a full criminal history check, so nothing is missed including: County Criminal courthouse to search records for any felony, misdemeanor or traffic offense.Federal Criminal searches at the federal courthouse in the designated search jurisdiction.Statewide Criminal research to find felony and misdemeanor criminal incidents in the designated state.Sex Offender Registry to find listed sex offenders. Verification Reports for Employment We understand how easy it is for potential new hires to misrepresent themselves on their resumes. We will confirm all information provided including references and professional licenses as well as: Driving Records (DMV)Commercial Drivers Record SearchEducation VerificationEmployment VerificationPersonal & Business Reference ChecksProfessional License VerificationsSocial Security Trace Efficient Property Searches We address all aspects of property searches to capture multiple records on real estate transactions including: Real estate, tax, and deed recordsProperty descriptionLand descriptionMarket and assessed valueSale priceCurrent property ownersOutstanding liens on the propertyAnything that can cloud title and affect ownership Business Searches You can make smarter, more profitable decisions with the right information whether you’re selecting business partners, suppliers, or customers. Our thorough business searches cover: BankruptciesJudgmentsLawsuitsLiensCorporate recordsAssetsExecutive namesBusiness addresses Reputation Management Negative news and Social Media Searches help to manage your digital reputation. We uncover potentially damaging information that can put you at legal or financial risk. Patriot Act / OFAC Search Without proper research, business, real estate, financial, or insurance-related transactions can unknowingly violate the Patriot Act and/or OFAC Regulations. We will ensure you are not supporting or conducting business with a sanctioned individual or institution to avoid severe civil and criminal penalties. Clarifying Legal & Financial Risk with AADS AADS is a national public records research company. We have become a top choice for due diligence, document filing/retrieval, service of process, and background checks in Georgia. We support the title insurance and lending industry with public records research and retrieval and provide title abstracts, legal due diligence, courthouse retrieval, and many different searches, including municipal lien searches, estoppel, UCC searches, and corporate searches for law firms, businesses, real estate agents, and private investigators. For more information on how we can help you reduce legal and financial risk, please visit our homepage or contact us today!"}, {"title": "AADS is Your Source for Georgia Title Searches and Municipal Lien Searches", "url": "./blog-georgia-title-searches-and-municipal-lien-searches.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "AADS has decades of experience providing Law Firms, Title Agencies and Title Service Providers with Georgia title searches and municipal lien searches. We will present you with all", "text": "AADS has decades of experience providing Law Firms, Title Agencies and Title Service Providers with Georgia title searches and municipal lien searches. We will present you with all the information you and your client’s need to know before completing a transaction. The Type of Information AADS Can Provide We can provide you with a current owner search, which covers the current vesting deed for the property. The current owner search will provide details on any judgments and liens against the present owners. Our team can find out more about the tax status of the property, including delinquencies against the owners. We can complete a chain of title search for commercial properties. This process encompasses all leases and contracts that may affect the title of the building. Within our commercial searches, we can also find out about liens, assignments, and open mortgages associated with the building. Requirements for Georgia Title Searches Under state law, a record title covering a period of 50 years is considered sufficient to determine property marketability. That is true unless an agent consults a local underwriter for approval related to a reduced search time on the property. The Marketable Record Title Act has a 40-year title search requirement but follows the guidelines set out by state law. A marketable title is “free from reasonable doubt or any sort of threat of litigation.” Turn to AADS for Georgia Title Searches and Municipal Lien Searches Our team has decades of experience in the title search process and can find out crucial details that might impact your upcoming property transactions. In addition to being the preferred service provider for municipal lien searches for Law Firms, Title Agencies and Title Service Providers in Georgia, we have access to the following via the Real Estate Georgia Deed System: Deed Indexes – all counties in GeorgiaDeed Images – all counties in GeorgiaConveyance Transfer Tax Data (PT-61) – all counties in GeorgiaTransfer Tax Images – all counties in GeorgiaGrantor and Grantee search features – all counties in GeorgiaStatewide, regional, or individual county search featuresBook and Page search features – all counties in GeorgiaSubdivision and Lot search features – participating counties in GeorgiaInstrument Type search features – all counties in GeorgiaStreet address search features – all counties in GeorgiaMap search features – all counties in GeorgiaDaily transactions search and reporting – all counties in GeorgiaSmartphone search capabilities – all counties in GeorgiaSales price data – all counties in GeorgiaCreation of user-driven search reports Our access to this level of comprehensive data means we can provide you with up-to-the-minute information about your client’s target property, ensuring all title questions are addressed. Our trusted and experienced team at AADS is here now to help guide you through the title search process. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today."}, {"title": "Yes, Our Georgia Title Searches Include Records Not at the Courthouse", "url": "./blog-georgia-title-searches.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property", "courts"], "summary": "When you order Georgia title searches, you receive information pertinent to the deed. What you may not see are all the other debts encumbering the property.", "text": "When you order Georgia title searches, you will receive all information pertinent to the deed and mortgage on a property. What you won’t see are all the other debts that could be encumbering the property. Only by working with a public records search company like AADS can you enjoy quick turnaround times on Georgia title searches that include municipal lien searches. Below we’ll share the issues these municipal lien searches can uncover and why AADS is the team to partner with for your Georgia transactions going forward. If you’re ready to order now, click here: Georgia Real Estate Package. Water Bill Liens on Property Liens on a property can prevent you from taking ownership of the property title and therefore stop you from completing the purchase of the building. In Georgia, liens can be placed on properties for which there are still water bills owed. At the current time, there is no public record for these liens, and so it’s important to work with our experts at AADS in order to complete your title search. Department of Revenue Liens Another piece of data that the state of Georgia does not record at the courthouse is Department of Revenue liens. These liens are the result of non-payment of state taxes on a property and will result in title transfer challenges for those looking to purchase the property. We can help you get access to this Department of Revenue data so that you don’t waste time and resources. Choose AADS to Avoid Transaction Delays One of the biggest challenges for clients completing property transactions are delays in the project. Any delay could cost you thousands of dollars in lost revenue and lead to you waiting several months for a transaction to be completed. At AADS, we’re committed to helping you avoid these challenging delays to your property transactions. A key area in which we stand out in the industry is our use of next-generation technology to quickly and effectively complete research for our clients. We scan thousands of databases in a short amount of time to find the information that you need to complete your transactions. We also employ qualified customer service professionals who can help to answer your questions and guide you in making the right decision for your transaction. Our speed of service is among the fastest in the industry, and we can often complete our searches within 48 to 72 hours of your first contact with our team. Each of our team members has a comprehensive background in the real estate and legal industries and understands the challenges that many local Georgia property buyers face. Building Long-Term Relationships Based on Reliable Results Our company has thrived over our decades in the industry by delivering reliable results to our clients. Whether you’re searching for title information, lien information, or historical records, we can find the data you’re looking for in a quick time frame. It’s why so many clients have been working with AADS for several years. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "How Heirs’ Property Owners Can Protect Their Land and Pass Clear Title", "url": "./blog-heirs-property-clear-title.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Heirs’ property is land that has been passed down informally from one generation to the next. When parents die without a will, property ownership is split equally by all tenants-in", "text": "Heirs’ property is land that has been passed down informally from one generation to the next. When parents die without a will, property ownership is split equally by all tenants-in-common, regardless of whether they pay the taxes, live on the land, or have ever set foot on it. Heirs’ property owners typically believe that their land will pass on to their next of kin. However, that assumption and dying without a will (or intestate) can leave owners and the next generation vulnerable to property loss. In order to ensure your clients are protected, trust the public record search experts at AADS (AADS). Potential Consequences and Risk of Heirs’ Property Without clear ownership, heirs’ property owners risk the loss of the estate. If the court needs to divide properties or sell shares, it may result in the family being forced to sell the entire plot. AADS is positioned to help clients streamline real estate closings, increase legal compliance, and expedite document delivery. What Laws Affect Heirs’ Property Owners? Fourteen states have passed the Uniform Partition of Heirs Property Act, which expands heirs’ rights in partition. Unfortunately, North Carolina, Florida, and the majority of states have not passed this act. It is more important than ever for owners to protect their lands, and we can help with a variety of property search packages. What Can Heirs’ Property Owners Do to Protect Their Land? At AADS, we’ll help you or your clients secure all property information. With our comprehensive knowledge of legal and real estate industries, we’ll be able to help protect your land within a quick time frame. We research and retrieve the necessary documents that relate to your property’s status, whether that’s ownership, liens, or otherwise. Create a Paper Trail to Prove Ownership If you’ve inherited your property without formal estate proceedings, we’ll help you file an affidavit of heirship with property records to establish your ownership. Write a Will for Clear Title Create a will or prepare a transfer-on-death deed to pass on a clear title to your family. Detailing the rights of heirship and leaving your ownership to the dependents of your choice will solidify land titles. Pay Your Property Taxes Ensure that all of your taxes are paid and that the address of the persons responsible for coordinating bills is up to date. If you pay for expenses on the property, such as home improvements, create a paper trail for them. Consolidate and Manage Ownership Use the names on the deed of your land to start a family tree of heirs. Legal documents from the county, like birth certificates and marriage licenses, can help you record all generations of legal heirs of the property. Once you have a family tree of heirs, consider asking if they’d be willing to transfer their interest in the property to those with the closest ties to the land. AADS can help with gift deeds, family LLCs, land trusts, and more. Choose AADS to Clear Up Property Ownership Our professionals at AADS can help you and your clients find a variety of critical property records. We’ll scan thousands of documents for you, helping you find title documents, lien information, and related property data. Our team has a comprehensive background in legal and real estate industries, assisting clients in North Carolina, Florida, and Georgia. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today."}, {"title": "Solar Customers Surprised by Hidden Liens", "url": "./blog-hidden-liens-solar.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "As public records search experts, we at AADS have seen all manner of hidden liens. SolarCity, one of the nation’s largest solar contractors, is now finding itself in hot water afte", "text": "As public records search experts, we at AADS have seen all manner of hidden liens. SolarCity, one of the nation’s largest solar contractors, is now finding itself in hot water after dozens of complaints started rolling in about hidden leans, inaccurate disclosures, and deceptive sales tactics surrounding the company’s zero-down, 20-year lease on solar systems. Many homeowners who signed contracts with SolarCity are reporting minimal savings. In some cases, electric bills have skyrocketed. What’s worse? Some consumers have received UCC letters from their banks notifying them that SolarCity had placed a lien on their home. Is SolarCity Part of a Larger Problem? This is a cause for concern, especially coming from a supposedly reputable company like SolarCity, which has received an excess of $500 million in tax subsidies as part of Obama’s green energy program. As you’ll see, hidden liens can be snuck into contracts of all kinds. Without a reputable and thorough document search and abstract company, these hidden liens can affect new homebuyers, sellers, investors, and businesses. Fixture Filing or Lien? SolarCity’s contract states that their UCC-1 Fixture Filing is intended to give notice of its rights to the installed solar system specifically, and that it’s not a lien on the home. According to lien laws, by attaching a fixture filing to a property, SolarCity would be second in line behind the original mortgage lender in collecting proceeds from a default. The bottom line is that banks consider fixture filings liens, and many homeowners who have entered into lengthy contracts like those offered by SolarCity will end up paying fees to their bank for the additional lien. Other contracts have provisions that allow solar companies to block the sale of a home until they convince the buyer to sign another contract. Arizona Lawmakers Offer a Remedy Legislators in Arizona have passed some of the nation’s most comprehensive transparency laws, many of which are designed to protect consumers from secretive liens like those used by SolarCity. The following requirements, which many people would expect to be mandatory already, went into effect January 1st. In Arizona, all contracts must contain: 10-point font with no blank spaces; The total price, including interest, over the life of the contract must be clearly stated; Potential tax ramifications; Disclosure of impacts or restrictions if the buyer transfers or modifies the property; Schedule detailing depreciation; An option to cancel for up to three business days after the purchase. Solutions from AADS While the rest of the country catches up with Arizona’s transparency laws, AADS will continue to offer accurate investigative solutions that mitigate risk. We are a full-service public records research and retrieval company that offers peace of mind to lenders, corporations, and real estate professionals across the country. For more information, please visit our homepage or contact us today."}, {"title": "A Quick Guide to the Laws of Foreclosure in Florida", "url": "./blog-laws-of-foreclosure-in-florida.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The laws of foreclosure in Florida are complex. This blog from All American Document Service will give you a quick reference point.", "text": "Florida is a lien theory state, which means the property acts as security for the underlying mortgage loan. The legal instruments that establish a Florida mortgage are called a mortgage, a note or a security agreement depending on the kind of property in question. Some properties may be encumbered with a mortgage and a security agreement, as is common in commercial real estate. Florida is a judicial foreclosure state, which means lenders must go to court to issue a final judgement of foreclosure. The foreclosed property is then sold in a publicly advertised foreclosure sale. A complaint is filed in court along with a lis pendens, a recorded document that provides public notice of the foreclosure. Uncontested foreclosures generally take between 180 and 200 days to effectuate. Florida has a statutory right of redemption, which allows a person to reclaim their foreclosed property if they pay the sum of the loan, plus costs, within a predetermined period of time. Deficiency judgements are permitted in Florida. A deficiency judgement is a judgement placed on the former owner when their foreclosed property does not cover the amount owed on the loan. The laws of foreclosure in Florida are found in F.S. 702.01 et. seq. Let AADS Simplify the Due Diligence Process AADS is the trusted due diligence partner for lenders and foreclosure law firms across Florida, Georgia and North Carolina. We provide on-site search and retrieval of all business, real property, tax, and legal documents in 48-72 hours, though most searches are completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. To learn more about what makes us different from other Florida due diligence providers, check out our AADS Advantage page. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Leases, Lien Searches and the Law", "url": "./blog-leases-lien-searches-and-the-law.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Mechanic’s Liens and Leased Property The legal ability for a claimant to file a mechanic’s lien against an owner’s interest in leased property can require a complex analysis. For e", "text": "Mechanic’s Liens and Leased Property The legal ability for a claimant to file a mechanic’s lien against an owner’s interest in leased property can require a complex analysis. For example, Missouri law states that anyone who performs any work on land for any building, or improvements upon the land itself, under contract with the owner or proprietor, or with any contractor or subcontractor, shall have a lien upon the building or improvements to it. Therefore, a tenant cannot rely solely on his or her landlord-tenant relationship to subject an owner’s interest in that property to a mechanic’s lien for improvements made by the tenant. Instead, there must be an established agency relationship. Complex Lease Provisions Express provisions within a lease can establish an agency relationship in the context of mechanic’s liens on leased premises. Disputes do regularly arise, however, when agency relationships are implied. Missouri courts look for a few different circumstances when determining whether an implied agency exists. They examine the lease’s covenants obligating a tenant to make substantial and permanent improvements, whether a tenant must make significant improvements to accomplish a specific performance, and whether improvements made provide betterment to the premises or substantially enhance its value. Mechanic’s Liens Filed by Subcontractors This analysis was clarified earlier this year by a Missouri Court of Appeals ruling in Crafton Contracting Company, et al. v. Swenson Construction, Inc. that considered if the owner of Plaza Frontenac was subject to mechanic’s liens filed by subcontractors that were carrying out work for a tenant. The Details The agreement between Crafton, the owners of the plaza, and Allen Edmonds, a tenant, required Edmonds to perform certain tenant improvements. Allen hired a general contractor, who in turn hired subcontractors. Allen paid the general contractor, but the contractor failed to pay two of its subcontractors, who subsequently filed mechanic’s liens on the shopping mall against Plaza Frontenac. The Finding Upon review of the lease, the court found that Edmonds was, in fact, an agent for Plaza Frontenac, as the lease required the tenant to make substantial and permanent improvements. The subcontractors were therefore entitled to file liens against the owner’s interest in the real estate. This case serves as a useful notice for tenants, owners, contractors and subcontractors to take necessary precautions before construction projects are carried out for a lessee. Lien Searches & More from AADS AADS is a public records research company providing UCC lien searches, municipal lien searches, title searches and much more to clarify legal and financial risk. To learn more about how we serve title agents, lenders and the corporate and legal worlds, please visit our homepage today!"}, {"title": "Tech Startup Scramble: Security Interest versus License Agreement", "url": "./blog-license-agreement-security-interest.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "We live in the age of the tech startup. Entrepreneurs with exciting ideas and nothing but a nickel in their pockets have created the next big thing and reshaped the very society we", "text": "We live in the age of the tech startup. Entrepreneurs with exciting ideas and nothing but a nickel in their pockets have created the next big thing and reshaped the very society we live in. Far more frequently, the startups go belly-up, leaving investors and lenders to divvy up what’s left. In many cases, lenders leave themselves unnecessarily exposed due to a lack of due diligence. A Battle for Priority Priva Technologies, Inc. received a secured loan from lender one, Pro Marketing Sales, Inc., while the company was in its early stages in order to develop a microchip encryption technology called Secured Key Storage Integrated Circuit (SKSIC). The security agreement drafted by Pro Marketing Sales, Inc. included a first-position lien on all Priva’s assets, including intellectual property. The collateral description also included all assets to be acquired by Priva. Investing in Encumbered Intellectual Property During Bankruptcy Fast-forward three years. Priva is filing for chapter 11 bankruptcy in Michigan. A key part of Priva’s reorganization plan was to develop its assets and use them to pay for its debts. Enter lender number two, Cyber Solutions International, LLC., who entered into a Design Service and Intellectual Property License Agreement with Priva in exchange for two payments of $200,000. Priva used this money to develop SKSCI (the technology which Pro Marketing Sales’ initial loan had financed) into a second-generation product called Tamper Reactive Secure Storage (TRSS). The license agreement granted Cyber Solutions International exclusive license to and other rights in TRSS. Pro Marketing Objects to License Agreement Obviously Pro Marketing objected to Priva’s licensing agreement when Priva presented it to the bankruptcy court. Their argument was pretty straightforward: the license agreement was subordinate to Pro Marketing’s lien under the security agreement. The court The court approved the license agreement but acknowledged that it could not stop Pro Marketing from foreclosing on Priva’s intellectual property. Priva continued its development of TRSS in bankruptcy as part of its reorganization plan. It didn’t work out. Cash shortages forced Priva to convert its case to a chapter 7 liquidation. Pro Marketing foreclosed on all of Priva’s intellectual property. Priva, in turn, terminated its agreement with Cyber Solutions. Cyber Solutions filed suit and against Pro Marketing, seeking a declaration that its license agreement was enforceable and that TRSS was not in the scope of Prop Marketing’s lien because it was a second-generation product. The Case Boiled Down to Two Phrases One from Pro Marketing’s security agreement, which granted a first-position lien in “‘all rights, priorities and privileges relating to’ copyrights, trademarks, patents and trade secrets, ‘as well as mask works fixed in semi-conductor chip products.’” The other from Cyber Solutions’ license agreement, which assigned to Cyber Solutions “all right, title and interest in and to any and all updates, modifications, or improvements” to any Priva technology funded by Cyber Solutions. In the end, the court sided with Pro Marketing. For one, their security agreement prohibited Priva from transferring any interest in the collateral without the prior written consent of Pro Marketing. Consent had not been obtained. Further, because the license agreement itself acknowledged the existence of liens on Priva’s pre-TRSS technology. The Lesson? You can lead a horse to water but you can’t make it drink. Priva was in bankruptcy. All information regarding its capital structure and secured debt was publically available. Cyber Solutions had all the information it needed to make an adequate assessment of the risk of licensing Priva’s intellectual property. It just didn’t make the right call. Cyber Solutions could have continued with the license agreement and tried to contract around the pre-existing liens by approaching the lienholder and making a deal. Instead, they ended up with a complete loss of their investment. Uncover Relevant Risk Information with AADS Knowledge is power and AADS is your extra set of eyes. We make it easy to get an accurate picture of borrowers, identify any liens encumbering their assets, validate key data against public record databases, and achieve peace of mind! Our turnaround time is 48-72 hours, but most searches are completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. Check out our UCC Corporate Services or learn more about our AADS Advantage. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Why Choose AADS for Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches?", "url": "./blog-miami-dade-county-municipal-lien-searches.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "AADS is the leader in Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches because we find things other researchers miss. Here's how.", "text": "AADS has become a leading provider of Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches because we understand the unique laws and customs of the county that would challenge an inexperienced researcher. Our Process for Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches AADS provides title companies and real estate attorneys with a comprehensive due diligence solution including municipal lien searches, tax reports, and UCC filings and searches for commercial properties across all the cities and counties in the State of Florida. We have become a leader in Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches because of our unique process. First, we determine the property’s current municipality and research the annexation history of the property to ensure there are no fees from a former governing municipality, which can happen if one city annexes a section of another. Next we confirm the property’s utilities, which may sound simple but is actually quite complex. Many towns in Miami-Dade County are not serviced for water by the county but by a neighboring municipality or county. This is usually the case if a property lies on unincorporated land. A simple county-level search of one of these properties in the Water Utility Lien System would yield no results on the property, but would not mean no municipal liens exist. The next step in performing Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches is to find the governing authority with the power to levy special assessments on things like road paving, street lights, stormwater work. In Miami-Dade County, this can be tricky because of the many Assessment Districts, previously known as Special Taxing Districts, wherein a majority of property owners have agreed to allow the county to provide public improvement and services via on-ad valorem assessments that are not covered via regular taxes. Once the property’s utilities and governing authority are clarified, we submit our request to the appropriate departments and return the results to our clients in a neatly organized Municipal Lien Search Report including a tax overview, special assessments, code enforcement history, permitting history and utility information. Simplify Florida Municipal Lien Searches with AADS Since 2002, our accurate investigations, rapid turnaround time, superior customer service and paperless delivery have helped to expedite thousands of commercial real estate transactions. Most searches are completed within 24 hours. Whether you need a trusted provider for Miami-Dade County Municipal Lien Searches or you need full title searches, owner verification, and chain of title searches on Florida residential properties, we can help. You may also be interested in our and HOA estoppel letters, which we provide in Broward, Palm Beach and Miami-Dade counties. Place your order today!"}, {"title": "Celebrating Minority-Owned Businesses and Black History Month", "url": "./blog-minority-owned-businesses-celebrating.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "AADS is proud to be a minority-owned business, and we want to take this opportunity to support other minority-owned businesses. Minority-Owned Businesses Growing 10x Faster than Av", "text": "AADS is proud to be a minority-owned business, and we want to take this opportunity to support other minority-owned businesses. Minority-Owned Businesses Growing 10x Faster than Average Minority-owned businesses play an increasingly vital role in the U.S. economy. Over the past ten years in particular, they have been a steady and reliable engine of economic growth. Between 2007 and 2017 minority-owned small businesses grew 79%. That’s 10x faster than the average U.S. small business over the same time period. Funding for Minority-Owned Businesses Still Lacking The problem? Despite these huge gains, and the fact that there are now over 11 million minority-owned businesses, many minority business owners still struggle disproportionately to obtain small business loans. Because of the ceiling placed on traditional funding for minority business owners, many business owners end up turning to the many grants that are available to fix the funding gap. Before Applying for Grants, Get Certified as a MBA or MBE Before applying for any grant, the business needs to be certified to ensure that the owner is who they say they are. Many forms of certification are available, either through public sector (National Minority Supplier Development Council, National Minority Supplier Development Council) federal government (U.S. Small Business Administration, Office of the Chief Counsel for the Minority Business Development Agency)as well as state and local governments offer certifications for minority-owned businesses. Join AADS in Supporting the NAACP It’s Black History month, which is why 5% of all new-client orders in the month of February will be donated to the NAACP. If you are a new client, we invite you to Create Your Order Here. Thank you for reading!"}, {"title": "Dreading License Renewal Season? 10 Tips for Stress-Free NMLS Licensing", "url": "./blog-nmls-licensing.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The end of the year is a time for rest, relaxation and, unfortunately, NMLS licensing. Here's how to make the process simple and stress-free!", "text": "The end of the year is a time for relaxation, meditation, and thankfulness for the season’s blessings. But for mortgage professionals, money transmitters, collection agencies and more — it’s also time to begin preparing for the annual license renewal process through NMLS, or Nationwide Multistate Licensing System and Registry. It’s important for both companies and individuals to keep their licensure properly updated, as well as minimizing deficiencies to ensure a quick and easy experience. NMLS Licensing Tips In the holiday spirit of giving, we’d like to pass along 10 tips to make this renewal season the easiest one yet: 1. Timing is everything. The renewal process is long and complex, so planning should begin — at a minimum — well before Thanksgiving. 2. Having trouble accessing necessary resources and documents? It could be your web browser. The NMLS website’s technical requirements make it inaccessible using outdated Internet programs, and this goes doubly if remote external assistance from Mortgage Loan Originators or Control Persons is needed. 3. Ensure that any open deficiencies are closed well prior to filing the renewal request. Even if the deficiency is resolved before the renewal is actually processed, the lingering record can cause confusion or even a challenge for renewal eligibility. 4. Many states have individual regulations that go beyond simply filing a renewal request online. Make sure to check carefully for any documentation required to be submitted outside of NMLS, as well as its associated due dates. 5. As of 2016, Control Persons (individuals in positions of power such as directors, CEOs, or general partners) must now undergo Criminal Background Checks — which in turn requires both MU1 and MU2 attestation filings. Be sure to allocate time for both of these tasks. 6. Also necessary for Control Persons is a current credit report, which requires a Control Person MU1 submission and MU2 attestation. 7. Another instance where starting early pays off: Control Persons should be diligent about ensuring branch managers and qualified individuals complete the necessary continuing education and Mortgage Loan Origination renewals. Failure to do so on the part of the individual could result in the main branch or whole organization’s renewal being delayed. 8. On a similar note, some continuing education providers take up to seven days to report course completion to the NMLS. Since many states don’t even allow the renewal to be submitted without this accreditation, make sure to plan accordingly. 9. As of 2016, some states require that paper documentation of surety bonds be converted into the digital format of Electronic Surety Bonds, or ESBs. The NMLS website has an adoption map and date table for those unsure as to whether they are in an affected state or not. 10. Make sure to read the fine print. NMLS renewal is a complex process that requires much effort before the “submit” button can be pressed, and it’s a popular misconception that the final deadline is January 31. Many states’ individual statutes actually require an earlier submission to allow for processing time. And if the renewal isn’t approved by January 1, all activity must stop — period. Clarifying Legal and Financial Risk with AADS AADS provides search and abstraction of a wide range of documents essential to the real estate, legal, corporate, and transaction processing industries. Our accurate background checks, abstracts, and filings have helped to clarify legal and financial risk for thousands of companies and individuals since 2002. To learn how, please visit our homepage today!"}, {"title": "North Carolina Lien Priority: A Pure Race State…With Exceptions", "url": "./blog-north-carolina-lien-priority.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "To secure your property, you must first understand the property rights laws in that state. North Carolina is a pure race state, which means that the first person to record a deed o", "text": "To secure your property, you must first understand the property rights laws in that state. North Carolina is a pure race state, which means that the first person to record a deed or lien against a property has priority over all subsequent guarantees or lien holders. Here, we’ll explore the pure race jurisdiction and explain why a title examination is still vital even in pure race states. Don’t Rely on the Pure Race Legislation The goal of the pure race legislation within the state of North Carolina is to encourage the prompt recording of all deeds. This means the state has the information in the shortest possible time frame. However, many now assume that because they were the first person to record their rights to a property, they gain access to the property above all other parties. While North Carolina is a pure race state, and the law protects the first person to record an interest in a parcel of property, there are examples of legislators ruling against those with pure race property rights. For example, regardless of which party recorded the deed with the state first, North Carolina legislators will provide “super-priority” status to local tax liens. So, if the local government has a tax lien on the property, it owns the deed to the property. According to the following piece of legislation, local tax liens have superior priority: “(a) On Real Property. –The lien of taxes imposed on real and personal property shall attach to real property at the time prescribed in G.S. 105-355(a). The priority of that lien shall be determined in accordance with the following rules: Subject to the provisions of the Revenue Act prescribing the priority of the lien for State taxes, the lien of taxes imposed under the provisions of this Subchapter shall be superior to all other liens, assessments, charges, rights, and claims of any and every kind in and to the real property to which the lien for taxes attaches regardless of the claimant and regardless of whether acquired prior or subsequent to the attachment of the lien for taxes.” Lenders and legal professionals should know pure race does not supersede the rules of lien priority that have been established under state and federal law. These rules will be upheld by the courts, regardless of who won the race to record the deed in the courthouse. Choose AADS to Avoid Transaction Delays Our professionals at AADS can help you find all the property information that you and your clients need to know within a quick time frame. Our team has a comprehensive background in legal and real estate industries and has a vast knowledge of North Carolina law. We’ll scan thousands of documents for you, helping you find lien information, title documents, and related property data. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today."}, {"title": "Yes, OFAC Regulations Supersede State Insurance Statutes", "url": "./blog-ofac-regulations-insurance.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Insurance companies must abide by state insurance statutes, obviously, but OFAC regulations supersede these statutes. Here's what you need to know.", "text": "Insurance companies must abide by state insurance statutes, but they must also sometimes “commit an ostensible violation of state insurance regulations to comply with OFAC regulations.” OFAC Regulations Preempt State Insurance Regulations The Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) is an agency within the U.S. Department of Treasury charged with administering and enforcing trade restrictions or sanctions on foreign countries and companies registered in those countries. OFAC maintains databases and publishes information regarding current restrictions and sanctions to ensure that Americans – including insurance companies – do not engage in financial transactions or provide services to Specially Designated Nationals (SDNs) or others on one of OFAC’s sanctions lists. OFAC Compliance Requirements for Insurance Companies According to the Department of the Treasury, companies like State Farm, All State, Blue Cross Blue Shield, and Aetna are: Under an obligation not to issue the policy if the applicant is an SDN Required to block payment if the SDN sends a deposit along with the application Barred from providing services to an SDN Advised to “review the specific treatment prohibitions associated with that list carefully before taking any action” if they receive an application from a party on one of OFAC’s other sanctions lists. OFAC Fines can be Substantial Performing an OFAC search is a necessary first step for insurance companies when they receive an application. OFAC searches are a tool insurance companies can use to verify the identity of their applicants, and to ensure that they are not on the SDN list or any other OFAC sanctions list, before taking further action. Failure to perform such a search can result in civil and criminal penalties, as was the case in Miami in 2014, when three health insurance providers were hit with a $129,000 fine for issuing policies to various individuals whom the Department of the Treasury had sanctioned under narcotics trafficking regulations. In addition to providing coverage for the sanctioned persons, OFAC said that the health insurance providers also issued support, including “a wide range” of marketing, administrative and operational services, including retention of agents, customer service and the issuance of claim and reimbursement checks.” How to Avoid OFAC Violations It is recommended to perform OFAC searches on new applicants to ensure they are not SDNs or on any other sanctions list. It is also recommended that insurance companies scrub their databases of current policyholders every month to ensure that none have become SDNs or been added to another OFAC sanctions list. Sanctioned individuals assets must be frozen immediately. Insurance companies that only scrub their databases every three months for OFAC compliance may be fined for taking too long to take action. If you discover that a policyholder is or becomes a Specially Designated National (SDN), contact OFAC Compliance immediately. OFAC will work with you on the specifics of the case. Clarify Legal & Financial Risk with AADS AADS clarifies legal and financial risk, delivers rapid and accurate investigations, and provides finished reports with seamless, paperless delivery. That’s why we’ve been the top choice for OFAC searches, verification reports, and background checks since 2002! Visit our homepage to place your order."}, {"title": "How to Run an OFAC SDN Search for Cryptocurrency Addresses", "url": "./blog-ofac-sdn-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Do you have to run an OFAC SDN search for addresses involved in digital currency transactions? As of November 28th, yes. In a long anticipated move, the U.S. Department of the Trea", "text": "Do you have to run an OFAC SDN search for addresses involved in digital currency transactions? As of November 28th, yes. In a long anticipated move, the U.S. Department of the Treasury’s Office of Foreign Assets Control (“OFAC”) formally identified the digital currency addresses of two Iranian hackers who had targeted government offices and businesses in the US and Canada and asked for Bitcoin ransoms. It is the first time OFAC has moved directly to prevent the illicit use of cryptocurrencies since signalling in March of 2018 that it would be actively monitoring emerging payment systems and blockchain technologies. The two Iranian hackers were added to the Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons (SDN) list on the same day, meaning all U.S. persons are prohibited from engaging in digital currency transactions with them. What does it mean? OFAC due diligence requirements remain the same even if your business is working in or with digital currencies. The anonymous nature of cryptocurrencies does not exempt individuals and businesses from OFAC regulations, especially when OFAC has made a definitive link between an SDN and a digital currency address. To order an OFAC SDN search from AADS, scroll past the OFAC FAQ below to: Order an OFAC SDN Search from AADS. Read OFAC’s FAQs on Virtual Currency To help answer any other questions you may have about cryptocurrency compliance, we are sharing the Questions on Virtual Currency FAQ, courtesy of the U.S. Department of Treasury Office of Foreign Asset Control: For purposes of OFAC sanctions programs, what do the terms “virtual currency,” “digital currency,” “digital currency wallet,” and “digital currency address” mean? Virtual currency is a digital representation of value that functions as (i) a medium of exchange; (ii) a unit of account; and/or (iii) a store of value; is neither issued nor guaranteed by any jurisdiction; and does not have legal tender status in any jurisdiction. Digital currency includes sovereign cryptocurrency, virtual currency (non-fiat), and a digital representation of fiat currency. A digital currency wallet is a software application (or other mechanism) that provides a means for holding, storing, and transferring digital currency. A wallet holds the user’s digital currency addresses, which allow the user to receive digital currency, and private keys, which allow the user to transfer digital currency. The wallet also maintains the user’s digital currency balance. A wallet provider is a person (individual or entity) that provides the software to create and manage wallets, which users can download. A hosted wallet provider is a business that creates and stores a digital currency wallet on behalf of a customer. Most hosted wallets also offer exchange and payments services to facilitate participation in a digital currency system by users. A digital currency address is an alphanumeric identifier that represents a potential destination for a digital currency transfer. A digital currency address is associated with a digital currency wallet. [03-19-2018] Are my OFAC compliance obligations the same, regardless of whether a transaction is denominated in digital currency or traditional fiat currency? Yes, the obligations are the same. U.S. persons (and persons otherwise subject to OFAC jurisdiction) must ensure that they block the property and interests in property of persons named on OFAC’s SDN List or any entity owned in the aggregate, directly or indirectly, 50 percent or more by one or more blocked persons, and that they do not engage in trade or other transactions with such persons. As a general matter, U.S. persons and persons otherwise subject to OFAC jurisdiction, including firms that facilitate or engage in online commerce or process transactions using digital currency, are responsible for ensuring that they do not engage in unauthorized transactions prohibited by OFAC sanctions, such as dealings with blocked persons or property, or engaging in prohibited trade or investment-related transactions. Prohibited transactions include transactions that evade or avoid, have the purpose of evading or avoiding, cause a violation of, or attempt to violate prohibitions imposed by OFAC under various sanctions authorities. Additionally, persons that provide financial, material, or technological support for or to a designated person may be designated by OFAC under the relevant sanctions authority. Persons including technology companies; administrators, exchangers, and users of digital currencies; and other payment processors should develop a tailored, risk-based compliance program, which generally should include sanctions list screening and other appropriate measures. An adequate compliance solution will depend on a variety of factors, including the type of business involved. There is no single compliance program or solution suitable for every circumstance. [03-19-2018] How will OFAC use its existing authorities to sanction those who use digital currencies for illicit purposes? The United States’ whole-of-government strategies to combat global threats such as terrorism, transnational organized crime, malicious cyber activity, narcotics trafficking, weapons of mass destruction (WMD) proliferation, and human rights abuses include targeting an array of activities, including the use of digital currencies or other emerging payment systems to conduct proscribed financial transactions and evade U.S. sanctions. The strategies draw from a broad range of tools and authorities to respond to the growing and evolving threat posed by malicious actors using new payment mechanisms. OFAC will use sanctions in the fight against criminal and other malicious actors abusing digital currencies and emerging payment systems as a complement to existing tools, including diplomatic outreach and law enforcement authorities. To strengthen our efforts to combat the illicit use of digital currency transactions under our existing authorities, OFAC may include as identifiers on the SDN List specific digital currency addresses associated with blocked persons. [03-19-2018] How will OFAC identify digital currency-related information on the SDN List? OFAC may add digital currency addresses to the SDN List to alert the public of specific digital currency identifiers associated with a blocked person. OFAC’s digital currency address listings are not likely to be exhaustive. Parties who identify digital currency identifiers or wallets that they believe are owned by, or otherwise associated with, an SDN and hold such property should take the necessary steps to block the relevant digital currency and file a report with OFAC that includes information about the wallet’s or address’s ownership, and any other relevant details. [03-19-2018] What is the structure of a digital currency address on OFAC’s SDN List? Digital currency addresses listed on the SDN List include their unique alphanumeric identifier (up to 256 characters) and identify the digital currency to which the address corresponds (e.g., Bitcoin (XBT), Ethereum (ETH), Litecoin (LTC), Neo (NEO), Dash (DASH), Ripple (XRP), Iota (MIOTA), Monero (XMR), and Petro (PTR)). Each digital currency address listed on the SDN list will have its own field: the structure will always begin with “Digital Currency Address”, followed by a dash and the digital currency’s symbol (e.g., “Digital Currency Address – XBT”, “Digital Currency Address – ETH”). This information is followed by the unique alphanumeric identifier of the specific address. [06-06-2018] Is it possible to query a digital currency address using OFAC’s Sanctions List Search tool? No, it is not currently possible to query for digital currency addresses using OFAC’s Sanctions List Search tool. Alternatively, OFAC’s SDN List and other OFAC sanctions lists are available in a number of file formats and downloads, which can be used to identify and screen for listed digital currency addresses. Additional information on OFAC list file formats and downloads, can be accessed here. [06-06-2018] How do I block digital currency? Once it has been determined that your institution is holding digital currency that is required to be blocked pursuant to OFAC’s regulations, you must ensure that access to that digital currency is denied to the blocked person and that your institution complies with OFAC regulations related to blocked assets. Institutions may choose, for example, to block each digital currency wallet associated with the digital currency addresses that OFAC has identified as being associated with blocked persons, or opt to use its own wallet to consolidate wallets that contain the blocked digital currency (similar to an omnibus account) titled, for example, “Blocked SDN Digital Currency.” Each of these methods is satisfactory, so long as there is an audit trail that will allow the digital currency to be unblocked only when the legal prohibition requiring the blocking of the digital currency ceases to apply. The institution is not obligated to convert the blocked digital currency into traditional fiat currency (e.g., U.S. dollars). Blocked digital currency must be reported to OFAC within 10 business days. Questions about whether a transaction should be blocked should be directed to OFAC at 202-622-2490 or ofac_feedback@treasury.gov. [11-28-2018] Should an institution tell its customer that it blocked access to their digital currency and, if so, how does the institution explain it to the customer? An institution may notify its customer that it has blocked digital currency pursuant to OFAC regulations. The customer has the right to apply for the unblocking and release of the digital currency. To apply online to have the virtual currency released, please go to OFAC’s online application page. [11-28-2018] Order an OFAC SDN Search from AADS AADS makes it easy to view all relevant risk information about any individual or company, either on an ongoing basis or on demand. We have been trusted by large companies for streamlined OFAC searches, verification reports, and background checks since 2002. Learn more about what makes us different from other OFAC due diligence providers on our AADS Advantage page. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "OFAC Searches – What Are They and Why Do You Need Them?", "url": "./blog-ofac-searches-need.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "OFAC searches are critical to protecting yourself and your business from severe civil and criminal penalties. But what is OFAC and who should be searched?", "text": "OFAC searches are critical to protecting yourself and your business from severe civil and criminal penalties. But what is OFAC and who should be searched? The Office of Foreign Assets Control OFAC is an agency within the U.S. Department of Treasury charged with administering and enforcing trade restrictions or sanctions on foreign countries and companies registered in those countries. Additionally, OFAC maintains databases and publishes information regarding current restrictions and sanctions to ensure that Americans do not, whether knowingly or unknowingly, engage in financial transactions that contribute to terrorist activities, human trafficking, narcotics trafficking, and/or other illegal activities. OFAC Searches OFAC searches help identify information as to whether there are currently restrictions on trade with a specific person or company of interest from the specially Designated National (SDN) list. Failure to perform such a search can result in civil and criminal penalties, including jail time. Protect Your Business Using OFAC searches as a tool to verify the identity of your customers is an important step in conducting business safely. You may be at risk, vulnerable to individuals and/or organizations planning to commit fraud. As a business owner, you should do everything that you can to prevent and mitigate financial loss. OFAC searches can be an important step in that regard. For more information on OFAC regulations and how they relate to state insurance statutes, check out our blog on OFAC regulations. Meet AADS OFAC searches will enable you to make informed decisions about your business partners and avoid restricted companies and/or organizations. At AADS, clarifying legal and financial risk is our specialty. We have been providing background checks of all kinds, including OFAC searches, since 2002. Visit our homepage to learn more!"}, {"title": "The Perils of an ‘Instant’ Online Background Check", "url": "./blog-online-background-check.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "For many employers, using a so-called \"instant\" online background check service to vet potential employees seems like a no-brainer. Think again.", "text": "For many employers, using a so-called “instant” online background check service to vet potential employees seems like a no-brainer. These online businesses charge a fee to deliver a report on any red flags in an applicant’s record. Since critical positions often need to be filled quickly, an instant online background check may seem like a great option. Though businesses who provide instant online background checks seem to be filling an important niche, the potential for backfire is enormous. The Problems with an Online Background Check Since the online databases used by these services are not vetted for accuracy by legal professionals, relying on them is a disaster waiting to happen. Using incomplete, limited, or erroneous sources of information to make a judgment call on an employee’s hiring potential can open up the company to massive liability. The main issue with the “instant” model of an online background check is that it, by definition, can only search records that are digitized and available online. While electronic record-keeping is becoming increasingly common, not all areas or jurisdictions have made the leap. Furthermore, many county courthouses that do keep digital records require that they be accessed via the in-house computer terminal. If a potential employee was arrested or convicted in one of these regions, the record won’t be shown on an online search. An online background check doesn’t just miss crucial information: it often throws up false red flags. Many online background check services rely on databases that were provided as-is that have not updated since the initial purchase. This means that expunged records or acquittals will be flagged as active cases. Much of the information you’re receiving could be months or even years behind! Lastly, in addition to missing or outdated information, an online background check can also return erroneous results. Since they do not use the thorough identification criteria of a traditional records search, a potential employee can be flagged for the criminal conviction or arrests of someone with a similar name. The Risks A hiring decision based on an online background check isn’t just inconvenient and inefficient — they’re also legally dangerous. When adverse employment action is taken based on incorrect criminal records, it opens up the company to an enormous amount of liability. In 2015, General Information Services and its affiliate e-Background-checks.com, Inc. were found guilty of matching public records to incorrect consumers. The companies were forced to pay $1,000 to each consumer negatively affected by erroneous reports— $10.5 million in total— plus a civil monetary penalty of $2.5 million. Just in the last two years, Whole Foods, Wells Fargo Bank, and LinkedIn Corporation have been the target of class action lawsuits for alleged Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA) violations while performing background checks and reference searches. The Solution The bottom line? The advertised advantages of online background checks are all too often undercut by the serious legal and financial risks they create. The best and most reliable way to perform a background check is to choose a public records research company that can deliver accurate, streamlined reports that satisfy all FCRA requirements. Background Checks and Verification Reports from AADS AADS is a public records research company that provides accurate and rapid due diligence for corporations, employers, real estate professionals, attorneys and paralegals. We provide everything employers need to screen applicants: Driving Records, Commercial Drivers Record Search, Education Verification, Employment Verification, Personal & Business Reference Checks, Professional License Verifications, Social Security Traces and County, State and Federal Criminal Records searches. To learn more about our on-the-ground courthouse and due diligence services, please visit our homepage today."}, {"title": "PACA Liens: Farm Legislation’s Surprising Effects on Title Marketability", "url": "./blog-paca-liens-marketability.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "PACA liens and the 1930s law that created them should be better known by title professionals. Here's what you need to know from the risk mitigation experts!", "text": "The Perishable Agricultural Commodities Act (PACA) has existed since 1930, initially passed by Congress for the protection of produce suppliers from commercial vendors who unscrupulously refuse to pay for the goods after receipt. While the Act may have its origins in agricultural policy, its overarching applications are far broader — and of interest to title professionals — than meets the eye. Here’s everything you need to know about PACA liens from the risk mitigation experts at AADS. Legislation Background The legislation provides specific benefits to farmers and other produce creators, creating a statutory trust system where suppliers are the beneficiaries and vendors the trustees. The vendors (referred to as “dealers” in the Act) are compelled to hold the inventory — and any proceeds gained from them — until the suppliers receive full and complete payment. Specifically, these dealers must maintain the trust’s assets to make sure that they are “freely available to satisfy outstanding obligations.” Accordingly, if a vendor is in breach of this fiduciary duty, the supplier may file a PACA claim in order to make good on any improperly-diverted assets. Title Applications So what does an 85-year-old piece of agricultural legislation have to do with real estate title? As it turns out, quite a lot. Until recently, the Act was typically employed in bankruptcy claims, enabling suppliers to receive lien priority over other creditors. However, a 2013 New York summary judgment established that PACA claimants had legitimate and superior right to a piece of real property, even over the mortgage holder itself. This particular case involved a bank seeking to foreclose on the home of an individual whose commercial property (a grocery store) was in forbearance. However, several PACA suppliers joined the case to argue their claim’s priority over that of the mortgager’s. Their intervention was a qualified success: the court found that while the complainants did not have right to the full amount of the unpaid PACA debt, their lien was superior to other creditors’ with regard to any mortgage payments made after incurring said debt. This position was strengthened in 2016 with an ensuing New York case that established PACA claims’ right to real property liens — as long as the payments were directly linkable to the trust assets. Factors for Consideration Title professionals examining issues of food-retail commercial property such as grocery stores, catering establishments, food stands or restaurants would be well advised to investigate any potential agricultural liabilities before sale. Savvy leaseholders can establish strong provisions to specifically indemnify themselves and the property owner against PACA liens (with the agreement of the tenant). Finally, any property owner must produce an affidavit and indemnity agreement in favor of the title company upon any act of sale: incorporating specific PACA exemption language into this document is wise extra protection when it comes to real property with food-selling tenants."}, {"title": "Patriot Act Search Requirements in Real Estate Transactions", "url": "./blog-patriot-act-search-requirements-in-real-estate-transactions.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Whether buying or selling, remaining compliant with OFAC regulations requires knowledge of the Patriot Act search requirements in real estate transactions.", "text": "The Patriot Act, passed in October of 2001, allows federal officials greater authority in tracking and intercepting communications, both for purposes of law enforcement and foreign intelligence gathering. It has fundamentally altered how we travel, identify ourselves, and do business. Yet the ways in the which the Act affects due diligence during commercial and residential real estate transactions is a topic that’s frequently overlooked. This blog will provide a brief explanation of the Patriot Act search requirements in real estate transactions. Patriot Act Search Requirements in Real Estate Transactions Section 352(a) of the Patriotic Act requires “persons involved in real estate closings and settlements” to establish anti-money laundering programs to ensure they are not transacting with entities or individuals being sanctioned by the Treasury Department’s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC). This blog will explain: – How the Patriot Act impacts residential and commercial transactions – What those involved in real estate transactions can do to protect themselves Who is sanctioned by the Patriot Act? The Patriot Act “blocks property and prohibits transactions with persons who commit, threaten to commit, or support terrorism.” OFAC regularly adds names of persons covered by the Patriot Act to their Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons (SDN) list. Searching this list to ensure no party to the transaction is a sanctioned individual is referred to as running an OFAC SDN search. How does the Patriot Act impact commercial transactions? Nearly 13 years later, the Act’s application remains a concern for buyers and sellers of real estate, both traditional and non-traditional real estate lenders, equity funds and promoters, landlords in lease transactions, brokers, attorneys, and any other professionals dealing in these commercial activities. The Patriot Act and the Executive Order which followed soon on its heels both require anyone involved in a real estate transaction — buyers and sellers, traditional and alternative lenders, equity funds, landlords involved in lease transactions, etc. — to be aware of activities that could, directly or indirectly, finance or promote terrorism. Keys to Remaining Compliant with the Patriot Act in Real Estate Transactions 1. Know who you are doing business with You must aware of who you are transacting business with. You are responsible for knowing who the buyer and seller are, who the owners, controlling persons, investors, and affiliated entities are. You must know who is financing the transaction. 2. Perform Due Diligence Perform an OFAC search to investigate all concerned parties. Title insurance companies may perform this kind of search, but if the purchasing funds come from outside sources then a typical title insurance search will not suffice. Clarify Legal and Financial Risk with AADS To ensure you are complying with all Patriot Act search requirements in real estate transactions, choose AADS. We makes it easy to view all relevant risk information about any individual or company, either on an ongoing basis or on demand. We have been trusted by large companies for streamlined OFAC searches, verification reports, and background checks since 2002. Learn more about what makes us different from other OFAC due diligence providers on our AADS Advantage page. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "The Risks of Not Performing a Patriot Act Search", "url": "./blog-patriot-act-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "The criminal and financial penalties of not performing a Patriot Act Search can be immense. Here's what you need to know to stay compliant.", "text": "Until the terrorist attacks on September 11th, 2001, most companies in the United States had few reasons to worry about laws related to financing terrorism. The passing of the Patriot Act changed that. In response to the attacks, the US Government stepped up its efforts in identifying financiers of terrorist organizations and drug cartels. This blog will answer the following questions: What is OFAC? What is the SDN list? What are the civil and criminal penalties for violating Treasury Department regulations? Who needs to perform a Patriot Act Search? How can I avoid these penalties and reduce my legal and financial liability? What is OFAC? According to Federal law, every business in the U.S. is responsible for ensuring that they do not conduct business with any individual or entity that is listed on the Office of Foreign Asset Control’s Specially Designated Nationals (SDN) List. To find out if an individual or entity is on the SDN list, a search must be performed. This is often called a Patriot Act Search or an OFAC Search. OFAC (The Office of Foreign Asset Control) is part of the U.S. Treasury, and is the official body that determines whether or not an entity or individual is allowed to do business in the United States. What is the SDN List? The OFAC SDN list includes organizations that are owned by or controlled by targeted countries, like Iran, North Korea, and Cuba. It also includes specially designated individuals and groups that are not country-specific, including drug traffickers and terrorists. According to OFAC, the SDN List includes: The Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons list (“SDN List”) The Foreign Sanctions Evaders List The List of Persons Identified as Blocked Solely Pursuant to E.O. 13599 The Non-SDN Iran Sanctions Act List The Part 561 list The Sectoral Sanctions Identifications List The Non-SDN Palestinian Legislative Council List Civil and Criminal Penalties for Violating Treasury Dept. Regulations The Office of Foreign Asset Control has the authority to impose civil penalties for inadvertent or unintentional violations. Penalties for Inadvertent Violations Civil penalties for accidently doing business with prohibited individuals and organizations can be significant. Inadvertent violations of the Patriot Act have resulted in civil penalties of up to $11,000 per violation, the seizure of goods and/or funds, and suspension from certain business activities, such as exporting overseas. It’s also important to remember that a single transaction may result in a multitude of violations under different Treasury Department Regulations, which can multiply the final penalty cost many times over. Penalties for Willful Violations In cases where violations appear intentional, the Treasury Department may defer to the Justice Department for criminal prosecution which can result in up to 10 years of jail time for officers, directors or agents involved in the violations. Civil penalties for willful violations can total up to $50,000 per violation. Who Should Perform a Patriot Act Search? The best way to reduce the risk of facing these penalties is to have a good compliance system that includes regular searches the OFAC SDN list and other terrorist watch lists for clients, potential clients, business partners, investors, etc. Naturally, employers would not want to hire anyone on the SDN list. Insurance companies are also barred from issuing policies to individuals on the SDN list. Financial institutions and mortgage lenders cannot conduct business or give loans to individuals or organizations on the SDN list. Those conducting business transactions will want to perform a Patriot Act Search as a standard part of performing due diligence. Order a Patriot Act Search If you need to conduct a prompt, accurate and complete Patriot Act Search, look no further than AADS!"}, {"title": "Your Guide to Perfecting Security Interests", "url": "./blog-perfecting-security-interests.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "Perfecting security interests is all about securing the performance of an obligation, typically a debt payment. Here's what you need to know.", "text": "Perfecting security interests is not as simple as it may seem. Most people working in the banking industry know what a security interest is. For the uninitiated, it’s a form of property interest created by operation of law or by agreement to secure the performance of an obligation, typically a debt payment. If the borrower fails to fulfill his or her obligations, the bank is able to foreclose and collect the collateral. Perfecting Security Interests This article is about a topic related to security interests: perfection. The general concept of perfection is that lenders should either possess or at least have control over the actual collateral. This allows the lender to “put the world on notice” that they have a security interest (for instance, by filing a UCC-1 Financing Statement). Not all security interests can or even should be “perfected.” Read on for an overview of some perfection guidelines pertaining to a few of the common types of collateral. Fixtures Fixtures, usually defined as equipment or furniture joined to the land or building, can be perfected in multiple ways: If a mortgage is in place, wording within that agreement can require the perfection of fixtures on the property. If no mortgage is in place, the lender can file a UCC-1 with land records in the jurisdiction of the property and fixtures. Filing a UCC-1 with the Secretary of State (SoS) also will perfect fixtures. Trademarks and Patents Perfecting trademarks and patents should be completed by filing a UCC-1 with the SoS and filing a security agreement with the United States Patent and Trademark Office (USPTO). Do your due diligence before attempting to perfect trademarks and patents. Parties have as much as three months to file for a transfer with the USPTO. That filing would take precedence, even if it is submitted later. Copyright Perfect a security interest in copyrights by submitting an Intellectual Property Security Agreement (IPSA) and a UCC-1. The IPSA goes to the US Copyright Office, and the UCC-1 goes to the appropriate SoS. When filing with the US Copyright Office, it’s important to note that a security interest isn’t perfected if the copyright is unregistered. Deposit Accounts A deposit account security interest can only be perfected through “control.” You can obtain control via three different means: The lender is the bank that maintains the deposit account The lender enters into a control agreement with the borrower and the deposit account bank The lender is a customer of the bank that maintains the deposit account Any Document from Any Court in Any Jurisdiction At AADS, we perform on-site search, retrieval, preparation, filing and recording of a variety of documents at both the state and federal level. We have helped lenders, factoring companies, banks, corporations, and legal professionals clarify legal and financial risk since 2002. If you need UCC, Judgment, Lien, or Trademark searches with quick turnaround time and accurate results, contact us today!"}, {"title": "Process Service: Getting Valid Money Judgments from Out-of-State or Evasive Defendants", "url": "./blog-process-service.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property", "courts"], "summary": "If you work in real estate litigation and have questions about process service, you've come to the right place! Tips on serving evasive defendants and more.", "text": "One question comes up often in commercial real estate litigation: Can I receive a money judgment if the defendant resides out of state or is evading service? The issue arises frequently, because anyone filing a complaint seeking damages resulting from a breach of a guaranty or lease must serve the party against which they are taking action. If that party is unable to be found because they relocated to another state or they are intentionally avoiding service, it poses a problem for the plaintiff. If you receive a money judgment before executing proper service, the judgment will be easier to challenge, void, and set aside. If the statute of limitations runs out on the claim after the judgment is set aside, you can’t refile. Personal service is an imperative for plaintiffs in many cases. However, there are two misconceptions regarding personal service that cause confusion. Let’s address both of them. If You Can’t Locate the Party, Publishing a Copy of the Claim is Sufficient This is FALSE. Serving a party through publication will not earn you a valid money judgment. Serving via publication permits you to get a judgment for a property in rem (for instance, a foreclosure action), but it will do you no good in your quest for a valid money judgment stemming from a breach of contract. You Must Be Physically Present When Serving the Defendant This also is FALSE. You physically do not need to hand documentation of the complaint to the party. You do not need to be present when serving the defendant. What You Should Do To successfully get a money judgment for breach of guaranty or lease against a party intentionally evading service or residing out of state, the process of substitute service should guide your actions. Substitute service allows a plaintiff to allege that a defendant has relocated out of state or is avoiding service. The plaintiff is then able to execute service after completing a process that begins with serving the Secretary of State. To use substitute service, a plaintiff needs to prove that he or she made reasonable attempts to find the defendant and to gain the information required for effective personal service. When seeking a valid money judgment against a defendant residing out of state or a defendant intentionally evading service, plaintiffs must strictly comply with laws pertaining to substitute service. AADS can help. Process Service & Skip Tracing from AADS Don’t let your pursuit of a money judgment land you in legal and financial trouble. AADS employs fully licensed and highly trained skip tracers that work in accordance of all state, local, and federal laws. As registered agents, we can also receive process service on behalf of companies and corporations. To learn more about how we Clarify Legal and Financial Risk, visit our homepage today."}, {"title": "How PropTech is changing real estate", "url": "./blog-proptech.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Every industry since we have entered the information age has experienced a large-scale shake up. Now it’s real estate’s turn, and the culprit is something called PropTech — the com", "text": "Every industry since we have entered the information age has experienced a large-scale shake up. Now it’s real estate’s turn, and the culprit is something called PropTech — the combination, or the “intersection,” of property and technology. In 2011, global investment in PropTech totalled just $200 million. By 2016 that number had hit $4.2 billion. Last year it reached $12.6b. It’s being called a real estate revolution. New companies are coming to market with technologies that are redefining how real estate sector operates. Applications and platforms currently under development have the potential to undercut numerous industries that rely on how real estate is currently purchased, sold, leased and transferred. Here’s what you need to know. ‘Space as a Service’ ‘Space as a Service’ companies like WeWork are providing a better balance between supply and demand and far greater flexibility than traditional office building landlords and property management companies. That’s because they’re fulfilling a market need: Today’s companies and workers prize flexibility and mobility over all else. As a result, ‘Space as a Service’ companies are diminishing the role of real estate agents, as companies today are more likely to lease small offices than to purchase commercial properties outright. Sale and Title Transfer Platforms Numerous online sales platforms, where buyers and sellers can meet, are making the market for real estate investors more transparent and lowering transaction costs by using big data and analytics. Billions of dollars have also flowed into blockchain-powered platforms that aim to improve the liquidity of real estate. One such platform allows ‘shares’ in a piece of property to be divided up and sold as easily and securely as a stock on the stock market. Others are promising to end title insurance as we know it by more accurately verifying ownership and expediting the overly bureaucratic process of transferring real estate title. The applications of PropTech are near endless. Smart buildings will track the usage of not only an entire building, by tracking how many people enter and exit, but also the usage of certain areas and rooms to improve use, efficiency and productivity. To learn more, check out this excellent article: PropTech: The Costs And Benefits Of A Real Estate Revolution Work at the Speed of Technology with AADS AADS helps real estate professionals, attorneys, lenders and real estate investors do their jobs faster and more efficiently with numerous due diligence services, including municipal lien searches and title abstracts, background checks and verifications, and comprehensive UCC and lien services. To see what makes us different from every other public records research company you’ve ever worked with, look no further than the AADS Advantage. Ready to get started? Place your order today!"}, {"title": "A Beginner’s Guide to Public Records Searches", "url": "./blog-public-records-searches.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "Public records searches are the tool to perform everything from bankruptcy searches to UCC lien searches for business partnerships. See how with AADS!", "text": "Do you need a bankruptcy search for a real estate or business transaction? Do you need to perform a Universal Commercial Code (UCC) lien search for a new business partnership? Many new investors are unfamiliar with the due diligence process and aren’t aware of the costs. Today we’ll break down the basics. Service Fees Due diligence companies navigate the complex arena of public records. Obviously there is cost involved in having a search performed. A typical service company will charge on a per-name or per-jurisdiction basis. Oftentimes, however, searches can be bundled into packages to reduce cost. Correspondent or Vendor Charges Across all 50 states and Washington D.C., there are over 3000 counties in the United States. That’s a lot of ground to cover. In any areas where your service company cannot physically visit, they will enlist a local vendor to carry out your search request. Unfortunately, many search companies outsource their services to offshore vendors when searches can be performed online—and this can reduce quality for the sake of cost. Jurisdictional or Statutory Fees While it is mandated for public records to be available to the general population, there is no rule stating they must be free of charge. In certain cases, a service company may need funds in advance to pay the filing office where the search is performed. This is called a certified charge. A jurisdictional fee, sometimes known as a statutory fee, occurs when an expense is paid out of pocket by your service provider directly to a filing office. Copy Costs The service fee is the cost charged to finish the research necessary and write a report on the findings of your search. Depending on your situation, you might need to view copies of the items from the search. AADS has 30 years of syndicated service in legal due diligence, courthouse retrieval, title abstracts, and searches. Our robust national network of local experts, property researchers, and data professionals help to reduce costs, increase quality, and streamline results. For more information visit our homepage."}, {"title": "Due Diligence Checklist for Purchasing a Business in Florida", "url": "./blog-purchasing-a-business-in-florida.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Two things you absolutely must know if you're considering purchasing a business in Florida are the legal and financial standing of the target business.", "text": "There are two things you absolutely need to know if you’re considering purchasing a business in Florida: the legal standing and the financial standing of the target business. That means conducting due diligence. Due diligence is the process of verification and information gathering prior to purchasing a business. Basically, due diligence verifies that you know exactly what you’re purchasing and that you’re paying a fair price. Due diligence uncovers potential deal breakers, like hidden environmental liability concerns or court judgements, and clarifies the target business’ financials. This blog will break down two key areas of due diligence if you’re planning on purchasing a business in Florida: financial and legal. Financial Due Diligence Checklist Annual and quarterly financial info, including income statements, balance sheets and cash flow statementsQuarterly and annual projectionsThree years of tax returnsThree years of bank statementsAccounts receivable infoBusiness inventory, including current inventory and its valueMarket value of real estate and/or copy of leaseSummary of debts and their termsSummary of current investors and shareholdersEmployee list and payroll informationEquipment list Special Considerations: Purchasing a Business that deals in Cash Special considerations must be made if you are planning on purchasing a business in Florida that deals primarily in cash. These include: Three years of register tapes plus the current year to dateThree years of Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) plus current year to dateThree years of cash expenses including payroll and COGS Legal Due Diligence Checklist Legal due diligence is the second critical task prior to purchasing a business in Florida. To adequately measure risk, you should receive copies of the business’ contracts, including: Purchase agreements Distribution agreementsSales contractsBusiness registration documentsArticles of incorporationLeasesEmployee and contractor agreementsTrademarks, copyrights, trade secrets, and patents Let AADS Simplify the Due Diligence Process AADS helps lenders, investors, and business owners perform comprehensive due diligence on businesses, individuals, and properties. If you’re involved in a business transaction, AADS can give you a complete picture of the asset side and the legal liability side of the seller. We provide due diligence with accurate and timely on-site searches and retrieval of all business, real property, tax, and legal documents. To learn more about what makes us different from other Florida due diligence providers, check out our AADS Advantage page. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Belize Dreams: Why Real Estate Due Diligence is Your Best Investment", "url": "./blog-real-estate-due-diligence.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "In real estate due diligence is a must. Here’s why. Serene seaside vistas, emerald green grass, golden sunsets — the Dreamscapes of Belize planned community seemed like a can’t-mis", "text": "In real estate due diligence is a must. Here’s why. Serene seaside vistas, emerald green grass, golden sunsets — the Dreamscapes of Belize planned community seemed like a can’t-miss for real estate investors and private buyers alike. But ten years later, the ground is unbroken and bewildered customers are locked in a court battle over their missing funds. The Investigation The problems lurking behind Dreamscapes’ slick marketing campaign came to light in 2015, as West Palm Beach investors filed a lawsuit against disbarred attorney Kenneth Dunn. The plaintiffs alleged that Dunn convinced them to pay twenty to thirty thousand dollars apiece for lots in the planned Belize community that would eventually boast an 18-hole golf course, police and fire station, boat harbor and suite of luxury homes. The project never got off the ground, and the lots are still vacant — with paying customers left high and dry. Offshore Dealings Even more troubling, early April’s leak of the Panama Papers — a set of documents detailing massive, illegal offshore financial activity — indicated that Dreamscapes’ holding company was a shell corporation set up by Mossack Fonseca, a Panamanian law firm at the center of the scandal. Performing Real Estate Due Diligence Due diligence is an essential part of any investment, including investment in commercial or residential real estate. Commercial real estate investments require far more in-depth investigations, into the financial state of the property, the financial and legal standing of the business itself, and the background of the owner or owners. AADS can streamline real estate due diligence by providing all required information under one roof. Clarifying Legal & Financial Risk with AADS AADS provides search and abstraction of a wide range of property documents essential to the title, real estate, and mortgage industries. We also serve the corporate and legal world with research and retrieval of a variety of courthouse documents, lien searches, background checks, and pending litigation searches. Our accurate investigation, rapid turnaround time, and paperless delivery have helped to expedite thousands of business and real estate transactions since 2002. Visit our homepage today to find out more!"}, {"title": "Four Ways to Hold Real Property Title in Florida", "url": "./blog-real-property-title-in-florida.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Even attorneys and real estate professionals with hands-on experience in the home-buying process can get confused when it comes to the moving parts of the transaction and the diffe", "text": "Even attorneys and real estate professionals with hands-on experience in the home-buying process can get confused when it comes to the moving parts of the transaction and the different ways of holding real property title in Florida. Carefully considering how your clients will take the title of a property is critical. Many kinds of ownership exist and each type lends unique advantages and disadvantages. In the blog below, we explain the different ways title can be vested in Florida to help you and your clients find the best option. 1. Sole Ownership Sole ownership of residential real estate is a title ownership option that most people choose when buying a home. It’s the simplest form of real estate ownership for non-married individuals or married individuals who wish to assume the sole title for investment purposes. For example, a married person can invest in real estate as long as their spouse signs a quitclaim relinquishing any ownership rights in the property. However, it’s essential to consider that sole ownership has no tax benefits or estate planning benefits for the owner. So, when the sole owner passes away, the property will be passed through the probate courts, increasing the time before passing down the property to heirs as well as the cost of the process. 2. Tenancy in Common Tenancy in common is the process through which two individuals who are not married to each other can take joint ownership of a property. Each tenant owns a specific interest in the property, and the percentage of ownership is highlighted on the deed. One key benefit of this style of property ownership is that the owner of the shares may pass their percentage interest down in their will. On the other hand, co-owners may end up owning a property with a stranger after the original buyer’s interest is inherited. 3. Joint Tenancy Joint tenancy is a strong alternative to tenancy in common and is typically used among family members to keep the family’s interest in a property current. Under a joint tenancy agreement, all owners must take a joint share in the property and must have taken their joint interest at the same time. When a tenant passes away, that tenant’s rights pass equally to the other tenants. The process helps avoid some probate costs and delays associated with sole ownership. 4. Tenancy by the Entirety Tenancy by the entirety is created by two individuals who are married to each other at the time that the property was acquired. Each spouse holds an equal share to the property, and their rights cannot be relinquished as long as they are both alive and remain married. If tenants divorce, they are considered to own two separate but equal shares in the home. Choosing a title style can have a significant impact on your client’s property rights further down the road. It’s vital to discuss options with a title specialist and find out more about the benefits of each style of title ownership. Learn About the AADS Advantage Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today."}, {"title": "Does My Business Need a Registered Agent?", "url": "./blog-registered-agent.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Let’s say you want to register your business in another state to take advantage of lower corporate taxes. If you don’t have a physical location in that state, you will need a regis", "text": "Let’s say you want to register your business in another state to take advantage of lower corporate taxes. If you don’t have a physical location in that state, you will need a registered agent to receive paperwork on your behalf. In today’s blog we will answer some commonly asked questions about registered agents. What is a registered agent? A registered agent is a responsible third-party that is registered in the same state your business entity was established. They receive service of process notices and official government notifications (correspondence from the Secretary of State, tax forms, lawsuit notices, etc.) on behalf of your company. Why do I need a registered agent? If you are registered in a state, you need a contact person in that state with a physical address — not a PO box. Your registered agent does not have to have a role in the operation of the business itself, but may serve other purposes if necessary. If you simply need someone to receive important tax information and legal documents on behalf of your company, a registered agent is the way to go. Registered agents are also useful for companies that do have other representatives in a given state. For example, rather than receiving potentially embarrassing or damaging lawsuit paperwork at your principal place of business, you can have them received at your registered agent’s office. Delegating responsibilities, especially those as important as receiving legal and tax documents, gives business managers the freedom to focus on their own specific responsibilities. Having a registered agent also decreases the chances that your business will miss an important document. Meet the Trusted Registered Agents at AADS AADS provides trusted registered agent services to companies across the country that value privacy, security and reliability. We don’t just give our clients summons and other paperwork in a timely manner, we develop contingency plans that spring into action the moment a process of service occurs: we’ll know all the details, who to contact and what they need to know. To learn more or to get started, visit our homepage today."}, {"title": "The New Retail Industry: Why Asset-Based Lenders Should be Optimistic", "url": "./blog-retail-industry-lenders.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "If you read the newspaper or used the internet in 2017, you can be forgiven for thinking that the retail industry is dead. You read about demographic shifts and way they’ve totally", "text": "If you read the newspaper or used the internet in 2017, you can be forgiven for thinking that the retail industry is dead. You read about demographic shifts and way they’ve totally altered consumer habits. You observed first-hand the steady rise of Amazon.com and the demise of retail mainstays, most recently Toys “R” Us. Which makes this ‘hot take’ all the more interesting: Lenders should be optimistic about the retail. At least that’s the opinion of Irene Marks and Lynn Whitmore of Wells Fargo Corporate Banking and Wells Fargo Capital Finance, respectively. Retail Industry Doing Just Fine The Millennial generation — those born between the early 1980s to early 2000s — are in the driver’s seat. They’re often credited with “killing retail,” but a close look at the data suggests this isn’t the case. Although they do use the internet far more than other generations for product research and comparison, almost 67 percent of their purchases are made offline. Perhaps most importantly, the youngest Millennials, those born in the late 1990s and early 2000s, are showing a greater preference for brick-and-mortar shopping than older Millennials. That may be why last year ended on a surprisingly positive note for retailers. Holiday sales exceeded expectations. There was healthy interest among investors looking to get in on the bottom floor of a turnaround story. The key for lenders involved in retail is to understand the market changes and recognize brands that are situating themselves to thrive — not just survive. The Retail Industry of Tomorrow Retail can’t simply rely on their location to draw customers. Window displays and traditional advertising simply won’t cut it. Successful retail means using the store as an integrated part of the brand’s overall offering. The store isn’t simply a place to sell goods, in other words, but must be an extension of the brand’s overall experience. To navigate and succeed in the new retail paradigm, brick-and-mortar brands must play to their strengths. That means allowing so-disposed customers to simply pick up their goods in store. It means integrating features in the store with smartphone technology. The retail stores of tomorrow can’t simply be cookie-cutter, homogenous franchises with no connection to the local community. One of the most powerful trends among Millennials is to support local businesses. If retail brands situate themselves correctly, they can capitalize on that trend. Asset-Based Lenders Must Adapt Asset-based lenders also have to adapt to the new reality. While lenders are traditionally focused on traditional collateral, like inventory, real estate and accounts receivable, now they must be much more willing to consider other assets, like customer lists, patents, trademarks and other intangible assets, according to Irene Marks and Lynn Whitmore of Wells Fargo. More information can be found in the April issue of The Secured Lender Minimize Legal & Financial Risk with AADS AADS is a public records research company that helps lenders, financial institutions, and attorneys and their clients minimize legal and financial risk. We are the experts in boots-on-the-ground transactional due diligence. Visit our homepage to learn more about The AADS Advantage, browse our extensive UCC Lien Litigation & Corporate Services, or Place Your Order!"}, {"title": "Beware of the Rules of Explicitness in Commercial Transactions", "url": "./blog-rules-of-explicitness.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "It’s a virtue to mean what you say, but in commercial transactions it’s even more virtuous to say what you mean — and that means being explicit. The rules of explicitness are a col", "text": "It’s a virtue to mean what you say, but in commercial transactions it’s even more virtuous to say what you mean — and that means being explicit. The rules of explicitness are a collection of rules (some statutory, others judicial) that require a heightened degree of explicitness for a particular term to be effective in a written agreement or commercial transaction. Because some rules of explicitness only apply in one state and others apply in common or federal law, transacting parties that draft their own written agreements and documents are at risk of breaking them. The ends result? Like secret liens, the rules of explicitness can surprise well-intentioned parties and render clauses and indeed entire agreements ineffective. Examples of Rules of Explicitness Disclaiming the Warranty of Merchantability Section 2-316 of the U.C.C. requires that a seller, in a contract for the sale of goods, must mention “merchantability” or use language similar to “with all faults” and “as is” to make explicitly clear that there is no warranty on the goods. Describing Collateral in a Security Agreement U.C.C. § 9-108(c) provides that a description such as “all the debtor’s assets” or “all the debtor’s personal property” is ineffective. Therefore, an additional 17 words are sometimes necessary: all accounts, chattel paper, deposit accounts, documents, goods, general intangibles, instruments, investment property, letter-of-credit rights, letters of credit, and money. Describing Commercial Tort According to U.C.C. § 9-108(e)(1), describing collateral by its statutorily defined type is ineffective for a commercial tort claim. Therefore, saying that the collateral includes “all commercial tort claims” will in fact cover none. Providing for Attorney’s Fees As of this writing, in at least one state, the clause “in any action relating to this Agreement, the prevailing party will be entitled to reasonable attorney’s fees” does not cover fees incurred in a successful appeal. To have such fees included, the clause must explicitly refer to fees incurred in appellate proceedings. Providing for Interest At least two explicitness rules deal with the right to interest on indebtedness. In agreements between creditors and debtors, the phrase “until full payment is made” may equal “until judgment is rendered” without sufficient explicitness. Uncover Relevant Risk Information with AADS Knowledge is power and AADS is your extra set of eyes. We make it easy to get an accurate picture of borrowers, identify any liens encumbering their assets, validate key data against public record databases, and achieve peace of mind! Our turnaround time is 48-72 hours, but most searches are completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. Check out our UCC Corporate Services or learn more about our AADS Advantage. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today! The materials on this website are for informational purposes only and not for the purpose of providing legal advice."}, {"title": "Satisfaction of Mortgage: Skipping it has Disastrous Consequences", "url": "./blog-satisfaction-of-mortgage.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The seller has their proceeds. The buyer has the keys. Time for a celebration, right? Wrong. Before the champagne is uncorked, there remains one crucial post-closing task to be com", "text": "The seller has their proceeds. The buyer has the keys. Time for a celebration, right? Wrong. Before the champagne is uncorked, there remains one crucial post-closing task to be completed. If not completed, it can cause huge headaches down the line. It’s called recording the satisfaction of mortgage, discharge of mortgage, or lien release. If the satisfaction of mortgage isn’t recorded, the property owner may have trouble when they try to refinance or sell the property. They’ll have to chase down the discharge documents and get them recorded in order to clear the title. It can even cause a delay to the closing. Because real estate is a referral-based industry where customer satisfaction is the key, failing to record the satisfaction of mortgage can have huge implications for your business down the line. But lenders, you’re in luck! Kind of…Tired of hearing from angry homeowners, state legislatures across the country have provided incentives for lenders to promptly record the satisfaction of mortgage. Some of these incentives have teeth. In many states, statutes require lenders to record the satisfaction of mortgage, lien release or a similar filing within a specific time-frame after a loan has been paid off. Some jurisdictions only have these requirements for residential mortgages, however. The time-frame varies by state. For instance, in Massachusetts, M.G.L. Chapter 183, Section 55 states that lenders must discharge the mortgage within 45 days of receipt of full payment of the loan. Failure to comply within the 45-day timeframe subjects the lender to penalties “in an amount equal to the greater of $2,500 or the actual damages sustained by the mortgagor as the result of the failure, together with reasonable attorney’s fees and costs, in addition to all other remedies available at law.” Find out what the statute is in your state to stay compliant! Compliance at the Speed of Business with AADS AADS provides search and abstraction of a wide range of property documents essential to the title, real estate, and mortgage industries. We also serve the corporate and legal world with research and retrieval of a variety of courthouse documents, lien searches, background checks, and pending litigation searches. Our accurate investigation, rapid turnaround time, and paperless delivery have helped to expedite thousands of business and real estate transactions since 2002. Visit our homepage to learn more about The AADS Advantage, browse our extensive Real Estate Title Abstract Services, or Place Your Order!"}, {"title": "Who will end up Holding the Sears Bankruptcy Bag?", "url": "./blog-sears-bankruptcy.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general", "courts"], "summary": "The Sears bankruptcy was a long time coming before Sears Holdings’ officially filed for Chapter 11 protections on October 15. In this blog we’ll take a look at the remarkable histo", "text": "The Sears bankruptcy was a long time coming before Sears Holdings’ officially filed for Chapter 11 protections on October 15. In this blog we’ll take a look at the remarkable history of Sears, the plans to save the brand, and how a liquidation would affect its 100,000 creditors. Fall of the Original Retail Giant Sears, Roebuck & Co. was the undisputed retail giant of the 20th century. Its pioneering mail order catalog, which was the first of its kind, and doorstep delivery anywhere in the country put tens of thousands of small town stores out of business. Before it was the store that sold everything — the king of department stores — Sears was also a manufacturing giant making everything under the sun. You could even order a house from the Sears catalog. The firm sold 70,000 build-it-yourself home kits in almost 450 different housing styles between 1908 and 1940. At least one remains standing, barely, in New Mexico. Their enormous manufacturing complex in Chicago, a true city within a city that boasted its own radio station, fire department, and banks, has been gone for decades. The only thing remaining is its 14-story tall office tower — the original Sears tower. At its peak in 2012, there were 4,000 Sears stores across the U.S. Sears was Amazon before Amazon. Now Amazon is putting them out of business. Everyone but the 100,000 creditors who are still owed money by Sears can appreciate the irony. A Closer Look at the Sears Bankruptcy The plan is to close an additional 142 unprofitable stores, reorganize, and operate with a smaller store base. But if other recent bankruptcy cases are any guide, Sears may not be able to avoid liquidation. Both Bon-Ton and Toys ‘R’ Us planned to cut debts and close unprofitable stores, but neither managed to emerge from Chapter 11 bankruptcy. Most experts agree that liquidation is likely. Sears simply does not have enough remaining collateral ($7 billion in assets) to satisfy its $11.3 billion in liabilities. How the Sears Bankruptcy will affect Creditors The banks and lenders that are funding Sears’ operations and secured creditors will be at the top of the list, followed by lenders who loaned to Sears in exchange for inventory or real estate. After secured creditors are paid, priority creditors (anyone who recently sold Sears goods, anyone owed back wages, and employees who are owed benefits) will fight for the scraps. Next come unsecured creditors — appliance, tool, and electronics vendors; service providers, and shareholders, who will be lucky to receive pennies on the dollar. Commercial real estate players are also keeping a close eye on the Sears bankruptcy case. If the company liquidates, the market would be flooded with over 1 million sq. ft. of vacant retail space. For many smaller malls, filling the vacancies left by Sears stores will be difficult. Join AADS in Fighting Breast Cancer in October One in eight women will be diagnosed with breast cancer in their lifetime. It is the most commonly diagnosed cancer in women. But breast cancer isn’t a death sentence. There are over 3.3 million breast cancer survivors living in the United States today. That’s thanks largely to the tireless efforts of breast cancer charities that help fund research, spread awareness and eliminate breast cancer-linked chemicals and radiation. Join us this October in helping to find the cure! 5% of profits from all new-client orders this month will be donated to one of these three organizations: Breast Cancer Research Foundation, National Breast Cancer Coalition Fund, Breast Cancer Prevention Partners. If you are a new client, we invite you to Create Your Order Here."}, {"title": "Art Heist: Security Agreement Fails to Encumber Consigned Goods", "url": "./blog-security-agreement-encumber-consigned.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Would you use boilerplate language from the UCC in your security agreement? As it turns out, it may be inadequate to truly encumber all intended assets. That was the lesson in a re", "text": "Would you use boilerplate language from the UCC in your security agreement? As it turns out, it may be inadequate to truly encumber all intended assets. That was the lesson in a recent case out of New York involving a lender, a bankrupt art gallery and a famous Botticelli painting called Madonna and Child. Case Study First Republic Bank loaned $29 million to the New York art gallery Salander-O’Reilly Galleries, LLC. In the security agreement, the gallery granted the bank a security interest in “assets and rights of the Borrower wherever located, whether now owned or hereafter acquired” and “all personal and fixture property of every kind and nature including without limitation all goods (including inventory).” Seems like a pretty all-inclusive collateral description, right? Well, shortly after receiving the $29 million loan, the gallery began accepting world-class art on consignment, including a famous piece by Early Renaissance Italian painter Sandro Botticelli called Madonna and Child. In a consignment relationship, you have: – A consignor: the one who owns the asset but allows the consignee to sell it. In this instance, an art dealer. – A consignee: the one who agrees to sell the asset, usually for a percentage of the final sale price. In this instance, the art gallery. When the art didn’t sell, the gallery entered bankruptcy. The consignor (art dealer) sought the painting back, but the bankruptcy trustee, who had received an assignment of the bank’s rights, claimed the painting was part of the collateral described in the security agreement. The bankruptcy court ruled in the trustee’s favor, likely thanks to the trustee’s argument: the gallery did not own the consigned goods, but Article 9 of the UCC gives a consignee “rights and title to the goods identical to those the consignor had,” including the right to transfer or encumber them. In other words, the gallery could encumber the painting even though it did not truly own it. This ruling would obviously be a huge break for the bank, which was struggling to recoup whatever it could on its $29 million loan. Yay! But not so fast. On appeal, the district court reversed the decision. The bank’s security agreement limited the collateral only to goods “ now owned or hereafter acquired” and the court argued that the gallery never “owned” or “acquired” the painting — a fact that the trustee admitted to — even though it had the right to transfer it. The lesson? A typical security agreement for inventory financing will likely describe the collateral as “all of the debtor’s currently owned and after-acquired Inventory” and then include the UCC definition of inventory from § 9-102(a)(48). The problem is that terms like “owns or hereafter acquires” do not always encumber assets that the debtor previously sold to a buyer who failed to perfect their security interest. Lenders and transactional lawyers would be wise to use a phrase like “currently or hereafter consigned to” in their security agreement, especially if the business sells things on consignment! The Salander-O’Reilly Galleries, LLC case has since been used as a precedent in other cases involving inventory, including in the court wrangling after Sports Authority declared bankruptcy. AADS: Trusted by Lenders and Transactional Lawyers Nationwide AADS makes UCC-1 filing, perfecting security interests and maintaining priority position simple and straightforward. With old-fashioned customer service and next-generation paperless technology, we make you look good to your client and give you the much-needed bandwidth to grow your business. We provide debtor monitoring, UCC searches, lien and litigation monitoring, comprehensive filing services, and much more. To learn more about how we can serve as a partner to your firm, visit our homage today."}, {"title": "Can you Perfect a Security Interest in Cryptocurrency?", "url": "./blog-security-interest-bitcoin.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "It’s a little bit like an immovable object meeting an unstoppable force, a perfect storm: How can a creditor perfect a security interest in an asset that is, by its very nature and", "text": "It’s a little bit like an immovable object meeting an unstoppable force, a perfect storm: How can a creditor perfect a security interest in an asset that is, by its very nature and design, hidden? What is and what isn’t currency? FinCEN describes currency as “coin and paper money of the United States or any other country that is designated as legal tender, circulates and is customarily used and accepted as a medium of exchange in the country of issuance.” Bitcoin does not fit any of those parameters. In fact, its very existence, it’s reason for being, is not meeting any of those parameters. But Bitcoin isn’t anything to sneeze at. As of November 20th, 2017, each bitcoin was worth over $8,000. Over 100 cryptocurrency-focused hedge funds have been founded in the last year, and traditional investment funds and financial institutions are finding it increasingly difficult to ignore the virtual currency revolution. For example, although the CEO of JPMorgan Chase has repeatedly called Bitcoin a “fraud” and predicted its imminent demise, JPMorgan is now planning on allowing its clients to trade bitcoin futures, pending regulatory approval. What is Bitcoin? Bitcoin, for the uninitiated, is a virtual currency that provides complete purchasing transparency by listing all transactions on a public ledger. Anyone can trace the ownership of each bitcoin. Bitcoin transfers are irreversible. Though the public ledger identifies ownership of each bitcoin, it does not provide information on liens — who those individuals are and what kind of debt they hold. After all, the primary purpose of Bitcoin is to conceal the identity of the individual. All that matters in the Bitcoin universe is the transfer of value, not who is giving and who is receiving. Perfecting a Security Interest in Cryptocurrency How can creditors perfect a security interest in cryptocurrency? First, one must define what kind of collateral it is. As it turns out, this is impossible because it does not precisely fit any existing category. Although a Texas federal district court found that Bitcoin could “be used as money,”and therefore ordered a bitcoin investment company to reinstate $40 million in profits and interest to its clients, it does not meet the Uniform Commercial Code’s definition of money because it is not regulated by any government. Using the definition of “general intangibles” in Article 9 of the Uniform Commercial Code or classifying Bitcoins as “investment properties” seems the only available remedy. But still, to take possession of virtual currency after a borrower defaults, a creditor would have to identify the individuals electronic wallet or other non-deposit account, login to it, and then transfer the currency to an account in their name. Unless the borrower breaks the law in a major way, like Silk Road founder Ross Ulbricht, law enforcement and the courts are unlikely to take the necessary steps to uncover the individuals virtual wallets and seize their assets. The Bottom Line(s)? The government and the legal world are lagging far behind when it comes to understanding cryptocurrencies. For the time being, bitcoins are unlikely to become viable in large commercial transactions until the existing uncertainties regarding security interests are resolved. In the future, Article 9 may be revised to account for cryptocurrencies. But that day is a still a long way off. It would require adoption of statutory amendments in individual states. One possible solution is for the Bitcoin world to adopt the indirect holding system of financial intermediaries outlined in Article 8 of the UCC. In this system, the financial asset is held by a third party in an account maintained by the third party, which account is credited to the person with rights to the asset in the account. As it stands, certain individuals are undoubtedly hiding assets from creditors, spouses, etc. by storing value in cryptocurrency. Most judges, solicitors and lawyers wouldn’t think to look for crypto-assets. And even if they did, finding them would be a different matter entirely. Crypto, you’ll recall, means ‘hidden’. Minimize Legal and Financial Risk with AADS AADS is a public records research company that helps lenders, financial institutions, and attorneys and their clients minimize legal and financial risk. We are the experts in boots-on-the-ground transactional due diligence. Visit our homepage to learn more about The AADS Advantage, browse our extensive UCC Lien Litigation & Corporate Services, or Place Your Order!"}, {"title": "An Extension of Your Firm: Georgia’s 2017 Solo & Small Firm Institute", "url": "./blog-small-firm-institute-georgia.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "The 2017 Solo & Small Firm Institute event was a blast, with AADS showing attorneys and paralegals how we can function as an integral part of their team!", "text": "AADS is happy to be attending Georgia’s 2017 Solo & Small Firm Institute July 14th and 15th in Atlanta. Why Choose AADS? We are a public records research company with: Advanced skip tracing software Rapid process service and skip tracing Experienced researchers to perform UCC searches, Real-estate title searches, liens searches Civil and Criminal Searches, OFAC, Bankruptcy We are more than just a vendor to small law firms across the country. We are an extension of your firm. We have invested in the investigative tools and the incredible people that make your job easier. Reports leave you even more confused than before? Public records research and skip tracing can be confusing—but we actually ENJOY the work. We are happy to explain the intricacies of our world to anyone willing to listen. Ordering searches feels like a scavenger hunt? If simply placing an order for your search is a hassle, the finished reports are likely to be disorganized as well—forcing you to complete yet another job that you’re paying them to do. AADS can offer simplicity and clarity at the front end, because we understand everything on the back end. Constantly overpaying for flashy companies with padded fees and subpar service? Many companies invest extensively in marketing and overhead—not on improving their processes. AADS is old school. No glitz. No glamor. We offer real, complete, organized results in as little time as possible. Lured toward inferior researchers that market price over quality? Many public records companies and skip tracers have turned their service into an assembly line that is focused on price pressures rather than quality. That’s NOT how we operate. AADS sends real people to the source to research, file and retrieve records. Bombarded with crazy spam emails? AADS does not collect, share or sell any client information with third-party marketers. Surprised by tacked-on fees? AADS builds long-term relationships measured in decades, not days. We never violate our clients’ trust by billing you for what has not been approved. Company avoids you, fails to provide status updates or return calls? We feel your pain, and that’s why when you call our customer support team, you’ll be connected with a self-identified “people person” that actually enjoys helping people. In the fast-paced, high stakes real estate and corporate world, this can make all the difference. Turnaround time fluctuates dramatically? AADS offers turnaround time within 48-72 hours—most searches completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. Set up a time to talk in Atlanta To set up a time to talk with Al from AADS, call 866-318-8638 or email Al. Visit our homepage or contact us for more information. We look forward to seeing you in Atlanta!"}, {"title": "Super Liens: How Homeowners’ Association Fees Can Impact Property Finances", "url": "./blog-super-liens.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "In some states, unpaid homeowners association fees can trigger super liens, which have high legal priority and present significant risk to investors.", "text": "Homeowner associations, commonly abbreviated to HOAs, serve many purposes: keeping up property values, marketing a neighborhood or residential block to prospective buyers, and generally maintaining a high standard of living for area residents. These services are usually funded by fees and contributions from the areas’ homeowners, and governed by professional real estate developers. If the owner of an HOA-governed home fails to pay their required fees and special assessments, a lien can automatically be leveled upon the property. In certain states, these can even qualify as a “super liens” that are given high legal priority and present significant risk exposure for potential investors. What Is A Super Lien? In the most general terms, a lien is a form of debt security. If an individual owns property that is not paid off, and subsequently fails to make good on their debt, the lender is granted certain rights such as the ability to sell the property and recoup their funds. A super lien is simply a lien that takes priority over all others: in the event of a foreclosure sale, it is paid off in full first and any other debts are addressed by the remaining funds. This is a significant departure from the typical structure, in which first mortgages are granted priority over all other liens and encumbrances on a property. Super Liens and First Mortgages Approximately 20 states grant HOA liens “super lien” status, making them senior to even first mortgages. Depending on the state’s specific bylaws, this means that HOAs are technically allowed to foreclose on properties in the event of unpaid fees. In practice, however, this rarely happens: a mortgage lender will typically choose to pay off the amount in order to preserve its status as first in line. Why Checking for Super Liens is Necessary Thoroughly examining a homeowner’s financial standing, both present and past, is critical before embarking on any kind of real estate transaction. This is doubly important for states with HOA super lien allowances. While the actual amount owed may be relatively minor, the priority status given to an HOA lien can cause major complications in terms of assessment, foreclosure, and eventual debt repayment. Savvy buyers and realtors will give the utmost care to making sure that all property information is present and correct in order to avoid unpleasant surprises down the road. Legal & Financial Risk Clarified with AADS AADS is a national public records research and retrieval partner serving the real estate, corporate, legal and financial worlds a one-stop solution for due diligence, property reports, title searches, document retrieval, courthouse services and much more. For more information on our services, or to place your order today, please visit our homepage!"}, {"title": "How Asset-Based Lenders are Responding to Tariff Uncertainty", "url": "./blog-tariffs.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Recent tariffs levied on imports from China, Mexico, Canada, Turkey, and the European Union have resulted in countermeasures, threatening an all-out trade war. That’s what’s in the", "text": "Recent tariffs levied on imports from China, Mexico, Canada, Turkey, and the European Union have resulted in countermeasures, threatening an all-out trade war. That’s what’s in the headlines, at least. But it’s important to remember that, historically speaking, tariffs are still quite low. At their peak in 1932, the average U.S. import tariff rate was just below 20 percent. Current rates are around 1.5 percent. Still, the number of goods affected is quite large, ranging from imported aluminum and steel to exported grain, automobiles, and technology components. Tariffs may increase or decrease, and asset-based lenders are adapting to the increased uncertainty and risk in a number of ways. Increased Portfolio Monitoring According to a joint TSL and Gordon Brothers survey, the majority of asset-based lenders are adjusting their portfolio monitoring. The specific sectors where lenders have altered portfolio monitoring include: 55 percent in metals 41 percent in automotive 35 percent in retail 31 percent in agricultural commodities Limiting Exposure Asset-based lenders are also: Increasing their diligence regarding potential direct and indirect impacts on borrowers; Relying on third-party appraisal firms to assist in monitoring go-forward changes; Limiting certain sectors and asset categories from their portfolios; and Avoiding cross-border activities with Chinese companies. Forecasting Asset Values Difficult In the short term, input cost increases will boost some inventory values. But once that product is sold and removed from inventory the one-off gain will disappear. Machinery and equipment values may increase as new investment flows in to domestic industries to make up for decreased imports. This has already occurred in numerous sectors, most dramatically in solar. The impact on retail and consumer finished goods inventory will depend largely on consumers’ willingness to withstand price increases, which means the question goes back to the economy writ large. If increased costs cannot be passed onto consumers, asset values will flag. Retail and wholesale borrowers most affected by tariffs will have to push cost increases through the supply chain or shift purchasing away from affected countries. The Big Picture For asset-based lenders, knowledge is power. A ratcheting up of trade tensions could lead some manufacturers to switch suppliers or shift production overseas. Decreased margins and slackening sales due to increased prices could lead some borrowers into distress. On the other hand, some companies will thrive if a new trade paradigm takes hold. It depends on the company, industry, and sector in question. As we noted, asset-based lenders are already shifting portfolio monitoring and increasing appraisal discipline to mitigate downside risk. Uncover Relevant Risk Information with AADS Knowledge is power and AADS is your extra set of eyes. We make it easy to get an accurate picture of borrowers, identify any liens encumbering their assets, validate key data against public record databases, and achieve peace of mind! Our turnaround time is 48-72 hours, but most searches are completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. Check out our UCC Corporate Services or learn more about our AADS Advantage. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Tax Lien Search: Why You Should Always Perform One", "url": "./blog-tax-lien-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "When and why would you need to perform a tax lien search? The risk mitigation experts at AADS have the answers.", "text": "When and why would you need to perform a tax lien search? The experts at AADS have the answers. Determining a loan applicant’s ability to repay a loan means considering many aspects of their financial situation. During pre-funding public records research, it is customary to look into things like credit reports and income tax returns. One thing that is often overlooked but that can throw a major wrench in the works, however, is a tax lien. Many times, taxpayers aren’t even aware that such liens exist, so they can’t voluntarily bring them to the lending company’s attention. Without performing such a tax lien search, then, you can easily overlook tax liens that could seriously jeopardize your ability to collect bad debt later. Importance of a Tax Lien Search It is crucial to properly and thoroughly assess an applicant’s ability to repay and their outstanding financial obligations to ensure that a given loan is suitable for them. Without ordering a tax lien search, you can easily miss something that could cause major problems later. Special Considerations For one thing, tax liens are not voluntary. Many times, they are issued without the direct knowledge of the taxpayer. Sometimes, notifications about these liens are never received for whatever reason. Other times, taxpayers deliberately stick their heads in the sand because they don’t understand the implications. Either way, it is crucial to unearth tax liens if they exist to avoid serious complications in the future. Further, since a tax lien can wreak serious havoc on a lender’s ability to collect on an old debt, it should be given high priority when stacked up against other types of liens. Make searching for tax liens a standard part of the process for best results. How to Conduct an Effective Tax Lien Search Finally, make sure that you know how to conduct an effective tax lien search. It is important to know how to order the report so that it includes the results that you need. Additionally, you must know how to assess and interpret the search results. Otherwise, you may mistake something innocuous for something problematic, or vice-versa, and that could cause even more headaches. When a tax lien search comes back clean, you can rest easy in the knowledge that an old lien won’t come back to haunt you. If you need a tax lien search alone or as part of a larger due diligence effort, look no further than AADS. Visit our homepage today for more information!"}, {"title": "Title Insurer Liability and Client Misconduct", "url": "./blog-title-insurer-liability.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The bounds of title insurer liability just got a little bit larger with a recent ruling in a North Carolina Federal Court. See what it means for you.", "text": "In a complex four-way suit between three real estate developers and one’s title insurance company, a noteworthy legal precedent about title insurer liability has emerged. Chief U.S. District Judge Frank D. Whitney recently granted a summary judgment motion in favor of Old Republic National Title Insurance Company, affirming that they were not liable for paying damages incurred by client LJW LLC as part of a fraud case. Case Backstory LJW and its owner Bill Waters were accused of conspiring with Waters’ son and his own company, Reverdy LLC, to cut the disputed property’s original owner out of valuable sales rights. The 28-acre development had been started in 2007 by Ardley Land LLC, who sold it to Waters, Sr. and LJW in 2012. Through a complex series of document exchanges, Reverdy (and its owner Waters, Jr.) came to hold the property’s title — financed by a $1.2m loan from LJW. In exchange, the father-son team allowed Ardley to retain sales rights on several lots in the property and promised that the development would be done by February 2013 to take advantage of the yearly sales-boosting “Homearama” event. Suit and Damages Reverdy failed to hit their development deadline, and in January 2013 LJW began the foreclosure process on the entire property. Ardley then filed suit against both Waters under the claim that they had conspired to strip Ardley of rightfully owned assets through inaction. The suit also included a “quiet title” provision that sought to reclaim first lien rights for Ardley, given that the Waters had been working together as essentially one unit. Ardley came out triumphant, and both LJW and Reverdy were ordered to remediate the damages caused by their fraud. Waters, Sr. then attempted to file restitution with the project’s title insurer Old Republic — but was rapidly shut down. Quiet Title Whitney’s ruling rested on the fact that the property’s deed of trust extinguishment was the product of active deception. He also specifically stipulated that even though LJW had asked for the quiet title complaint to be considered separately from suit damages, conspiracy allegations tainted the entire claim. The legal reasoning is clear: for an insurance system to work for all involved, the beneficiary cannot have an interest in actively causing the circumstances of a claim. In other words, an insurer cannot assume liability for bad behavior on the part of their client."}, {"title": "How to Conduct a Title Search in North Carolina", "url": "./blog-title-search-in-north-carolina.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Do you need a title search in North Carolina? Whether you’re a real estate agent, a North Carolina law firm or a private investigator, you may find the process of conducting a titl", "text": "Do you need a title search in North Carolina? Whether you’re a real estate agent, a North Carolina law firm or a private investigator, you may find the process of conducting a title search to be challenging. It may also be stressful, as your home purchase or your real estate business depends on the accuracy and thoroughness of the search. While a title search company will conduct the actual search for you, there’s still a lot to do, from collecting documents to choosing the company that provides the most complete search. Let us walk you through the process of conducting your title search from choosing your title search company to ensuring the final report is accurate. Collect Previous Title Work Of course, you need to collect basic information about the property, such as the address, but you should also dig a little deeper. You may have access to old title searches or other information that can help your title search company complete the search. Compare Companies Shop carefully for your title search company. Not all companies offer you the same searches, or level of search. If you’re in North Carolina, you can benefit from AADS’ thorough service. For example, we conduct municipal lien searches, which don’t appear in traditional title searches. Municipal lien searches tell you if anyone has placed a lien on the property, along with an assortment of other useful information such as: If there are unpaid utility billsIf the property has code enforcement violationsIf there are open or expired permits associated with the property When it comes to liens, North Carolina is a pure race state. This means that smaller liens from unusual sources are more common. In many cases the state may lien may supersede the first lien, but the other parties who are competing to have their rights to the property are encouraged to do so quickly. It’s wise to get this information quickly yourself. Consider Search Types When you speak to the different title search companies you’re considering, be sure to ask exactly what kind of information will be included in the search. We offer a wide range of searches that may be very valuable to you as a home buyer or to your clients, if you’re a real estate agent. These include: Deed SearchChain of TitleOwner VerificationTax Assessor SearchAssignment ChainJudgement/UCC/Lien SearchBankruptcy Search At AADS, we can advise you as to which kinds of searches are most valuable in your situation. Order the Title Search After you’ve decided on a company, go ahead and order the search. When you work with us, it’s as simple as filling out our online order form, with the information you gained during step one. Review the Search After you’ve placed an order with us, you’ll receive the results of your search in 24-48 hours. This quick turn around gives you the time you need to review the results before you go ahead with your sale. Double-check that the search is for the property you’re reviewing, and then reach out to us if you have questions. Choose AADS to Avoid Transaction Delays Our professionals at AADS can help you find all the property information that you and your clients need to know within a quick time frame. Our team has a comprehensive background in legal and real estate industries and has a vast knowledge of North Carolina law. We’ll scan thousands of documents for you, helping you find lien information, title documents, and related property data. Experience a quicker turnaround time, smoother delivery, and superior customer care with the AADS Advantage. Place your order today."}, {"title": "Don’t forget the Title Search!", "url": "./blog-title-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Before you seal the deal on your dream home, don’t forget to do a title search. A title search is a critical piece of due diligence. Here's why.", "text": "Before you seal the deal on your dream home, don’t forget to do a title search. A title search is critical due diligence, as it determines if the person selling you the piece of property actually has the right (title) to sell it. Thus, it ensures that you, the buyer, will get full rights to the property that you are paying for. Read on to learn a bit more about what a title search entails. Chain of Title This part of the search digs into the history of the ownership of a piece of property. This information can be found in title plants privately owned by title companies or in public records, usually housed under the County Clerk or Recorder’s office. Property Tax/Lien Search The tax search is done to determine if there are any unpaid taxes on the property. It can also unearth any special assessments, or prior liens on the property. If the property in question has unpaid taxes, the property may be up for sale by the government in the case of nonpayment of those taxes. Report on Possession – Supplement to the Title Search The report on possession is done to supplement the basic title search. The inspector searches for evidence of easements that were not properly reflected in public records. If there are unforeseen outstanding rights on the property, this could jeopardize the status of the buyer’s title and the value of the property. Judgment and Name(s) Search A search must be done to ensure that there are no existing judgments against the current titleholder or previous owners. An outstanding judgment means that the property is being used as collateral for a debt of some sort. It is important to search for any variation of the spelling of the name, as it may be inconsistently recorded across various documents. UCC searches and Municipal lien searches UCC searches and Municipal lien searches are also involved in transactional work. UCCs are notated and provide during a title search. There is no closing without a municipal lien search for unrecorded liens – such as city, sewer, code enforcement, etc. After the Searches, Commitment to Insure Once all of the title searches have been done, the title company issues a commitment to insure the title. The buyer, seller, and mortgage lender can then move forward with the closing. It is important to note that the title insurance policy of the lender and that of the buyer are distinct and serve different purposes and interests. Streamline Your UCC Search Process with AADS AADS expertly conducts title searches, providing accurate investigations, rapid turnaround time, streamlined paperless delivery, and unrivaled customer service. Visit our homepage today for more information!"}, {"title": "When Passive Voice should be used by Transactional Lawyers", "url": "./blog-transactional-lawyers.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Forgive the clumsy title. It’s in the passive voice. The subject, transactional lawyers, is being acted upon by the verb rather than vice versa. Those who advise transactional lawy", "text": "Forgive the clumsy title. It’s in the passive voice. The subject, transactional lawyers, is being acted upon by the verb rather than vice versa. Those who advise transactional lawyers typically say to never use the passive voice in contracts, and most times this is sound advice. The passive voice creates undue ambiguity and risk. But never say never. Like all parts of speech, grammatical rules, and punctuation marks, the passive voice can be useful. Today we’ll look at two passive voice cautionary tales and then explore some situations when the passive voice would be beneficial in a written agreement. East Texas Copy Systems, Inc. v. Player An asset purchase agreement for the sale of a small business included clauses: – Requiring the buyer to employ the seller for a period of four years; and – Prohibiting the seller from engaging in a competing business The agreement also stated that the prohibition on competition would no longer be binding “if the seller’s employment with the Buyer is terminated” less than two years after the effective date of the agreement. One day before the two-year period was going to expire, the seller quit and then sought and won a declaratory judgement from the court that the non-compete clause was no longer enforceable. The seller hadn’t been terminated by the buyer, of course, but the passive language in the contract (“seller’s employment with the Buyer is terminated”) implied that the termination could be by either party. Using the active voice (“if the Buyer terminates the Seller’s employment” would have protected by the buyer in this circumstance. Keltner v. Estate of Simpkins In this case, a real estate purchase agreement ostensibly gave the buyers the option to purchase adjacent tracts of land, stating “[s]hould Buyer exercise said option to purchase, a fair and equitable price for said property will be established at a later date.” When the buyers attempted to purchase said tracts, the trial court (and later appellate court) ruled the option unenforceable because the price term was expressed in the passive voice (“equitable price for said property will be established at a later date”) was too vague. It didn’t make clear how the price was to be determined, and it rendered completely invalid a key term of the agreement. The Lesson? Use the passive voice with care. But do not think its use is always undesirable in contracts. As a general rule, contract terms should make clear the action rather than the actor, especially when the actor is irrelevant or unknowable. In covenants, use of the passive voice is incredibly risky and should almost never be used because it does not identify which party is under obligation. In conditional statements, such as in loan agreements, the passive voice can be useful, even critical. the conditions of a default should be described in the passive voice because a default can include things that require no action on the part of the borrower. The active voice would actually limit the scope of default to the detriment of the lender. Or take a merger or acquisition that’s conditioned by the absence of material adverse change before closing. The condition should be described in the passive voice because the buyer wants to be able to get out of the deal regardless of who or what causes the material adverse change. 5% of Profits in September Donated to Prostate Cancer Research In case you missed it: It’s Prostate Cancer Awareness Month, and we’re doing our part to help raise awareness and money. One of the best weapons in the fight against prostate cancer is information. We invite you to review the risk factors associated with prostate cancer, the signs & symptoms of the disease, and to learn more about when to start screening. The other weapon? Money, of course, and every penny counts. That’s why we’re donating 5% of profits from all new-client orders in the month of September to the PCF. If you are a new client, we invite you to Create Your Order Here. If you are an existing client, you can donate directly to PCF here. Uncover Relevant Risk Information with AADS Knowledge is power and AADS is your extra set of eyes. We make it easy to get an accurate picture of borrowers, identify any liens encumbering their assets, validate key data against public record databases, and achieve peace of mind! Our turnaround time is 48-72 hours, but most searches are completed within 24 hours. We also fulfill priority and rush requests, and same-day document retrieval, filing and recording in some counties. Check out our UCC Corporate Services or learn more about our AADS Advantage. If you’re ready to get started, Place Your Order today!"}, {"title": "Transferring Title to a Trust can Protect it from Creditors", "url": "./blog-transferring-title.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Earl Benard Blasingame owed $4 million to Third National Bank of Nashville (TNB), a debt that was secured by (among other assets) a residence at 337 South Maple, Adamsville, TN. TN", "text": "Earl Benard Blasingame owed $4 million to Third National Bank of Nashville (TNB), a debt that was secured by (among other assets) a residence at 337 South Maple, Adamsville, TN. TNB declared its intention to foreclose on the home, but stated it would release its lien on the home in return for a payment of $490,000. Earl didn’t have the money, but Earl’s mother did. The very next day she wired $490,000 to TNB and then executed a warranty deed transferring title to the property back to Earl and his wife through a trust called “The Blasingame Family Residence Trust.” Earl and his wife became co-trustees and their children became beneficiaries and contingent beneficiaries of the trust. Specifically, the trust stated that the home was to be the residence of Earl and his wife during their lifetimes. It also named four other individuals who could live in the home upon Earl and his wife’s death. Earl Goes Bankrupt Fast forward two decades. Earl and his wife file for Chapter 7 bankruptcy. A creditor of theirs sues. Though both debtor and creditor agree that title to the property is held by the trust, the creditor asks the court to turn the home over to the trustee, the argument being that their interest in the home was a transferable legal life estate. The bankruptcy court decided that the debtors, Earl and his wife, had no more than the right to live in the home, according to the equitable life estate. The language of the trust made clear that Earl and his wife and their children could not lose the right to live in the home. Further, the court found that the presence of a spendthrift provision, which protects a beneficiary from assigning away his or her inheritance and also protects against creditors, was further evidence that Earl’s mother intended to prevent Earl and his wife from transferring title to the home to the detriment of themselves or their children. Thanks mom! Transferring Title the Easy Way with AADS AADS is trusted by realtors, lenders and real estate investors to minimize legal and financial risk. Our rapid and comprehensive title searches, municipal lien searches, and Search Packages have helped clients serve their clients better since 2002. To experience the AADS Advantage — rapid turnaround time, streamlined paperless delivery, and unrivaled customer service — place your order today! Join AADS in Supporting Refugees This is a friendly reminder that AADS is helping support refugees during the month of June. 5% of all new-client orders this month will be donated to Oxfam, UNICEF, or Doctors without Borders. If you are a new client, we invite you to Create Your Order Here."}, {"title": "What Types of Background Checks are there?", "url": "./blog-types-background-checks.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "There are different types of background checks that may be required for certain positions in the corporate world and in public service. Here are the basics.", "text": "There are many different types of background checks that may be required for certain positions, both in the corporate world and in public service. Below is a summary of the types of screenings and their sources of information. Different Types of Background Checks Background checks for employment These are among the most common types of background checks. Checks typically cover criminal history, social security number verification, education verification, and character references. Pre-employment background checks vary based on the employer. Some jobs require specific checks, such as driving records for employment in trucking. Criminal Background Checks Criminal background checks include county, state, and/or federal records. Criminal records will include information regarding, arrests, convictions, court records, warrants, sex offenses, and incarceration records. Unfortunately, not all states have county records on file. However, all state courts will have records of all crimes prosecuted at the state level. Most national background checks actually search each state’s database as access to national databases is restricted. Interestingly, only 46 states actually have electronic databases. Court records must be reviewed in person in Delaware, Massachusetts, South Dakota, and Wyoming. Tenant Background Checks Many landlords or property management companies will require background checks for potential tenants. A typical review is done of criminal records and recent credit reports.They may also contact past landlords and/or personal references. Childcare Background Checks Childcare workers undergo extensive screenings.Requirements vary by state, but most include state criminal background checks, state and federal fingerprint checks, a child abuse and neglect list check, and state and federal sex offender checks. Volunteer Organization Background Checks NGOs and religious organizations tend to screen volunteers who will be working with vulnerable populations, such youth and the elderly. Criminal records and personal references are most commonly used, though it varies by organization. Gun Sales Background Checks Any one purchasing a gun from a Federal Firearms Licensee must have a background check done using the FBI’s National Instant Criminal Background Check System, pursuant to the Brady Handgun Violence Prevention Act. Security Clearance Background Jobs that require security clearance, particularly government jobs, require extensive screenings. In-person interviews will likely accompany multiple background checks. Streamline All Types of Background Checks with AADS AADS clarifies legal and financial risk, providing accurate investigations, rapid turnaround time, seamless paperless delivery, and attentive customer service. That’s why we’ve been the top choice for background checks and verifications since 2002!"}, {"title": "Types of Liens and What They Mean", "url": "./blog-types-of-liens.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "There are many different types of liens, and as a leading public records research company we know them all. Here’s a brief introduction.", "text": "There are many different types of liens, and as a leading public records research company we know them all. Here’s a brief introduction. What is a lien? A creditor mayplace a notice on your property requiring you to pay off your debt before you can sell the property and transfer title to the buyer. Liens are usually placed on real estate, but can also be attached to other personal property. Judgment Liens A judgment lien can be placed on your real estate or other property when a creditor files a lawsuit against you and wins the court judgment. Judgment liens vary from state-to-state. Other Types of Liens Creditors can also place a lien on your property without suing you in a variety of different ways. Property Tax Liens Regardless of when it was placed, a property tax lien takes precedent over other mortgages or liens on your property. Because the government can sell your property to cover your unpaid taxes, lenders will pay the taxes themselves to protect the mortgage and add it to your debt. You might, however, be able to pay the back taxes and get your property back once “sold.” Procedures vary by state. IRS Liens If you receive notice from the IRS and still fail to pay your taxes, it can place a lien on your property, especially if they cannot garnish your wages due to unemployed or sporadic employment. The IRS tends to act swiftly and can force a sale of your home if you owe a significant amount. Mechanic’s Liens If you hire a contractor and don’t compensate him or her within one to six months, the contractor canplace a lien on your property. The contractor can also sue you to enforce the lien within one month to six years, and potentially force the sale of your property. Procedures vary by state. Child Support Liens A recipient of alimony or child support from you may put a lien on your property until you pay what you owe, sell or refinance your real estate, or the recipient forces a lien sale. Family Law Real Property Lien In some states, such as California, a spouse may file a lien against his or her community real estate interests to pay attorney fees in a marital action. Meet AADS Now that you know the different types of liens, it’s time to learn how to protect yourself and your clients from other unnecessary risks. AADS is a national public records research company that has been clarifying legal and financial risk since 2002. We perform on-site search and retrieval of a variety of documents including bankruptcy, civil and criminal records, UCC, judgments, and recorded/unrecorded liens. Visit our homepage to learn more!"}, {"title": "UCC-1 Filing: Perfecting Assets and Security Interests", "url": "./blog-ucc-1-filing.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "A UCC-1 filing is a financial statement used by a creditor to stake a claim of interest on the property of a debtor. This can be a valuable tool for perfecting assets and security ", "text": "A UCC-1 filing is a financial statement used by a creditor to stake a claim of interest on the property of a debtor. This can be a valuable tool for perfecting assets and security interests. Security Agreements With the exception of a few statutory and regulatory situations, a security interest can be made on any asset. For personal property assets, security interests are regulated by Article 9 of the Uniform Commercial Code. Article 9 states that physical goods, intangible goods, tort claims, and accounts all qualify as personal property. To legally be considered personal property, an asset must be listed in a security agreement. These security agreements must be authenticated, written, and include a thorough description of any collateral. They should also explicitly state the grant of the security interest. After the basic requirements for a security agreement have been met, the creditor may check the qualifications for a UCC-1 filing. The value of the collateral must be stated, and the debtor must legally possess the rights to that asset. To prevent other creditors from claiming the collateral after the debtor’s bankruptcy, one should perfect the security agreement by filing a UCC-1 financial statement. If the asset meets the requirements of Article 9, the creditor can be prioritized over third-party creditors. Classes of Collateral Assets A UCC-1 filing can be used by a creditor to prioritize their lien for a wide range of assets. In general, any asset that meets the requirements in Article 9 can legally be listed as a collateral asset. Real Estate Commercial and residential properties can be listed as a collateral assets in a lien, so they are valid in a USS-1 financial statement. Land is also valid according to Article 9. Vehicles, Machinery, and Equipment Assets in this category are tangible, and they can be easily appraised. They are valid assets per Article 9. Financial Instruments and Cash Financial instruments can be listed as an intangible good, and they have a definite value. They undoubtedly qualify as personal property assets under Article 9. Cash deposits can also be claimed by a UCC-1 financial statement. Intellectual Property Intellectual property assets can be difficult to appraise, but they certainly fall under the category of intangible goods. Clarifying Legal & Financial Risk with AADS At AADS, our commitment is to quality, efficiency, and expedience, and that’s why we’ve developed long-lasting working relationships with title companies, lenders, and attorneys across the country. Stop juggling multiple vendors. Stop settling for unreliable service. Partner with AADS for everything from UCC and Municipal Lien Searches to Document Retrieval and Criminal Searches. Visit our homepage today for more information."}, {"title": "A UCC Search to Reflect is Like Locking the Door at Night", "url": "./blog-ucc-search-to-reflect.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Pretend you’re laying in bed after a long, hard day at work and you’re just getting comfortable when you realize you left the front door unlocked. Do you get up and lock it or fall", "text": "Pretend you’re laying in bed after a long, hard day at work and you’re just getting comfortable when you realize you left the front door unlocked. Do you get up and lock it or fall asleep? Probably nothing would happen, you say to yourself. After all, when was the last time someone tried to break into your house? What are the chances of a burglar deciding to rob your house on the very night you decide to leave your door open — especially when there’s never been an attempt before? It’s a convincing argument, but most of us end up getting out of bed and locking the door anyways. Why? Because it’s the smart and responsible thing to do. It’s a relatively effortless risk mitigation technique. Better safe than sorry. It’s the same story with a UCC Search to Reflect; it’s a critical piece of the due diligence process, and the smart and responsible thing for a secured party to do. Here’s why. True Due Diligence is a UCC Search to Reflect After a UCC-1 filing or amendment, secured parties should perform a UCC search to reflect, which is a UCC debtor search of the office where the UCC document was filed after the filing posts to the index. It’s a double check — more for peace of mind than anything else — but it can save a secured party’s priority position by revealing whether or not the filing office committed any typographical errors. Financing statement errors, such as to the debtor’s name, can cause your filing to be lost in the public record and can put a secured party at risk. A UCC search to reflect will also reveal whether or not another creditor filed another UCC, or one of the many other types of liens, against the debtor. Clarify Legal and Financial Risk with AADS AADS is your one-stop source for clarifying all types of legal and financial risk. To learn why to perform FCRA Compliant Background Checks or a Patriot Act Search, and much more, check out our blog! We also invite you to learn more about The AADS Advantage, browse our extensive UCC Lien Litigation & Corporate Services. If you are ready to roll, Place Your Order!"}, {"title": "3 Top UCC Search Challenges Faced by Title Agencies", "url": "./blog-ucc-search.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "property"], "summary": "A title search on a real estate property is fairly straightforward. A UCC search is a different matter entirely", "text": "A title search on a real estate property is fairly straightforward. A UCC search is a different matter entirely. Common UCC Search Challenges Faced by Title Agencies Most title searches take a similar amount of time and labor, and this consistency allows title agencies to develop a consistent title search process. Unfortunately, UCC searches are a different beast altogether. Below are the most common challenges title agencies have when conducting UCC searches. Cost Catch-22 Many title agencies struggle to find a balance between understaffing and overstaffing. With too few hands on deck, a surge in orders can leave your team buried and reduce the quality of your customer service. But it’s also expensive to maintain an in-house UCC search staff. High Volume of UCC Searches Even a seemingly straightforward UCC search must be checked, rechecked and then checked again. When operating at high volume, corners are cut and results come back inaccurate or incomplete. Many searchers do not search for DBAs, former names, aliases, etc. and are unfamiliar with common abbreviations used in different counties. Dead-Ends UCC searches are often plagued by seeming dead ends. Mis-indexed files can turn what should be a routine search into a wild goose chase. Only highly experienced UCC searchers that are familiar with the kinds of mistakes common at filing offices are able to search in a way likely to bring back the needed results. Sound Familiar? If you have experienced any of the pain points above – or any others for that matter – AADS can help. Streamline Your UCC Search Process with AADS AADS clarifies legal and financial risk, providing accurate investigations, rapid turnaround time, seamless paperless delivery, and attentive customer service. That’s why we’ve been the top choice for the Real Property, UCC, Real Estate and Corporate worlds since 2002!"}, {"title": "What are Unrecorded Liens?", "url": "./blog-unrecorded-liens.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "There are a number of issues that can affect the title to real property. Unrecorded liens are just one potential cause for trouble. Here's how to find them.", "text": "There are a number of issues that can affect the title to real property. Unrecorded liens are just one potential cause for trouble. Here’s how to find them. Examples of Unrecorded Liens Here are a few examples that you should look out for: Outstanding charges from the city for various services, such as snow removal, grass/weed cutting, or boarding up of abandoned property. Code violations, such as missing railings. It is easy for ordinance violations to go undetected, and some fees can accrue on daily basis. Outstanding utility bills. Delinquent utilities often go unnoticed until a new homeowner goes to turn on new service. An unpaid bill can cause of lien of thousands of dollars on a property. Unresolved fees for inspections or certificates. Special assessments for various property features, such as sewers, sidewalks, etc. Responsibility of the Title Examiner If a title examiner is aware of an unrecorded lien, they must show it in order for it to be terminated or an exception to title. Here are a few examples of such situations: At the time of the closing, the seller mentions that they have a second mortgage on the property that was not shown on the commitment A survey of the property shows a dirt road across it There were references made to unrecorded liens on the property on the deed Our Advice To Prevent Complications with Unrecorded Liens Unfortunately, there is no single repository of information that you can search to ensure that you are aware of all unrecorded liens. We advise buyers to have a title search done that includes a municipal lien search in order to discovered unrecorded liens or encumbrances on a property. An HOA Estoppel letter is also strongly encouraged before the closing if the property belongs to an HOA. Why Choose AADS? AADS is a public records research company that clarifies legal and financial risk for individuals and companies working in a number of industries, including real estate. We conduct prompt and accurate title searches, municipal lien searches, UCC searches and much more. We pride ourselves on providing accurate investigations, rapid turnaround time, streamlined paperless delivery, and unrivaled customer service. For more information, give us a call or visit our homepage today!"}, {"title": "They Stink: Municipal Liens & Unrecorded Sewer Easements", "url": "./blog-unrecorded-sewer-easements.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Buying a property? Learn about two sewer-related issues that can cause serious financial loss: municipal liens and unrecorded sewer easements.", "text": "Today we will look at two sewer-related issues that can cause serious financial loss: municipal liens and unrecorded sewer easements. A Poor Inspection Leads to a Municipal Lien Let’s say you are a real estate investor and you plan on turning an old fire station into a brewpub – you know, the kind of place that makes their own beer and serves delicious food. The property, which is in a state of disrepair, was sold to you by the trustee of the deceased owner. You have a title insurance company perform a title search to identify any issues that would limit the use of the property or put your ownership in jeopardy. Nothing comes up, so you proceed with the transaction and eventually add a large deck where the old lot used to be. You put in expensive benches and tables. You spend $5,000 on landscaping and hardscaping alone. Then, there’s a problem with the sewer line. Only then do you find out the waste plumbing from the old fire station didn’t connect up to the city sewer main line, but led to a septic tank. What’s worse? The septic tank is buried right beneath the new deck. The city your property is located in passed an ordinance against septic tanks, and now you are responsible to connect it up to the city’s sewer line at a hefty cost. Stories like this are surprisingly common, and they can be the nail in the coffin for new businesses. Now, if this imaginary restaurateur was unable to pay for the improvement, the city would place a municipal lien on the property. If this property was sold, the responsibility to pay the lien would pass to the new owners. For this reason, it is essential that you have a thorough property inspection performed to uncover any violations you could be held accountable for. It’s also wise to have a municipal lien search, in addition to a title search, performed by a reputable public records research company like AADS. Unrecorded Sewer Easements A similar situation related to sewers that can cause property owners real financial loss are unrecorded easements. Generally, municipal works like sewers and water lines pass through roadways with existing right-of-ways that allow contractors to perform construction without having to warn property owners in adjacent properties. But in some cases, it’s not possible to construct a sewer line or water line in an existing right-of-way. In these cases, when the line must pass through or adjacent to another property, an easement is recorded to establish the right to use the land for a specified purpose. The problem is that not all sewer easements are recorded. If it’s not recorded, it won’t be picked up when a standard title search is done and the property owner may build something expensive (like a deck) over a piece of land that can be torn up by contractors. Contact AADS To make sure there are no municipal liens or sewer easements on your property, recorded or unrecorded, hire a public records research company that specializes in clarifying legal and financial risk. That’s AADS. Visit our homepage today to see how we can help you!"}, {"title": "Why Hipsters’ Beer Money is Being Seized by the Treasury Dept", "url": "./blog-venmo-treasury-dept.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Venmo, an app created by PayPal, has become Millennials’ default means of sending and receiving money. Splitting a bar tab? Venmo me. Utility bill due? Venmo me. Rent due? Venmo me", "text": "Venmo, an app created by PayPal, has become Millennials’ default means of sending and receiving money. Splitting a bar tab? Venmo me. Utility bill due? Venmo me. Rent due? Venmo me. The app is linked to users’ bank accounts and, usually, their Facebook account as well, which makes it incredibly easy to locate the person you want to pay. In order to send or request money, users must identify what the transaction is for. When anyone pays anyone else for anything, other users can see what the transaction is for. This provides a great opportunity to be witty and ironic — and most. Millennials probably assume this feature is simply for that reason: to make it more fun. But it’s not. “ISIS beer funds!!!” The Department of the Treasury defines Venmo as a “Money Service Business,” which means it is subject to the same oversight by the Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) as other financial institutions. Which explains why one payment of $42 for “ISIS beer funds!!!” was flagged by Venmo, and the money was not returned. Users paying friends for “Cuban food” have also had their transactions flagged and put through added scrutiny, because Cuba is a country sanctioned by the United States. The reason PayPal is so careful, even flagging transactions for ridiculous things like “iced coffee obama inside job nsa syria,” is because they have faced hefty fines for OFAC violations in the past. PayPal was previously assessed a $7.7 million fine for allowing users to transfer $44,000 in illegal payments to Sudan, Cuba and Syria. In essence, PayPal would rather keep some kid’s $42 dollars than risk having to pay the government $42 million — and we can’t really blame them. To learn more about how AADS provides comprehensive due diligence for financial institutions and their clients, visit our homepage today! In Case You Missed It… We recently wrote a blog about Minority-Owned Businesses — how they are growing at 10x the rate of the average U.S. small business but still struggle to qualify for traditional small business loans. We hope you check it out! Clarify Legal and Financial Risk with AADS AADS is your one-stop source for clarifying all types of legal and financial risk. To learn why to perform FCRA Compliant Background Checks or a Patriot Act Search, and much more, check out our blog! We also invite you to learn more about The AADS Advantage, browse our extensive UCC Lien Litigation & Corporate Services. If you are ready to roll, Place Your Order!"}, {"title": "We did this for you: AADS Launches Responsive, Client-Focused Website", "url": "./blog-website-launch.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "AADS is proud to announce the launch of our responsive, client-focused website! This is truly the next phase for AADS and the next phase for authentic, streamlined due diligence. W", "text": "AADS is proud to announce the launch of our responsive, client-focused website! This is truly the next phase for AADS and the next phase for authentic, streamlined due diligence. We partnered with a leading NYC digital agency and worked with them to design the website from head to toe. We didn’t put in all this hard work to pass the time; the site is going to play a huge role as we continue our important work of: Minimizing legal and financial risk Eliminating redundancies, inefficiencies and errors Providing onsite expertise, personalized service, and streamlined delivery We Feel Your Pain Since 2002, we have been sought out by companies and individuals in numerous industries – from corporations, title companies, registered agent service companies and private investigators to factoring companies, attorneys and paralegals. We understand the incredible legal and financial burden caused by the current regulatory climate – that time is money, and many organizations don’t have time or personnel to delve into public records. A Supporting Partner to Minimize Legal and Financial Risk That’s why we function as a supporting partner that can help you and your business succeed in a high-stress, deadline-oriented world. We look forward to meeting your exact needs with our new website, with onsite and/or online searches, or as your comprehensive due diligence service provider. We look forward to working with you and invite you to check out the new and improved home of AADS!"}, {"title": "Why Partner with AADS in 2017?", "url": "./blog-why-partner-with-all-american-document-services-in-2017.html", "type": "archive", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Are you sick of so-so customer service? Juggling public records research vendors? AADS can help you clarify legal and financial risk and make 2017 a successful and stress-free year", "text": "Are you sick of so-so customer service? Juggling public records research vendors? AADS can help you clarify legal and financial risk and make 2017 a successful and stress-free year. Here’s how. We Mean Business with our Customer Service We don’t skimp on quality, and we don’t take customer service lightly. Our commitment to real, people-focused customer service and real process innovations have made us a top choice for a growing number of companies and industries. We Serve Your Industry AADS invests in local experts that perform on-site search and retrieval of the documents essential to your industry, your clients and your business. We have been serving clients and individuals in the real estate, legal, corporate, transaction processing industries, and factoring industries since 2002. Our services include, but are not limited to: CORPORATE SERVICES, UCC LIEN SEARCHES, RECORDED AND UNRECORDED LIEN SEARCHES, REAL PROPERTY TITLE AND MUNICIPAL LIEN SEARCHES, CRIMINAL SEARCHES, CIVIL LITIGATION, BACKGROUND CHECKS, and FILINGS. We Know Your Pain Points Many public records research companies invest extensively in marketing and overhead—not on improving their processes.We offer simplicity and clarity at the front end, because we understand everything on the back end.The result is real, complete, organized results in as little time as possible. If you’ve experienced any of the following, we understand and we can help: Reports leave you even more confused than before Ordering searches feels like a scavenger hunt Constantly overpaying for flashy companies with padded fees and subpar service Lured toward inferior researchers that market price over quality Bombarded with spam emails Surprised by tacked-on fees Company avoids you, fails to provide status updates or return calls Turnaround time fluctuates dramatically Know that there is a better way forward with All American Document Service. To learn more about our accurate investigations, rapid results and competitive prices, please visit our homepage today!"}, {"title": "What to Provide Before Requesting an Apostille or Authentication", "url": "./guide-apostille-request-checklist.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["international"], "summary": "Identify the document’s origin, destination country and receiving party’s instructions before choosing a processing route. The word “apostille” alone does not define the whole request.", "text": "Home/What to Provide Before Requesting an Apostille or AuthenticationREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereIdentify the document’s origin, destination country and receiving party’s instructions before choosing a processing route. The word “apostille” alone does not define the whole request.Start with origin and destinationUSAGov explains that apostilles and authentication certificates verify signatures, stamps or seals on documents, and that the destination affects the required route. Its guidance distinguishes state-issued records from federal records and directs users to the relevant authorities. The U.S. Department of State also publishes separate preparation and submission guidance for authentication and apostille requests.These distinctions make a complete intake important. A document issued by a state authority and one issued by a federal authority should not be routed on the assumption that the same office handles both.The information to collectDocument type: identify the actual record, such as a corporate document, vital record, court record or other specified document.Issuing authority: state who issued or certified it and the state or federal origin. Supply a clear copy for route review when appropriate.Country of use: name the destination and provide the receiving party’s written instructions.Available version: identify whether you hold an original, certified copy, photocopy or electronic record.Additional requirements: include any translation, issuance-date, original-format or consular requirement stated by the recipient.Timing and delivery: give the date needed, delivery destination and any deadline dependency.Do not change the document before reviewAvoid adding a notarization, detaching a certification or mailing an irreplaceable original solely because a generic checklist suggests it. Preparation requirements depend on the document and authority. Confirm what is needed for this specific request before altering or submitting the record.Illustrative request wording“Please review the attached document copy and receiving-party instructions for use in the country identified below. Confirm the required document version, processing authorities, anticipated timing, fees and return-delivery arrangements before we send the original.”What should the confirmation cover?Keep a written record of the agreed route, what the client supplies, any documents to be retrieved, and what is being returned. If recipient requirements remain uncertain, identify the open question rather than promising acceptance. AADS can coordinate the confirmed processing work; the issuing and receiving authorities retain their respective roles.What does an apostille verify?It relates to the signature, seal or official capacity associated with the public document. It should not be represented as validating every factual assertion in that document.Official sourcesUSAGov — Authenticate a U.S. document for use abroad ↗U.S. Department of State — Document authentication ↗Sources checked 9 November 2026. Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "Certificate of Good Standing, Online Status & Certified Copies: What to Order", "url": "./guide-good-standing-certified-copy.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Match the document to the receiving party’s requirement. A certificate of good standing, an online status result and a certified copy serve different needs.", "text": "Home/Certificate of Good Standing, Online Status & Certified Copies: What to OrderREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereMatch the document to the receiving party’s requirement. A certificate of good standing, an online status result and a certified copy serve different needs.Ask for the requirement before ordering“Please send the company documents” leaves important choices open. Obtain the wording from the lender, law firm, authority or other recipient. Confirm the entity, issuing jurisdiction, document type, certification and date requirements before requesting production.Delaware provides a useful example: its online-status guidance distinguishes an online status result from an official certificate of good standing. It also explains that a status-and-history result does not supply images of the underlying filed documents. Other states may use different names and processes, so use the relevant issuing office’s instructions.Separate the deliverablesRequested itemWhat to clarifyOnline entity or status resultDoes the recipient accept a database result, or require an official certificate of good standing?Certificate of good standing (or the issuing office’s equivalent)Which issuing state, certificate of good standing form and issuance-date requirement apply?Copy of a filed documentWhich formation document, amendment, filing date or document number is needed?Certified copyWhich underlying document must be certified by the office holding the record?Prepare a complete corporate document requestConfirm the entity. Provide the exact legal name and file number if available. A similar company name is not enough to identify the intended record confidently.Name the jurisdiction. Specify the issuing state and distinguish a formation-state requirement from a requirement involving another registration.Specify the item. Identify whether you need a certificate of good standing, a plain copy or a certified copy, and any requested version or historical filing.Keep the recipient’s conditions. Include any document-age, original-format or delivery condition rather than assuming every certificate of good standing is accepted.Identify international use. If the document is going abroad, provide the destination country and recipient instructions so any separate authentication route can be reviewed.Illustrative request wording“The receiving party requires an official certificate of good standing for the entity identified below, issued within its stated date window. Please confirm the available form, fees, anticipated timing and delivery format before ordering.”AADS can coordinate the specified records and filings. The issuing authority determines what it can issue, and the receiving party determines whether the supplied item satisfies its requirement.Official sourcesDelaware Division of Corporations — Online status and official certificates ↗Sources checked 9 November 2026. Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "Title Search and Municipal Research: What Should the Request Include?", "url": "./guide-municipal-research-scope.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Treat recorded land records and local departmental responses as distinct parts of the research plan. Define the components your closing file needs before work begins.", "text": "Home/Title Search and Municipal Research: What Should the Request Include?REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereTreat recorded land records and local departmental responses as distinct parts of the research plan. Define the components your closing file needs before work begins.Why separate sources matterYour request identifies the property-specific departments, records and responses required for the matter.For a closing checklist, the practical question is which components are included in the order and which office or provider supplies each response. A title abstract and a municipal response should not be treated as interchangeable documents.Write down the componentsResearch componentWhat to specifyRecorded property recordsParcel identity, title-search depth, relevant owner or instrument references, and copies required.Permits and codeThe requested status information, record period where relevant, and supporting case or permit records.Utilities and assessmentsThe requested account or property-balance research and the responsible providers, when known.Zoning documentsWhether the request needs an available online classification, an official verification letter or specified supporting documents.Give the researcher a usable property identityInclude the street address, county, state and parcel number. Supply a legal description, owner name or prior instrument reference when available. If the property contains several parcels, identify whether the request applies to each one. An address alone may leave the scope unclear.Plan for partial responsesAsk how separately outstanding responses will be identified. If an office has not answered, the status should remain pending; it should not be converted into a finding of no balance, violation or open permit. Confirm whether delivery is expected as a complete package or in clearly labeled stages.Illustrative intake checklistProperty and parcel identifiers, including any multiple-parcel scope.Requested recorded-record research and municipal components.Known government offices and separate utility or service providers.Supporting copies, official-letter requirements and date needed.Instructions for reporting unanswered requests or unavailable records.Does a municipal search replace a title search?Do not assume it does. Confirm the required sources and components with the ordering team. AADS reports the agreed factual research and responses; legal interpretation and title-insurance decisions remain with the appropriate professionals.Official sourcesCity of Orlando — Property information and separate record sources ↗Sources checked 9 November 2026. Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "How to Prepare a Multistate UCC and Lien Search Request", "url": "./guide-ucc-search-scope.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Start with the name, sources, jurisdictions and deliverables—not simply “a nationwide search.” A written scope gives the researcher and reviewing team the same reference point.", "text": "Home/How to Prepare a Multistate UCC and Lien Search RequestREQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 9 NOVEMBER 2026Start hereStart with the name, sources, jurisdictions and deliverables—not simply “a nationwide search.” A written scope gives the researcher and reviewing team the same reference point.Begin with the matter’s checklist“UCC and liens” can describe several different requests. Before ordering, identify the record categories your reviewing team requires and which agencies or courts should be included. A state UCC search should not be assumed to include county records, litigation or bankruptcy research unless these are expressly part of the request.The California Secretary of State illustrates why naming instructions matter: its UCC information-request guidance calls for a separate request for each debtor name or variation and notes that other filing offices may offer different information options. This is a California example, not a rule to apply unchanged in every state.Build a request the next person can useIdentify the subject. Provide the exact name, entity type and known file identifiers. List former names or other variations separately; avoid leaving the researcher to infer which names are authorized.List the jurisdictions and sources. State which states, counties and courts are requested. If you need help identifying source options, ask for scope review before treating the request as final.Define the record categories. Specify UCC records, tax liens, judgments, litigation or bankruptcy as applicable to your team’s instructions.Define the output. Say whether an official UCC search certificate, plain copies, certified copies, a case index or particular documents are required. Identify copy limits.State the timing. Include the date needed and any search-period or effective-date requirements. Ask how source timing affects the deliverable.Illustrative request wording“Please review the attached subject-name and jurisdiction list for a UCC and lien search quote. The checklist identifies the requested sources, UCC search certificate and copy requirements. Please confirm scope, anticipated timing and any source limitations before proceeding.”This is an intake example, not a completed search or a recommendation of legal scope. Attach a clear checklist rather than placing several unstructured names and locations in an email thread.Check the delivery against the requestRetain the agreed scope with the returned work. Confirm that the report identifies the names searched, sources, relevant search dates and any outstanding items. A record not located in the specified research should not be restated as a universal finding that no obligation or lien exists.Does a UCC search prove priority?No legal conclusion about priority or enforceability follows simply from ordering a search. AADS supplies the factual records and requested research; the client’s legal team assesses their significance.Official sourcesCalifornia Secretary of State — UCC information requests ↗Sources checked 9 November 2026. Examples illustrate the named jurisdiction or authority; requirements should be confirmed for the actual request.General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; this guide does not select legal scope or provide a legal or title opinion.Related serviceExplore the relevant AADS service →Start Your Next RequestSend the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins.Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "Can AADS support nationwide due diligence?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-1", "type": "faq", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Yes. AADS coordinates public-record research and document work across states, counties and federal courts through one point of contact. The names, jurisdictions, sources and records included are confirmed for your matter.", "text": "Yes. AADS coordinates public-record research and document work across states, counties and federal courts through one point of contact. The names, jurisdictions, sources and records included are confirmed for your matter."}, {"title": "What records are commonly included in due diligence?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-2", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "corporate"], "summary": "Depending on the matter, the scope can include UCC records, tax liens, judgment liens, civil litigation, bankruptcy and a certificate of good standing. The research checklist identifies the sources and jurisdictions included.", "text": "Depending on the matter, the scope can include UCC records, tax liens, judgment liens, civil litigation, bankruptcy and a certificate of good standing. The research checklist identifies the sources and jurisdictions included."}, {"title": "What corporate compliance services does AADS support?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-3", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "AADS supports certificates of good standing, corporate record retrieval, plain and certified copies, filing submissions under authorized instructions, and registered agent coordination. The requested work and jurisdiction requirements are confirmed before proceeding.", "text": "AADS supports certificates of good standing, corporate record retrieval, plain and certified copies, filing submissions under authorized instructions, and registered agent coordination. The requested work and jurisdiction requirements are confirmed before proceeding."}, {"title": "Can AADS help obtain certificates of good standing?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-4", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Yes. Provide the exact legal entity name, issuing state, file number if known, recipient requirements and date needed. AADS coordinates the certificate of good standing or the equivalent certificate offered by that issuing office.", "text": "Yes. Provide the exact legal entity name, issuing state, file number if known, recipient requirements and date needed. AADS coordinates the certificate of good standing or the equivalent certificate offered by that issuing office."}, {"title": "How do I prepare an individual request?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-5", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Identify the service, subject or property, jurisdictions, supporting documents and date needed. AADS confirms scope, quoted fees and anticipated timing before work begins. You can begin with one defined request.", "text": "Identify the service, subject or property, jurisdictions, supporting documents and date needed. AADS confirms scope, quoted fees and anticipated timing before work begins. You can begin with one defined request."}, {"title": "How long does a search or document request take?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-6", "type": "faq", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Timing depends on the service, jurisdiction, source availability and the records required. Share your deadline at the start. Anticipated timing and any available rush option are confirmed for the actual request.", "text": "Timing depends on the service, jurisdiction, source availability and the records required. Share your deadline at the start. Anticipated timing and any available rush option are confirmed for the actual request."}, {"title": "What determines the quote and document copy fees?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-7", "type": "faq", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "The quote depends on the names or properties, jurisdictions, record categories, copies, certifications and any rush requirements. Confirm the service charges and applicable government or document fees in the quote before proceeding.", "text": "The quote depends on the names or properties, jurisdictions, record categories, copies, certifications and any rush requirements. Confirm the service charges and applicable government or document fees in the quote before proceeding."}, {"title": "Does the due diligence package cover state, county and federal sources?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-8", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "corporate"], "summary": "Yes. The package brings together state and county UCC, tax lien and judgment lien searches, federal district and county civil litigation searches, bankruptcy research, and a certificate of good standing. The specific names, states, counties and courts are confirmed for each request.", "text": "Yes. The package brings together state and county UCC, tax lien and judgment lien searches, federal district and county civil litigation searches, bankruptcy research, and a certificate of good standing. The specific names, states, counties and courts are confirmed for each request."}, {"title": "What is included in the Real Estate Package?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-9", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The package brings together a current owner title search, recording of documents, and municipal lien searches through the responsible city, county or other local authority. Confirm the property, search depth, municipal components and recording instructions for your quote.", "text": "The package brings together a current owner title search, recording of documents, and municipal lien searches through the responsible city, county or other local authority. Confirm the property, search depth, municipal components and recording instructions for your quote."}, {"title": "Does a title search include every municipal record?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-10", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Recorded land records and local departmental records are separate research paths. Identify the municipal components required, such as code, permits, utilities or assessments, and confirm the responsible departments or providers for the property.", "text": "Recorded land records and local departmental records are separate research paths. Identify the municipal components required, such as code, permits, utilities or assessments, and confirm the responsible departments or providers for the property."}, {"title": "Is a certificate of good standing the same as a certified copy?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-11", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "No. A certificate of good standing addresses entity status as defined by the issuing office. A certified copy reproduces a specified filed document with that office’s certification. Provide the receiving party’s exact requirement.", "text": "No. A certificate of good standing addresses entity status as defined by the issuing office. A certified copy reproduces a specified filed document with that office’s certification. Provide the receiving party’s exact requirement."}, {"title": "Does a UCC search establish lien priority or prove that collateral is clear?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-12", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "No. AADS supplies factual records and requested research from the agreed sources. The client’s legal team determines appropriate scope and the legal effect of the records, including priority and enforceability.", "text": "No. AADS supplies factual records and requested research from the agreed sources. The client’s legal team determines appropriate scope and the legal effect of the records, including priority and enforceability."}, {"title": "What is needed for an apostille or legalization request?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-13", "type": "faq", "topics": ["international"], "summary": "Identify the document, issuing authority, destination country, receiving party’s instructions and date needed. These details help establish the processing route and whether document retrieval or another certification step is needed.", "text": "Identify the document, issuing authority, destination country, receiving party’s instructions and date needed. These details help establish the processing route and whether document retrieval or another certification step is needed."}, {"title": "Does AADS provide a legal or title opinion?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-14", "type": "faq", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "AADS provides factual research, document retrieval and filing support. The client’s legal or title professionals assess the legal effect of the records. Research scope, sources and outstanding items are identified with the agreed work.", "text": "AADS provides factual research, document retrieval and filing support. The client’s legal or title professionals assess the legal effect of the records. Research scope, sources and outstanding items are identified with the agreed work."}, {"title": "UCC Filing Acknowledgment or Search to Reflect: What to Request", "url": "./guide-ucc-search-to-reflect.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Keep the filing acknowledgment and the later indexed search result distinct. Identify the filing, exact debtor name and search through date your review requires.", "text": "UCC Filing Acknowledgment or Search to Reflect: What to Request | AADS AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER UCC Filing Acknowledgment or Search to Reflect: What to Request Keep the filing acknowledgment and the later indexed search result distinct. Identify the filing, exact debtor name and search through date your review requires. REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start Here Keep the filing acknowledgment and the later indexed search result distinct. Identify the filing, exact debtor name and search through date your review requires. Start with the Filing Evidence Provide the office’s filing acknowledgment, financing statement number, exact debtor name and filing date. Identify whether the request concerns an initial statement or a later amendment. Ask for the particular record or indexed-search result your reviewer needs. Request the Search Separately A search to reflect is a check for the identified filing in the filing office’s searchable records. Specify the office, exact search name, certification, copies and required through date. Indexing and the scope of the response depend on the office. A Washington Example Washington’s rules describe a search requested with an initial financing statement being conducted when relevant records through that filing date can be retrieved. The state’s search-response rules identify the search name, through date and returned filings. This is an example of one jurisdiction’s process, not a nationwide timing promise. A Useful Request “Please obtain a search to reflect financing statement [number] filed in [office] on [date] under [exact debtor name]. Include [certification/copies required], identify the through date and return the filing acknowledgment with the result.” Review the Record, Then Resolve Questions Compare the requested filing number and debtor information with the response. Flag an unavailable record or mismatch for follow-up rather than treating it as proof of legal invalidity. A filing acknowledgment and indexed search do not decide priority, perfection or enforceability. Official sources Washington WAC 308-391-502 — Search timing ↗ Washington WAC 308-391-505 — Search responses ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request. General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records. Related service Explore the relevant AADS service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Choosing a Property Research Report: Owner, Vesting, Mortgage or Defined-Depth Search", "url": "./guide-property-report-options.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Name the record questions your file needs answered, then confirm the owners, period, sources and supporting copies included in the request.", "text": "Choosing a Property Research Report: Owner, Vesting, Mortgage or Defined-Depth Search | AADS AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Choosing a Property Research Report: Owner, Vesting, Mortgage or Defined-Depth Search Name the record questions your file needs answered, then confirm the owners, period, sources and supporting copies included in the request. REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start Here Name the record questions your file needs answered, then confirm the owners, period, sources and supporting copies included in the request. A Report Name Is Only the Starting Point Current-owner, two-owner and full-search labels can conceal different scopes. Specify the property and parcel, relevant owner names, requested period and record categories. Agree the search depth with your legal or title team before ordering. Match the Request to the Deliverable Legal and vesting deed report: recorded deed reference, owner names, vesting wording and legal description. Open-mortgage research: located mortgage instruments and requested related assignments, modifications or releases. Current-owner / O&E research: agreed ownership period, encumbrance categories and copies. Two-owner or defined-depth research: the specified owners, years or chain, with included record categories stated. Tax, municipal and zoning research: separately identified offices and components. Recorded Documents and Other Sources Flagler County’s official-records guidance, for example, lists deeds, mortgages, assignments, satisfactions, plats, liens and other recorded instruments. That list describes a record source. It does not make each item part of every report or replace separate departmental and tax inquiries. A Useful Request “For [address/parcel] in [county/state], please quote [report type] covering [owners/period], including [record categories and copies]. Separately identify [tax/municipal components] and any unavailable records. Our matter reference is [reference] and the requested date is [date].” Keep the Result and the Opinion Distinct The report records factual findings within the agreed sources and scope. It does not insure title, establish loan ownership or decide the legal effect of an instrument. Refer questions about depth, gaps or enforceability to the reviewing professional. Official sources Flagler County Clerk — Official Records ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request. General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records. Related service Explore the relevant AADS service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Zoning Letters, Certificates of Occupancy and Permit Copies: Preparing Your Request", "url": "./guide-zoning-permit-records.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Identify the property, responsible authority and exact document or question. Zoning responses, occupancy records and permit copies are separate requests.", "text": "Zoning Letters, Certificates of Occupancy and Permit Copies: Preparing Your Request | AADS AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Zoning Letters, Certificates of Occupancy and Permit Copies: Preparing Your Request Identify the property, responsible authority and exact document or question. Zoning responses, occupancy records and permit copies are separate requests. REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start Here Identify the property, responsible authority and exact document or question. Zoning responses, occupancy records and permit copies are separate requests. Identify the Correct Authority Provide the address, parcel number, city and county. Identify the department or jurisdiction already known to be responsible. A mailing address alone may not identify the office that holds zoning or building records. For a Zoning Verification Letter State the current or intended use and the specific questions the recipient wants answered. Supply the recipient’s wording or checklist when available. Do not assume a general zoning letter answers every question about a proposed use, variances or compliance. For Occupancy or Permit Records Identify whether you need an existing certificate of occupancy, permit copy, inspection record or another named document. Provide permit numbers, construction dates and prior addresses when known. Ask the authority to identify unavailable documents rather than assuming every building has the same records. A Hillsborough County Example Available records and retrieval procedures are confirmed with the responsible jurisdiction. A Useful Request “For [address/parcel], please obtain [specific letter or existing record]. The intended use is [use]; the recipient needs answers to [questions]. Known permits or dates are [details]. Please confirm the responsible authority, fees, anticipated timing and any record limits.” Describe the Deliverable Accurately An existing occupancy certificate or permit copy is evidence from the issuing authority’s records. Obtaining the copy does not independently approve a new use or certify all work at the property. Keep any legal or technical assessment with the client’s advisers. Official sources Hillsborough County — Zoning verifications ↗ Hillsborough County — Building permitting records ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request. General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records. Related service Explore the relevant AADS service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "OFAC Potential Matches: Preparing the Search and Reviewing the Record", "url": "./guide-ofac-potential-matches.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "An OFAC search can return similar names. Record the search scope and available identifiers so the client’s compliance team can assess potential matches.", "text": "OFAC Potential Matches: Preparing the Search and Reviewing the Record | AADS AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER OFAC Potential Matches: Preparing the Search and Reviewing the Record An OFAC search can return similar names. Record the search scope and available identifiers so the client’s compliance team can assess potential matches. REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start Here An OFAC search can return similar names. Record the search scope and available identifiers so the client’s compliance team can assess potential matches. Define the Names Before the Search List each individual and entity separately. Provide exact names, known aliases, relevant countries and identifying details already available for the transaction. Confirm which lists are in scope and what search documentation your reviewer requires. Understand a Potential Match OFAC’s tool uses approximate name matching against its SDN and consolidated non-SDN lists. Similar names can return results for different parties. OFAC’s match-assessment guidance calls for comparison with available identifying details and escalation under the organization’s compliance procedures. Keep a Useful Search Record Subject names and aliases submitted. Agreed lists, source, search date and available search settings. Result entries and relevant identifying details. Supporting search evidence, unresolved questions and the reviewing contact. A Useful Request “Please document an OFAC list search for [names and aliases], with [identifying details available]. Confirm the lists in scope and include the date, search evidence and potential matches. Send identity questions to [reviewing contact] before treating a result as resolved.” Keep Screening Separate from Transaction Approval Name screening is one input to a broader sanctions assessment. Ownership and geographic restrictions can require additional review. A result with no matching name does not authorize a transaction; the client’s legal or compliance team determines the outcome. Official sources OFAC — Sanctions List Search tool ↗ OFAC FAQ 5 — Assessing potential name matches ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request. General request-preparation information. AADS supplies factual research and document services; the client’s advisers determine legal scope and the legal effect of records. Related service Explore the relevant AADS service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "What is a UCC search to reflect?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-15", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The result identifies whether the specified filing appears in the office’s searchable records through the reported date, providing evidence for your legal team’s assessment.", "text": "What is a UCC search to reflect? The result identifies whether the specified filing appears in the office’s searchable records through the reported date, providing evidence for your legal team’s assessment."}, {"title": "How does a fixture filing search differ from a state UCC search?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-16", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The requests may involve different filing offices and record indexes. Identify the debtor, property, state and county and specify the sources required. A state UCC search should not be assumed to include the real-property records requested for fixture research.", "text": "How does a fixture filing search differ from a state UCC search? The requests may involve different filing offices and record indexes. Identify the debtor, property, state and county and specify the sources required. A state UCC search should not be assumed to include the real-property records requested for fixture research."}, {"title": "Can I request UCC filings, amendments and continuations?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-17", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Yes. Identify the filing action and office, supply authorized instructions and the relevant financing statement or filing number, and state the deadline. The submission acknowledgment and any separately requested search to reflect are different deliverables.", "text": "Can I request UCC filings, amendments and continuations? Yes. Identify the filing action and office, supply authorized instructions and the relevant financing statement or filing number, and state the deadline. The submission acknowledgment and any separately requested search to reflect are different deliverables."}, {"title": "Which property search depth should I request?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-18", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Specify a current-owner search, two-owner search or a defined period or chain of records. State the records, tax information and supporting copies required. AADS confirms the requested coverage; your legal or title team selects the depth appropriate to the matter.", "text": "Which property search depth should I request? Specify a current-owner search, two-owner search or a defined period or chain of records. State the records, tax information and supporting copies required. AADS confirms the requested coverage; your legal or title team selects the depth appropriate to the matter."}, {"title": "What does a legal and vesting deed report contain?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-19", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The agreed report identifies the owner names and vesting language shown in the recorded deed, its recording reference and the property’s legal description. It is a focused record report. Request lien, mortgage, tax or broader chain research separately where needed.", "text": "What does a legal and vesting deed report contain? The agreed report identifies the owner names and vesting language shown in the recorded deed, its recording reference and the property’s legal description. It is a focused record report. Request lien, mortgage, tax or broader chain research separately where needed."}, {"title": "What is included in an assignment-record request?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-20", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Identify the mortgage or deed of trust and request the recorded assignments and related modifications or releases within the agreed sources and period. The research can list located instruments and apparent gaps for review; it does not decide ownership of a loan or legal enforceability.", "text": "What is included in an assignment-record request? Identify the mortgage or deed of trust and request the recorded assignments and related modifications or releases within the agreed sources and period. The research can list located instruments and apparent gaps for review; it does not decide ownership of a loan or legal enforceability."}, {"title": "What is needed for a zoning letter, certificate of occupancy or permit-copy request?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-21", "type": "faq", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Provide the address, parcel identifier, responsible city or county if known, exact document requested and date needed. For zoning, include the intended use and questions for the authority. For occupancy and permit records, include known permit numbers, building dates or prior addresses. Availability depends on the authority’s records.", "text": "What is needed for a zoning letter, certificate of occupancy or permit-copy request? Provide the address, parcel identifier, responsible city or county if known, exact document requested and date needed. For zoning, include the intended use and questions for the authority. For occupancy and permit records, include known permit numbers, building dates or prior addresses. Availability depends on the authority’s records."}, {"title": "What should I provide for an OFAC sanctions search?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-22", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "Provide the exact individual or entity name, known aliases, relevant countries and identifying information already available for the matter. Confirm the lists, names, search date and documentation required. Identify additional transaction parties separately.", "text": "What should I provide for an OFAC sanctions search? Provide the exact individual or entity name, known aliases, relevant countries and identifying information already available for the matter. Confirm the lists, names, search date and documentation required. Identify additional transaction parties separately."}, {"title": "What does an OFAC potential match mean?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-23", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "Potential matches are assessed against the subject’s identifying details, with findings provided for your compliance or legal team’s review.", "text": "What does an OFAC potential match mean? Potential matches are assessed against the subject’s identifying details, with findings provided for your compliance or legal team’s review."}, {"title": "Does an OFAC name search establish complete sanctions compliance?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-24", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "No. It documents the agreed list search for the supplied names at a point in time. Ownership, geographic restrictions and other sanctions issues may require separate review. A no-match result is not approval of a transaction.", "text": "Does an OFAC name search establish complete sanctions compliance? No. It documents the agreed list search for the supplied names at a point in time. Ownership, geographic restrictions and other sanctions issues may require separate review. A no-match result is not approval of a transaction."}, {"title": "Can I request foreign qualification, annual reports or entity changes?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-25", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Yes. Identify the entity, formation jurisdiction, state where the filing is needed and the requested action. Provide the file number if known, authorized instructions, supporting documents and deadline. AADS confirms the filing requirements, fees and anticipated timing for that request.", "text": "Can I request foreign qualification, annual reports or entity changes? Yes. Identify the entity, formation jurisdiction, state where the filing is needed and the requested action. Provide the file number if known, authorized instructions, supporting documents and deadline. AADS confirms the filing requirements, fees and anticipated timing for that request."}, {"title": "What information is needed for a registered agent appointment or change?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-26", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Provide the entity’s legal name, formation state, file number if known, each state requiring an appointment, and an authorized end-client contact. Identify whether the entity is new or changing agents. Appointment acceptance, consent and filing requirements are confirmed before proceeding.", "text": "What information is needed for a registered agent appointment or change? Provide the entity’s legal name, formation state, file number if known, each state requiring an appointment, and an authorized end-client contact. Identify whether the entity is new or changing agents. Appointment acceptance, consent and filing requirements are confirmed before proceeding."}, {"title": "Is a registered agent appointment the same as arranging service of process?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-27", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "No. An agent appointment concerns the designated recipient for notices and service of process for an entity. Arranging delivery of legal papers to another party is a separate request with different instructions and availability.", "text": "Is a registered agent appointment the same as arranging service of process? No. An agent appointment concerns the designated recipient for notices and service of process for an entity. Arranging delivery of legal papers to another party is a separate request with different instructions and availability."}, {"title": "Are annual reports and ongoing monitoring included with an agent appointment?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-28", "type": "faq", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Do not assume they are included. Confirm the appointment, notice-handling arrangements, any annual-report filing work and any separately available follow-up services in the agreed scope.", "text": "Are annual reports and ongoing monitoring included with an agent appointment? Do not assume they are included. Confirm the appointment, notice-handling arrangements, any annual-report filing work and any separately available follow-up services in the agreed scope."}, {"title": "Public-Record Support for Law Firms", "url": "./for-law-firms.html", "type": "audience", "topics": ["general", "courts", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "international"], "summary": "Your deal spans states. Get the records your multistate matter needs through one point of contact.", "text": "Public-Record Support for Law Firms | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Public-Record Support for Law Firms Your deal spans states. Get the records your multistate matter needs through one point of contact. For Law Firms The Records and Follow-Through Your Team Needs Coordinate the factual research, official documents and filing evidence your attorneys and paralegals need for a transaction or matter. Define the names, sources and deliverables once, then keep related requests connected. Choose the Work in Front of You Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research Specify the courts, period, case categories, dockets and documents needed. Explore this service → Prepare this request → UCC and Lien Due Diligence Coordinate the requested state, county and federal research components. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Official Entity Records and Filings Match certificates of good standing, copies and filing instructions to the right entity. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Documents for International Use Confirm origin, destination and the receiving party’s certification requirements. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Prepare Your Request Exact party or entity names; identify additional names and related entities separately. States, counties, courts, search period and the documents your reviewing team needs. Any required certifications, copies, page limits and recipient instructions. Matter reference, responsible reviewer and date needed. Example Request “For [matter], please quote UCC and lien research for [entity/name] in [states/counties], civil litigation in [courts], bankruptcy within [scope], and a certificate of good standing from [state]. Include [specified copies] and identify any separate components, fees or source limits.” What the Handoff Should Make Clear The record package should make it clear what was searched, what was located and which documents remain outstanding. AADS supplies factual research and authorized filing support; your team makes the legal judgments about scope and effect. Answers for Your Request Can related requests use one matter reference? Yes. Identify the matter reference and each subject, jurisdiction and deliverable separately. The confirmed scope can coordinate the related research and document work through one point of contact. Can I request a particular complaint, order or judgment? Yes. Provide the court, case number and docket entry or document description if known. Identify certification and copy requirements; availability and any copy charges are confirmed for the request. Related Services and Guides GUIDE Prepare a Multistate UCC and Lien Request Use the checklist to define the records and deliverables. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Find plain-language explanations linked to related services. Explore → UCC Filing and Ongoing Follow-Through Coordinate an authorized filing, request a search to reflect, or discuss the dates and record changes your team wants to track. Monitoring coverage and availability are confirmed before enrollment. The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Public-Record Research for Lending Teams", "url": "./for-lenders.html", "type": "audience", "topics": ["general", "ucc-liens", "courts", "corporate", "property"], "summary": "Have the search results and supporting copies ready for your financing review.", "text": "Public-Record Research for Lending Teams | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Public-Record Research for Lending Teams Have the search results and supporting copies ready for your financing review. For Lenders The Records and Follow-Through Your Team Needs Bring borrower, entity, lien and related court records together before funding, renewal or a defined collateral review. Tell AADS what your credit or legal team requires so the research matches the decision in front of you. Choose the Work in Front of You UCC, Tax Lien and Judgment Research Define debtor names, jurisdictions, search certificates and copies. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Litigation and Bankruptcy Research Identify the courts, period and supporting records required for review. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Entity Records and Good Standing Request the official entity document required by the receiving team. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Property and Recorded Mortgage Research Specify property, title depth, mortgage instruments, assignments and taxes. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Prepare Your Request Exact borrower and related-party names, entity types and formation jurisdictions. The states, counties, courts and collateral-related sources selected by your reviewing team. Required UCC certifications, search through dates and supporting copies. Any filing acknowledgment to be checked by a search to reflect, plus the deadline and matter reference. Example Request “For [borrower] and [related names], please quote the agreed state/county lien searches, litigation in [courts] and bankruptcy research within [scope]. Include the required search certificates, supporting copies and source dates. Please separately quote a search to reflect filing [number] in [office].” What the Handoff Should Make Clear Clearly identified records, sources and search dates give your legal and credit teams the evidence needed to assess the findings. Answers for Your Request Can the request cover several borrowers or jurisdictions? Yes. List every subject and jurisdiction separately and specify the record categories and copies for each. AADS confirms the combined scope, fees and anticipated timing. Is a search to reflect automatically included with a filing? Confirm it separately. A filing acknowledgment records the office’s submission result, while a search to reflect checks the identified filing in indexed records through the reported date. Related Services and Guides GUIDE Filing Acknowledgment or Search to Reflect Use the checklist to define the records and deliverables. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Find plain-language explanations linked to related services. Explore → UCC Filing and Ongoing Follow-Through Coordinate an authorized filing, request a search to reflect, or discuss the dates and record changes your team wants to track. Monitoring coverage and availability are confirmed before enrollment. The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Property Research for Title & Settlement Teams", "url": "./for-title-settlement-teams.html", "type": "audience", "topics": ["general", "property", "ofac"], "summary": "Closing day is too late for surprises—get the answers before the scramble.", "text": "Property Research for Title & Settlement Teams | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Property Research for Title & Settlement Teams Closing day is too late for surprises—get the answers before the scramble. For Title & Settlement Teams The Records and Follow-Through Your Team Needs Bring the ownership research, local-source responses, tax information and supporting documents your closing file needs together. Identify the property and required components so recorded land records and departmental inquiries each receive the right scope. Choose the Work in Front of You Title and Ownership Reports Specify current-owner, two-owner or defined-depth research and copies. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Municipal and Property-Tax Research Identify code, permits, utilities, assessments, taxes and responsible providers. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Zoning, Occupancy and Permit Documents Request the exact authority response or existing document needed. Explore this service → Prepare this request → OFAC Sanctions Searches Identify transaction parties and the agreed list-search documentation. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Prepare Your Request Property address, parcel identifier, county and municipality if known. Current owner and legal description where available. Title depth, municipal components, utility providers and requested copies. Closing date, client matter reference and separate recording instructions. Example Request “For [address/parcel] in [county/state], please quote [title depth] with [copies]. Separately identify municipal code, open-permit, utility and assessment responses, plus [tax years]. The target closing date is [date]. Please confirm the responsible offices and any outstanding source responses.” What the Handoff Should Make Clear A recorded-document search and a response from a utility or code office are separate research paths. The deliverable should identify each requested component, the responding source and unresolved items. AADS supplies factual records for your title and legal review. Answers for Your Request Does title research automatically include municipal responses? No. Identify the municipal components separately and confirm the departments or providers responsible for the property. The confirmed request states which records and responses are included. Can recording work be coordinated with the research? Yes, under separate confirmed recording instructions. Identify the instrument, jurisdiction, required originals or electronic format and date needed. Related Services and Guides GUIDE Prepare Real Estate Title and Municipal Lien Research Together Use the checklist to define the records and deliverables. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Find plain-language explanations linked to related services. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Official Entity Records for Accountants & CPAs", "url": "./for-accountants-cpas.html", "type": "audience", "topics": ["general", "corporate", "international"], "summary": "Don’t let a missing certificate of good standing hold up the deal. Get the official records and documents your business clients need.", "text": "Official Entity Records for Accountants & CPAs | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Official Entity Records for Accountants & CPAs Don’t let a missing certificate of good standing hold up the deal. Get the official records and documents your business clients need. For Accountants & CPAs The Records and Follow-Through Your Team Needs Coordinate entity documents, authorized filings and international-use certification for your business clients. Start with the receiving party’s exact requirement so the request identifies the right entity, document and jurisdiction. Choose the Work in Front of You Certificates of Good Standing and Certified Copies Keep entity-status certificates distinct from copies of filed documents. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Entity Filing Support Identify the approved formation, qualification, amendment, annual report or other filing action. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Registered Agent Coordination Prepare appointment or change requests with an authorized end-client contact. Explore this service → Prepare this request → International Document Use Confirm issuing authority, destination country and recipient instructions. Explore this service → Prepare this request → Prepare Your Request Exact legal entity name, entity type, formation state and file number if known. The receiving party’s document wording and any issuance-date requirement. Plain or certified copies, requested filings and authorized instructions. Destination country for international use, client reference and deadline. Example Request “For [entity] formed in [state], please obtain the certificate of good standing required by [recipient], issued within [required period], and a certified copy of [specific filing]. The documents will be used in [country]. Please confirm the certification route, fees and anticipated timing.” What the Handoff Should Make Clear An online status result, official certificate of good standing and certified copy are distinct deliverables. AADS coordinates the requested factual records and authorized submissions. Tax, legal-structure and continuing compliance decisions remain with the client’s advisers. Answers for Your Request Can AADS obtain a certificate of good standing for my client? Yes. Provide the exact entity name, issuing jurisdiction, file number if known, recipient wording and date needed. The issuing office determines the available certificate and its stated meaning. Can document retrieval and international certification be coordinated? Yes. Identify the document, issuing authority, destination country and receiving party’s requirements. AADS confirms the retrieval and certification steps for that request. Related Services and Guides GUIDE Good Standing, Online Status and Certified Copies Use the checklist to define the records and deliverables. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Find plain-language explanations linked to related services. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Florida Public-Record Research: UCC, Property and Municipal Requests", "url": "./guide-florida-public-records.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Separate Florida’s central UCC registry, county recorded instruments and local departmental responses when preparing a transaction-research request.", "text": "Florida Public-Record Research: UCC, Property and Municipal Requests | AADS Skip to main content ☰ Services Records & Research UCC & Lien Research Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Real Estate Title & Municipal Liens OFAC & Sanctions Searches UCC & Lien Monitoring Documents & Filings UCC Filings & Continuations Corporate Documents & Filings Registered Agent Services Apostille & Legalization Support for Your Team Law Firms Lenders Title & Settlement Teams Accountants & CPAs View All Services → Search the Intelligence Center → Packages Intelligence Center About AADS Place an Order Get in Touch AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Florida Public-Record Research: UCC, Property and Municipal Requests Separate Florida’s central UCC registry, county recorded instruments and local departmental responses when preparing a transaction-research request. Home / Florida Public-Record Research: UCC, Property and Municipal Requests Nationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub → Florida’s Department of State directs UCC forms, filings, searches, copies and certification requests to the Florida Secured Transaction Registry. Corporate entity information remains a separate Sunbiz service. Current official UCC guidance · Source checked 11 November 2026. STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start with the Source Map Use this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope. Which Source Answers Which Question? Research component Responsible source Define before ordering Central UCC filings and searches The Florida Secured Transaction Registry, identified by the Department of State Exact debtor names, certification, copies and the search through date Recorded property instruments The responsible county Clerk or recording office Property, owners, search depth, deeds and specified encumbrance records Municipal and building records Responsible city, county department or utility provider Code, permit, occupancy, utilities and assessments as separate components State/county and federal litigation Named court clerks and separately defined federal sources Courts, names, period, categories, dockets and copies Keep the Central and County Requests Distinct Florida’s Department of State directs UCC forms, filings, searches, copies and certification requests to its designated registry. That central UCC request should not be used as a label for every property or county-record inquiry. Identify fixture or real-property record work separately when instructed. Identify the County and the Local Responders Flagler County’s official-records description includes deeds, mortgages, assignments, liens and other instruments. Hillsborough County’s building-record guidance uses separate retrieval routes and date ranges for permits and occupancy records. These illustrate different sources; they do not define a standard package for every Florida property. Ask for the Actual Documents State whether you need a UCC search report, a certified search response, document images, a recorded instrument or a departmental response. For a certificate of occupancy or permit, provide the specific document type, property identifiers and known permit details. Older or unavailable records may need direct follow-up. Request Checklist Exact subject names and the requested central UCC search and copy requirements. Property address, parcel identifier, county, owner period and requested title depth. Municipal components and known city, county or utility providers. Named courts or federal districts and any docket or certified-copy requirements. Closing or matter deadline, delivery requirements and separately confirmed fees. Example Request “For [entity] and [Florida property], please quote the central UCC search with [certification/copies], the requested county property research covering [depth], and separate [code/permit/utility/assessment] inquiries. Identify the responsible offices, search through dates and outstanding responses. Our deadline is [date].” Make the Result Assessable Ask the deliverable to identify the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual matter. Answers for Your Request Does the central Florida UCC registry replace county property research? No. The central registry handles its UCC records. Recorded property instruments and requested local-department responses are different sources and must be defined separately. Are all Florida permit or occupancy records available online? Availability depends on the authority, record type and date. Hillsborough County’s instructions, for example, identify different retrieval routes for different periods. Confirm the responsible authority and required document. Official Sources Florida Department of State — UCC information ↗ Florida Secured Transaction Registry — help and scope ↗ Flagler County Clerk — official records ↗ Hillsborough County — building permitting records ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request. Related Services and Guides SERVICE UCC and Lien Research Define the search and filing evidence needed. Explore → SERVICE Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research Specify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents. Explore → SERVICE Real Estate Title and Municipal Lien Research Coordinate property records and local responses. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Understand the terms used in your request. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order → Public Records Intelligence Center. Research. Knowledge. Human review. Quick Links → Home → Why Choose Us → Intelligence Center → Services → Packages → Our Process → Place an Order → Glossary → FAQs → Blog & Insights Get In Touch 866-318-8638 requests@allamericandocs.com Start the conversation → Get your next request moving. One point of contact. Clear follow-through. © 2026 All American Document Services, LLC Nationwide service"}, {"title": "Washington Public-Record Research: UCC, Entity and County Sources", "url": "./guide-washington-public-records.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "courts", "property", "corporate"], "summary": "Route Washington UCC requests to the Department of Licensing, entity records to the Secretary of State and county or court records to their responsible custodians.", "text": "Washington Public-Record Research: UCC, Entity and County Sources | AADS Skip to main content ☰ Services Records & Research UCC & Lien Research Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Real Estate Title & Municipal Liens OFAC & Sanctions Searches UCC & Lien Monitoring Documents & Filings UCC Filings & Continuations Corporate Documents & Filings Registered Agent Services Apostille & Legalization Support for Your Team Law Firms Lenders Title & Settlement Teams Accountants & CPAs View All Services → Search the Intelligence Center → Packages Intelligence Center About AADS Place an Order Get in Touch AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Washington Public-Record Research: UCC, Entity and County Sources Route Washington UCC requests to the Department of Licensing, entity records to the Secretary of State and county or court records to their responsible custodians. Home / Washington Public-Record Research: UCC, Entity and County Sources Nationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub → Washington UCC filing and UCC-11 searches are handled by the Department of Licensing, while business-entity records are handled by the Secretary of State. Its UCC forms distinguish information requests from amendments and information statements. Current official UCC guidance · Source checked 11 November 2026. STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start with the Source Map Use this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope. Which Source Answers Which Question? Research component Responsible source Define before ordering Central UCC records Washington Department of Licensing Debtor names, filing references, search type and copies Business entity records Secretary of State Corporations and Charities Filing System (CCFS) Entity name or UBI, filing history and the required certificate or copy Recorded property documents The responsible county recording office Property, instrument references, owner period and copies Court case information and documents Washington court search resources and the court of record Court, names, period, case number and underlying documents Use the Correct State Office Washington’s UCC filing and search guidance is published by the Department of Licensing. Corporate entity records are maintained through the Secretary of State’s CCFS. These are separate systems; an entity-status lookup is not a UCC search. Keep the Acknowledgment and Search Result Together Provide the filing acknowledgment and number, together with any separately requested search-to-reflect requirements, so your reviewing team receives the appropriate evidence. Check County and Court Coverage Thurston County’s recording guidance is one example of a county property source and distinguishes indexed information from documents available online. Washington Courts also explains that its name/case search is reference information; the official case record and document copies are held by the court of record. Request Checklist Exact debtor names and the Department of Licensing UCC request type. Legal entity name, UBI if known and the exact CCFS document or status certificate needed. County, property identifiers and any known recording references. Court and case information, period, dockets and copies needed. Filing acknowledgment, search through-date requirement, matter reference and deadline. Example Request “Please quote a Washington Department of Licensing UCC search for [exact name], with [certification/copies], and separately retrieve [entity document] from CCFS. For [county/property], include [specified records]. For court work, identify [court/period] and the documents required. Keep each source and through date distinct.” Make the Result Assessable Ask the deliverable to identify the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual matter. Answers for Your Request Does Washington’s Secretary of State handle the central UCC search described here? Washington’s Department of Licensing provides the central UCC filing and search service. The Secretary of State’s CCFS is the separate source for corporate entity records. Does the Washington name-and-case search provide the full official case file? Washington Courts states that the search is reference information. The court of record maintains the official case record, and requested copies should be obtained through the appropriate court process. Official Sources Washington Department of Licensing — UCC filing and searches ↗ Washington Secretary of State — CCFS records tools ↗ Washington Courts — name and case search limits ↗ Thurston County Auditor — property records ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request. Related Services and Guides SERVICE UCC and Lien Research Define the search and filing evidence needed. Explore → SERVICE Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research Specify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents. Explore → SERVICE Real Estate Title and Municipal Lien Research Coordinate property records and local responses. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Understand the terms used in your request. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order → Public Records Intelligence Center. Research. Knowledge. Human review. Quick Links → Home → Why Choose Us → Intelligence Center → Services → Packages → Our Process → Place an Order → Glossary → FAQs → Blog & Insights Get In Touch 866-318-8638 requests@allamericandocs.com Start the conversation → Get your next request moving. One point of contact. Clear follow-through. © 2026 All American Document Services, LLC Nationwide service"}, {"title": "Georgia Public-Record Research: UCC, Recorded Liens and Court Sources", "url": "./guide-georgia-public-records.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Distinguish GSCCCA record indexes from the specific Georgia courts needed for litigation research, and define the names, counties, period and copies for each request.", "text": "Georgia Public-Record Research: UCC, Recorded Liens and Court Sources | AADS Skip to main content ☰ Services Records & Research UCC & Lien Research Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Real Estate Title & Municipal Liens OFAC & Sanctions Searches UCC & Lien Monitoring Documents & Filings UCC Filings & Continuations Corporate Documents & Filings Registered Agent Services Apostille & Legalization Support for Your Team Law Firms Lenders Title & Settlement Teams Accountants & CPAs View All Services → Search the Intelligence Center → Packages Intelligence Center About AADS Place an Order Get in Touch AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Georgia Public-Record Research: UCC, Recorded Liens and Court Sources Distinguish GSCCCA record indexes from the specific Georgia courts needed for litigation research, and define the names, counties, period and copies for each request. Home / Georgia Public-Record Research: UCC, Recorded Liens and Court Sources Nationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub → Georgia’s GSCCCA UCC index offers name, filing-number, county/date and secured-party searches. Its notice effective 1 November 2026 requires UCC-11 information requests to be submitted electronically through Certified Search Request. Current official UCC guidance · Source checked 11 November 2026. GSCCCA UCC-11 electronic-submission notice STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start with the Source Map Use this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope. Which Source Answers Which Question? Research component Responsible source Define before ordering UCC record research GSCCCA UCC index and certified-search routes Exact debtor names, search method, certification and copies Real estate, recorded liens and plats GSCCCA indexes and the responsible county custodian County, property and instrument details, index coverage and copies State-system civil case research The relevant Georgia court and clerk Court class, county, names, period and case categories Federal litigation and bankruptcy Separately identified federal court or PACER scope Districts or index scope, subjects, dockets and documents Treat Each Index as a Defined Source GSCCCA lists separate UCC, real-estate, lien and plat search resources, including a certified UCC search request route. Select the index and requested deliverable explicitly. An index result and a certified response or underlying instrument are different records. Name the Georgia Court Type Georgia’s judicial guidance describes several trial-court classes. Superior, state and magistrate court sources can involve different records. The request should identify the court classes and counties chosen by the reviewing team; the phrase “county litigation search” alone leaves those choices open. Confirm Coverage and Obtain the Record The judicial directory helps identify the relevant court contact. County custody, available indexes, historical coverage, images and certification requirements need confirmation for the actual request. Federal district and bankruptcy records are a separate research path. Request Checklist Each subject name and the requested GSCCCA index, certification and copy requirements. County, property identifiers, instrument references and title depth where relevant. The actual superior, state, magistrate or other court sources requested. Civil case categories, period, pending/closed instructions and any known case numbers. Separate federal/bankruptcy scope, desired documents, matter reference and deadline. Example Request “For [subject] in [Georgia county], please quote the specified GSCCCA UCC/lien research and civil litigation in [named court classes]. Include [period/status categories], located case references and [documents/copies]. Separately confirm any federal or bankruptcy research. Identify the indexes and courts actually searched.” Make the Result Assessable Ask the deliverable to identify the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual matter. Answers for Your Request Does a GSCCCA recorded-lien search replace civil litigation research? No. Specify the recorded indexes and the court case sources separately. They answer different record questions and may have different coverage and deliverables. Which Georgia courts should be included? Your reviewing team should identify the appropriate court classes and counties for the matter. AADS confirms the requested sources and deliverables; one court search should not be represented as every Georgia court. Official Sources GSCCCA — UCC, real estate, lien and plat indexes ↗ Georgia Courts — trial-court types ↗ Georgia Courts — official directory and records resources ↗ PACER — finding federal court cases ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request. Related Services and Guides SERVICE UCC and Lien Research Define the search and filing evidence needed. Explore → SERVICE Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research Specify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents. Explore → SERVICE Real Estate Title and Municipal Lien Research Coordinate property records and local responses. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Understand the terms used in your request. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order → Public Records Intelligence Center. Research. Knowledge. Human review. Quick Links → Home → Why Choose Us → Intelligence Center → Services → Packages → Our Process → Place an Order → Glossary → FAQs → Blog & Insights Get In Touch 866-318-8638 requests@allamericandocs.com Start the conversation → Get your next request moving. One point of contact. Clear follow-through. © 2026 All American Document Services, LLC Nationwide service"}, {"title": "North Carolina Public-Record Research: UCC, Deeds and Court Records", "url": "./guide-north-carolina-public-records.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Define Secretary of State UCC work, county land-record requests and court-record retrieval separately, with the exact names, sources, dates and certifications your matter needs.", "text": "North Carolina Public-Record Research: UCC, Deeds and Court Records | AADS Skip to main content ☰ Services Records & Research UCC & Lien Research Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Real Estate Title & Municipal Liens OFAC & Sanctions Searches UCC & Lien Monitoring Documents & Filings UCC Filings & Continuations Corporate Documents & Filings Registered Agent Services Apostille & Legalization Support for Your Team Law Firms Lenders Title & Settlement Teams Accountants & CPAs View All Services → Search the Intelligence Center → Packages Intelligence Center About AADS Place an Order Get in Touch AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER North Carolina Public-Record Research: UCC, Deeds and Court Records Define Secretary of State UCC work, county land-record requests and court-record retrieval separately, with the exact names, sources, dates and certifications your matter needs. Home / North Carolina Public-Record Research: UCC, Deeds and Court Records Nationwide support across all 50 states. Explore the 50-state guide hub → North Carolina’s Secretary of State distinguishes free UCC research from an office search requested on UCC-11. Its UCC section maintains UCC and federal lien documents; deed and court requests require separate source instructions. Current official UCC guidance · Source checked 11 November 2026. STATE REQUEST-PREPARATION GUIDE · 10 NOVEMBER 2026 Start with the Source Map Use this guide to prepare the request. It maps source types and questions to confirm; it is not a statewide completeness certificate or a legal determination of required scope. Which Source Answers Which Question? Research component Responsible source Define before ordering Central UCC records North Carolina Secretary of State UCC section Debtor names, search/certification requirements, UCC-11 where requested and copies Deeds and recorded instruments The responsible county Register of Deeds County, property, owner names, instrument references and period Court-related land records and civil cases The relevant Clerk of Superior Court and court sources Case categories, names, court, period and copies Federal cases and bankruptcy Separately defined PACER or federal court scope Districts or index scope, subject names and requested records Specify the UCC Deliverable The Secretary of State maintains a UCC section and publishes its forms, including a UCC-11 information request. An interactive lookup, requested official response and copies of specified filings should not be treated as interchangeable. Identify the required deliverable and search through date. Separate Deeds from Court Records North Carolina’s land-record guidance identifies both county Register of Deeds offices and county Clerks of Superior Court as sources for different records affecting land. A deed copy or a single index search does not answer every property or litigation question. Use Court Search Results as a Starting Point The Judicial Branch explains access to case information and how to request court files and copies from the clerk. Identify the court, case number and particular documents needed. Online access and a docket listing are different from obtaining an official or certified record. Request Checklist Exact debtor or entity names, requested UCC search response and copy requirements. County, property address, parcel identifier, owners and defined search depth. Requested Register of Deeds records and any court-source components. Court, case categories, period and specified documents or certifications. Separate federal/bankruptcy instructions, matter reference and date needed. Example Request “For [subject/property] in [North Carolina county], please quote [defined UCC response and copies], the requested Register of Deeds research, and civil court research in [named court/source] for [period]. Obtain [specified documents/certifications] and identify source dates and unavailable records.” Make the Result Assessable Ask the deliverable to identify the exact names and property, sources and criteria used, covered period or through date, located references, supporting copies and any outstanding items. Online coverage, authentication, historical records and source response times vary. Scope, source fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the actual matter. Answers for Your Request Is a deed search the same as all North Carolina land-related research? No. The Secretary of State’s land-record guidance identifies different records at the county Register of Deeds and the Clerk of Superior Court. Specify the offices and record categories your reviewing team requires. What should I specify for a UCC-11 or other UCC request? Identify the exact debtor name, office, search response or certification, supporting copies and through-date requirement. Confirm the available request method and deliverable with the filing source. Official Sources North Carolina Secretary of State — UCC section ↗ North Carolina Secretary of State — UCC forms including UCC-11 ↗ North Carolina Secretary of State — land-record offices ↗ North Carolina Judicial Branch — obtaining court records ↗ PACER — finding federal court cases ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request. Related Services and Guides SERVICE UCC and Lien Research Define the search and filing evidence needed. Explore → SERVICE Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research Specify civil litigation, criminal searches, bankruptcy and court documents. Explore → SERVICE Real Estate Title and Municipal Lien Research Coordinate property records and local responses. Explore → GLOSSARY Public Records Glossary Understand the terms used in your request. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order → Public Records Intelligence Center. Research. Knowledge. Human review. Quick Links → Home → Why Choose Us → Intelligence Center → Services → Packages → Our Process → Place an Order → Glossary → FAQs → Blog & Insights Get In Touch 866-318-8638 requests@allamericandocs.com Start the conversation → Get your next request moving. One point of contact. Clear follow-through. © 2026 All American Document Services, LLC Nationwide service"}, {"title": "UCC, Lien & Litigation Research", "url": "./ucc-lien-research.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Have the search results and supporting copies ready for your financing review. Coordinate the requested UCC, lien, judgment, litigation and bankruptcy research through one point of contact.", "text": "UCC, Lien & Litigation Research | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER UCC, Lien & Litigation Research Have the search results and supporting copies ready for your financing review. Coordinate the requested UCC, lien, judgment, litigation and bankruptcy research through one point of contact. For transactional law firms, lenders, collateral teams and business-serving accountants. The Result Your Team Needs Get the UCC, lien and litigation findings and supporting records your legal or financing review needs. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Defined search findings The agreed names, jurisdictions, record sources and search dates or through dates, with located filing references. Requested search evidence Official search responses or certificates and supporting copies where available and included in the confirmed scope. Filing evidence, when ordered The office’s submission response or filing acknowledgment, plus any separately requested search to reflect. Outstanding items Unavailable records, unresolved source questions and components outside the agreed scope identified for your review. Additional names, jurisdictions, filing work, certifications, copies and rush handling are confirmed in the quote. Discuss Your Request Place an Order UCC, Lien and Court Research for Your Matter State and county UCC searches within the agreed jurisdictions. Tax lien and judgment research, with record sources identified. Civil litigation and bankruptcy research within requested courts and periods. Official UCC search certificates and supporting copies where available and requested. Related UCC filings and document retrieval by separate confirmed instruction. EPA / environmental lien research — upon request, with sources and jurisdictions confirmed. ERISA / pension-related lien research — upon request, with sources and jurisdictions confirmed. Other lien types — upon request; identify the lien category, subjects or property, and jurisdictions. Additional lien research is separately scoped and quoted. Coverage depends on the lien type and available records. Specify the sources, jurisdictions and supporting documents required. Prepare Your Request Exact subject name and entity type; identify additional names separately. Requested states, counties, courts, record categories and search period. Official UCC search certificate requirements, copy instructions and page limits. Matter reference, intended deliverables and date needed. Clear scope before work begins Your report identifies the records located, sources searched and relevant search dates within the agreed scope, supporting your legal team’s assessment. State, County & Federal UCC, Lien & Litigation Due Diligence Package Bring the search results and entity documentation your transaction needs together through one point of contact. UCC, tax lien and judgment lien searches — state level UCC, tax lien and judgment lien searches — county level Civil litigation search — federal district court Civil litigation search — county court Bankruptcy search Certificate of good standing Request a Quote UCC Filing and Post-Filing Support Keep the filing work and the evidence of that work connected to your matter. Initial UCC filing submissions under authorized client instructions. Amendment, continuation and termination submissions tied to the identified financing statement. County fixture filing searches and recording requests with the property and office identified. Searches to reflect requested filings, with the filing acknowledgment and search through date identified. A filing acknowledgment records the office’s response to a submission. A search to reflect is a separate check of the office’s indexed records; confirm whether both are required. Court Research and Supporting Records Define civil litigation and bankruptcy requests by subject, court, jurisdiction, period and requested records. Specify whether your team needs an index result, docket, pleadings, orders, judgments or certified copies. State, county and federal sources are identified separately. Request-preparation guide UCC filing acknowledgment or search to reflect: what should I request? → From Your Request to Delivery Share the subject or property, requested work and date needed. Confirm the jurisdiction-specific scope, fees, expected delivery format and anticipated timing. Coordinate the agreed research, filing or document work and review the returned material. Deliver the agreed work with any outstanding items or source limits identified. From the Intelligence Center How to prepare a multistate UCC and lien search request → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Answers for Your Next Request Does the due diligence package cover state, county and federal sources? Yes. The package brings together state and county UCC, tax lien and judgment lien searches, federal district and county civil litigation searches, bankruptcy research, and a certificate of good standing. The specific names, states, counties and courts are confirmed for each request. What records are commonly included in due diligence? Depending on the matter, the scope can include UCC records, tax liens, judgment liens, civil litigation, bankruptcy and a certificate of good standing. The research checklist identifies the sources and jurisdictions included. Does a UCC search establish lien priority or prove that collateral is clear? No. AADS supplies factual records and requested research from the agreed sources. The client’s legal team determines appropriate scope and the legal effect of the records, including priority and enforceability. What is a UCC search to reflect? The result identifies whether the specified filing appears in the office’s searchable records through the reported date, providing evidence for your legal team’s assessment. How does a fixture filing search differ from a state UCC search? The requests may involve different filing offices and record indexes. Identify the debtor, property, state and county and specify the sources required. A state UCC search should not be assumed to include the real-property records requested for fixture research. Can I request UCC filings, amendments and continuations? Yes. Identify the filing action and office, supply authorized instructions and the relevant financing statement or filing number, and state the deadline. The submission acknowledgment and any separately requested search to reflect are different deliverables. What is UCC11 (UCC-11), and what should I request? UCC11 (UCC-11) is a formal information request submitted, where accepted, to the appropriate UCC filing office for an official search of that office’s records and/or specified copies. The office conducts or processes the search through its staff or official system. The returned response and requested copies are the deliverables. Provide the exact debtor name, filing office, required scope, certification, copies and search-date requirements. The responsible office may be the Secretary of State or another agency; confirm current submission procedures. Read the glossary definition → · Official reference What is a transmitting utility UCC filing, and does it need continuations? Purpose: A UCC financing statement provides public notice of a claimed security interest in collateral. Transmitting utility is a special debtor classification for certain transportation, communications, pipeline and utility businesses. Where filed: A transmitting utility’s UCC financing statement is generally filed with the state’s designated central UCC filing office. Under the applicable transmitting-utility rule, that filing also serves as a fixture filing for the fixtures it identifies. Confirm the governing state’s requirements and any separate real-property recording needs. Duration: When the debtor qualifies and the filed initial financing statement indicates transmitting-utility status, the special duration rule keeps the financing statement effective until a termination statement is filed. It does not automatically lapse after five years under that rule. Continuations: Routine five-year continuation statements are not required for a financing statement governed by this continuing-effectiveness rule. Termination and review: An authorized termination statement ends the filing’s effectiveness. Confirm the debtor’s classification, the initial designation, governing-state rules and any later amendments or terminations with your legal team. Read the glossary definition → · Florida duration rule · North Carolina duration rule · Washington duration rule · Florida filing-office rule · North Carolina Article 9 — section 25-9-501 · Washington filing-office rule Related Services SERVICE OFAC & Sanctions Searches Document the sanctions-list searches requested for your transaction parties. Explore the service → SERVICE Real Estate Title & Municipal Lien Research Connect property, fixture and local-source research to the matter. Explore the service → Related research Court, litigation and bankruptcy research: define the courts and documents → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "Real Estate Title & Municipal Lien Research", "url": "./title-municipal-research.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Closing day is too late for surprises—get the answers before the scramble. Bring the property research, local-source responses and supporting documents your closing file needs together.", "text": "Real Estate Title & Municipal Lien Research | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Real Estate Title & Municipal Lien Research Closing day is too late for surprises—get the answers before the scramble. Bring the property research, local-source responses and supporting documents your closing file needs together. For title and settlement teams, real estate law firms and lenders. Title and municipal lien research for commercial and residential properties, scoped to your transaction and jurisdiction. The Result Your Team Needs Get property findings, municipal responses and supporting documents together for your closing review. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Property record findings The requested ownership, deed and encumbrance information, with property identifiers, record references and agreed search depth. Local-source responses The requested tax, code, permit, utility or assessment responses, with the responsible sources and available response dates. Supporting documents Specified deed, mortgage, assignment, zoning, occupancy or permit records where available and within scope. Completion and open items The agreed research and any recording response, with missing records or outstanding departmental responses identified. Title depth, municipal components, certificates, copies, recording and association requests are matched to the confirmed scope. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Property Research for Your Closing Checklist Title searches and ownership and encumbrance (O&E) research with an agreed search depth. Municipal research for requested code, permit, utility and assessment components. Tax information and related supporting records within the confirmed scope. Zoning verification and official document requests where available. Recording and property-document retrieval under confirmed instructions. Prepare Your Request Property address, state, county and parcel identifier. Owner information and legal description when available. Requested title depth and municipal research components. Any separate utility providers or agencies already identified. Copies, verification-letter requirements, matter reference and date needed. Clear scope before work begins Components and source availability vary by property and jurisdiction. Recorded land records and municipal departmental records are different research paths; confirm both where required. AADS reports the requested factual records and responses rather than issuing a title opinion or guaranteeing that a property is clear. Current Owner • Recording • Municipal Liens Real Estate Package Bring the ownership research, recording work and local-source responses your closing file needs together. Current owner title search (ownership & encumbrance) Recording of documents Municipal lien searches — responsible city, county or other local authority Request a Quote Choose the Property Report Your File Needs Current-owner and ownership & encumbrance research — agree the owner period, record categories and supporting copies. Two-owner or defined-depth title research — identify the owners, period or chain required. Legal and vesting deed report — recorded owner names, vesting language, deed reference and legal description. Open-mortgage and assignment-record research — identified mortgages, recorded assignments, modifications and releases within the requested scope. Property-tax research — the requested tax years, balances and relevant collection offices. Related retrieval — specified deeds, plats and foreclosure case records where available. Report names are a starting point. Confirm the sources, period, deliverable and outstanding items for the actual property; a focused deed report is different from a broader title search. Zoning Letters, Occupancy and Permit Records Obtain the specific local-authority response or document your review requires. Requests can include zoning verification letters, existing certificates of occupancy and permit copies, subject to the responsible authority’s records and procedures. Identify the intended use and questions for a zoning letter. For occupancy or permit copies, identify the building, permit numbers and relevant dates when known. Retrieval of a record is different from a determination that a proposed use or construction activity is lawful. Request-preparation guides Compare property report requests → · Prepare a zoning or permit-record request → From Your Request to Delivery Share the subject or property, requested work and date needed. Confirm the jurisdiction-specific scope, fees, expected delivery format and anticipated timing. Coordinate the agreed research, filing or document work and review the returned material. Deliver the agreed work with any outstanding items or source limits identified. From the Intelligence Center Title search and municipal research: what should the request include? → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Answers for Your Next Request What is included in the Real Estate Package? The package brings together a current owner title search, recording of documents, and municipal lien searches through the responsible city, county or other local authority. Confirm the property, search depth, municipal components and recording instructions for your quote. Does a title search include every municipal record? Recorded land records and local departmental records are separate research paths. Identify the municipal components required, such as code, permits, utilities or assessments, and confirm the responsible departments or providers for the property. Does AADS provide a legal or title opinion? AADS provides factual research, document retrieval and filing support. The client’s legal or title professionals assess the legal effect of the records. Research scope, sources and outstanding items are identified with the agreed work. Which property search depth should I request? Specify a current-owner search, two-owner search or a defined period or chain of records. State the records, tax information and supporting copies required. AADS confirms the requested coverage; your legal or title team selects the depth appropriate to the matter. What does a legal and vesting deed report contain? The agreed report identifies the owner names and vesting language shown in the recorded deed, its recording reference and the property’s legal description. It is a focused record report. Request lien, mortgage, tax or broader chain research separately where needed. What is included in an assignment-record request? Identify the mortgage or deed of trust and request the recorded assignments and related modifications or releases within the agreed sources and period. The research can list located instruments and apparent gaps for review; it does not decide ownership of a loan or legal enforceability. What is needed for a zoning letter, certificate of occupancy or permit-copy request? Provide the address, parcel identifier, responsible city or county if known, exact document requested and date needed. For zoning, include the intended use and questions for the authority. For occupancy and permit records, include known permit numbers, building dates or prior addresses. Availability depends on the authority’s records. Related Services SERVICE OFAC & Sanctions Searches Request documented list searches for the names in your property transaction. Explore the service → SERVICE UCC, Lien & Litigation Research Coordinate related lien, fixture and court research with supporting copies. Explore the service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "Corporate Documents, Filings & Registered Agent Support", "url": "./corporate-documents-filings.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Don’t let a missing certificate of good standing hold up the deal. Get the official entity records, filing work and registered agent support your business clients need. Business publication notices and supporting proof can be coordinated where required.", "text": "Corporate Documents, Filings & Registered Agent Support | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Corporate Documents, Filings & Registered Agent Support Don’t let a missing certificate of good standing hold up the deal. Get the official entity records, filing work and registered agent support your business clients need. For law firms, corporate legal teams, accountants and CPAs. The Result Your Team Needs Get certificates of good standing, corporate records and filing acknowledgments matched to the correct entity and jurisdiction. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Official entity documents The requested certificate of good standing or equivalent office-issued certificate, with its issuing jurisdiction and date. Specified filed records Plain or certified copies of the formation document or other identified filing, in the agreed available format. Submission evidence The responsible office’s acceptance, acknowledgment or other response for authorized filing work included in your request. Clear follow-through Unavailable documents, rejected or pending submissions and any further information needed are identified. Certificates, copies, filings, agent appointments and international certification are distinct components confirmed in your quote. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Certificates of Good Standing, Filings and Entity Records Certificates of good standing, including equivalent certificates of existence or status offered by the issuing office. Plain and certified copies of formation documents and other specified filings. Corporate filing submissions under approved client instructions. Registered agent appointment and coordination requests with scope confirmed. Related document retrieval and international-use coordination. Prepare Your Request Exact legal entity name, formation state and file number if known. The receiving party’s exact document or filing requirement. Plain or certified copies; short- or long-form certificate of good standing where offered. Any issuance-date requirement, destination country and date needed. Authorized filing instructions and contact details for any agent appointment. Clear scope before work begins Your filing acknowledgment documents the office’s response to the authorized submission. Ongoing filing and compliance requirements are confirmed separately. Filings Across the Entity Lifecycle Formations and name-related filing requests under approved instructions. Foreign qualification for a specified entity and destination state. Amendments, annual reports and reinstatement requests. Merger, conversion, dissolution and withdrawal submissions where available. Certificates of good standing and plain or certified copies of specified corporate filings. Identify the entity, requested action and filing state. Required approvals, forms, supporting documents, government fees and anticipated timing are confirmed for the specific request. Choosing a legal structure or deciding where an entity must qualify belongs with the client’s advisers. Registered Agent Appointments and Changes Keep the agent appointment connected to the correct entity, state and authorized client contact. Appointment or change coordination is confirmed separately from annual-report filing work and other corporate requests. See what to provide for a registered agent request → From Your Request to Delivery Share the subject or property, requested work and date needed. Confirm the jurisdiction-specific scope, fees, expected delivery format and anticipated timing. Coordinate the agreed research, filing or document work and review the returned material. Deliver the agreed work with any outstanding items or source limits identified. From the Intelligence Center Certificate of good standing, online status and certified copies: what to order → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Answers for Your Next Request What corporate compliance services does AADS support? AADS supports certificates of good standing, corporate record retrieval, plain and certified copies, filing submissions under authorized instructions, and registered agent coordination. The requested work and jurisdiction requirements are confirmed before proceeding. Can AADS help obtain certificates of good standing? Yes. Provide the exact legal entity name, issuing state, file number if known, recipient requirements and date needed. AADS coordinates the certificate of good standing or the equivalent certificate offered by that issuing office. Is a certificate of good standing the same as a certified copy? No. A certificate of good standing addresses entity status as defined by the issuing office. A certified copy reproduces a specified filed document with that office’s certification. Provide the receiving party’s exact requirement. Can I request foreign qualification, annual reports or entity changes? Yes. Identify the entity, formation jurisdiction, state where the filing is needed and the requested action. Provide the file number if known, authorized instructions, supporting documents and deadline. AADS confirms the filing requirements, fees and anticipated timing for that request. Related Services SERVICE Registered Agent Services Prepare an appointment or change request with the correct entity and client contact. Explore the service → SERVICE Apostille, Authentication & Legalization Coordinate the certification route for corporate documents intended for international use. Explore the service → Related research Court, litigation and bankruptcy research: define the courts and documents → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Business Publication Services. Formation, foreign qualification and assumed name (DBA) newspaper notices; placement, publication affidavits and required certificate filings. Requirements and scope vary by jurisdiction."}, {"title": "Apostille, Authentication & Legalization", "url": "./apostille-legalization.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["international"], "summary": "Get the certified documents your transaction is waiting on. Start with the document, its issuing authority and the country where it will be used.", "text": "Apostille, Authentication & Legalization | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Apostille, Authentication & Legalization Get the certified documents your transaction is waiting on. Start with the document, its issuing authority and the country where it will be used. For law firms, business-serving accountants, corporate teams and clients handling international document requirements. The Result Your Team Needs Get the document retrieval and certification work your international transaction requires, with origin and recipient requirements confirmed. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Requested source documents The official record or certified copy included in the agreed retrieval and international-use request. Certification output The apostille, authentication or legalization work available through the confirmed origin and destination route. Document and route identification The document, issuing authority and receiving-party requirements used to prepare the request. Delivery and outstanding items The agreed electronic or original-document delivery arrangements, with pending steps or unavailable items identified. Retrieval, translations where arranged, government processing, consular steps and original-document delivery are separately scoped and quoted. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Document Support for International Use Retrieval of specified official or certified documents where available. Apostille coordination through the relevant issuing authority. Authentication and legalization coordination for the confirmed destination. Review of stated recipient requirements and requested document format. Delivery and return-shipping arrangements within the agreed service. Prepare Your Request Document type, issuing authority and state or federal origin. Country of use and the receiving party’s written requirements. Whether the available document is an original, certified copy or other copy. Any translation, delivery or document-age requirement. Date needed and return-delivery instructions. Clear scope before work begins Confirm the required document preparation and processing route before proceeding. Authentication addresses the official signature or seal; the document’s contents are assessed separately. From Your Request to Delivery Share the subject or property, requested work and date needed. Confirm the jurisdiction-specific scope, fees, expected delivery format and anticipated timing. Coordinate the agreed research, filing or document work and review the returned material. Deliver the agreed work with any outstanding items or source limits identified. From the Intelligence Center What to provide before requesting an apostille or authentication → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Answers for Your Next Request What is needed for an apostille or legalization request? Identify the document, issuing authority, destination country, receiving party’s instructions and date needed. These details help establish the processing route and whether document retrieval or another certification step is needed. How long does a search or document request take? Timing depends on the service, jurisdiction, source availability and the records required. Share your deadline at the start. Anticipated timing and any available rush option are confirmed for the actual request. What determines the quote and document copy fees? The quote depends on the names or properties, jurisdictions, record categories, copies, certifications and any rush requirements. Confirm the service charges and applicable government or document fees in the quote before proceeding. Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request"}, {"title": "OFAC & Sanctions Searches", "url": "./ofac-sanctions-searches.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "Get documented OFAC sanctions-list search results for the names in your transaction, with the search date, sources and potential matches identified.", "text": "OFAC & Sanctions Searches | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER OFAC & Sanctions Searches Get documented OFAC sanctions-list search results for the names in your transaction, with the search date, sources and potential matches identified. Documented Results for Your Transaction Review Get documented OFAC sanctions-list search results for the names in your transaction, with the search date, sources and potential matches identified. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Names and list scope The supplied subject names and aliases, agreed sanctions lists and relevant identifying details used for the search. Dated search evidence The source, search date and available search settings or supporting evidence included in the request. Returned results The records or potential matches returned for the agreed list search, documented for the client’s review. Unresolved identity questions Potential matches and missing identifying details that require the client’s compliance or legal assessment. Additional parties, lists, repeat searches and wider compliance analysis are not assumed to be included in a name-search request. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Define the Names and Lists to Be Searched Request an OFAC sanctions-list search for the individuals and entities identified in your matter. Confirm the names, aliases, lists and supporting search evidence required. Additional parties or lists are separately defined scope. Prepare Your Request Exact individual or entity names; identify aliases and additional parties separately. Relevant countries and identifying details already available for the matter. Lists to be searched, search-date requirements and preferred documentation. Matter reference, reviewing contact and date needed. What the Search Record Should Identify The supplied subject name and the agreed lists and sources. The search date and any recorded search settings or identifiers. Results or potential matches returned, with supporting evidence within the agreed scope. Any unresolved identity questions or limits requiring the client’s review. Clear Scope Before Work Begins OFAC name screening supports a wider compliance review. Similar names may produce potential matches; further identifying details may be needed to assess them. A list-search result does not resolve ownership-based restrictions or other sanctions questions and is not transaction approval. The client’s legal or compliance team determines the next steps. From the Intelligence Center OFAC potential matches: what should your request and result record show? → Answers for Your Next Request What should I provide for an OFAC sanctions search? Provide the exact individual or entity name, known aliases, relevant countries and identifying information already available for the matter. Confirm the lists, names, search date and documentation required. Identify additional transaction parties separately. What does an OFAC potential match mean? Potential matches are assessed against the subject’s identifying details, with findings provided for your compliance or legal team’s review. Does an OFAC name search establish complete sanctions compliance? No. It documents the agreed list search for the supplied names at a point in time. Ownership, geographic restrictions and other sanctions issues may require separate review. A no-match result is not approval of a transaction. Official sources OFAC — Sanctions List Search tool ↗ OFAC FAQ 5 — Assessing potential name matches ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority; confirm current requirements for the actual request. Related Services SERVICE UCC, Lien & Litigation Research Bring related transaction research and supporting copies together. Explore the service → SERVICE Real Estate Title & Municipal Lien Research Get the property records and local responses your closing file needs. Explore the service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Registered Agent Services", "url": "./registered-agent-services.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Coordinate your registered agent appointment or change with the correct entity, state and authorized client contact.", "text": "Registered Agent Services | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Registered Agent Services Coordinate your registered agent appointment or change with the correct entity, state and authorized client contact. A Clear Starting Point for Your Agent Request Coordinate your registered agent appointment or change with the correct entity, state and authorized client contact. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Appointment details The agreed entity, jurisdiction, appointment or change request and responsible agent arrangements. Available appointment evidence The consent, acceptance, filing response or other appointment evidence included in the confirmed coordination scope. Notice-routing arrangements The designated authorized client recipients and agreed notice-handling contact arrangements. Pending requirements Outstanding acceptance, filing or contact requirements identified before treating an appointment as complete. Annual reports, other entity filings and separately available follow-up services require their own confirmed instructions. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Appointment and Change Coordination AADS supports registered agent appointment and change requests as part of its corporate services. Identify every state and entity involved so the appointment, acceptance and any filing steps can be confirmed before proceeding. Prepare Your Request Legal entity name, entity type, formation state and file number if known. Each state requiring an appointment or change. Whether this is a new entity, a qualification or a replacement of an existing agent. Authorized end-client contact details, desired effective date and related filing instructions. Confirm How Notices Will Reach the Right People Before an appointment begins, confirm the responsible agent, accepted service scope, designated client recipients and notice-forwarding arrangements. Keep those contacts current. Do not rely on an appointment request as evidence that the change has been accepted or filed. Separate the Related Work Annual reports, entity amendments, certificates of good standing and other filings are separately defined requests. Receiving service of process for an appointed entity is also different from arranging delivery of legal papers to another party. Ask for the specific work required. Answers for Your Next Request What information is needed for a registered agent appointment or change? Provide the entity’s legal name, formation state, file number if known, each state requiring an appointment, and an authorized end-client contact. Identify whether the entity is new or changing agents. Appointment acceptance, consent and filing requirements are confirmed before proceeding. Is a registered agent appointment the same as arranging service of process? No. An agent appointment concerns the designated recipient for notices and service of process for an entity. Arranging delivery of legal papers to another party is a separate request with different instructions and availability. Are annual reports and ongoing monitoring included with an agent appointment? Do not assume they are included. Confirm the appointment, notice-handling arrangements, any annual-report filing work and any separately available follow-up services in the agreed scope. Related Services SERVICE Corporate Documents, Filings & Registered Agent Support Connect the appointment to the corporate filing and document work required. Explore the service → SERVICE Apostille, Authentication & Legalization Prepare official entity documents for their intended international use. Explore the service → Start Your Next Request Send the service, subject or property, jurisdictions and date needed. We’ll confirm scope, fees and anticipated timing before work begins. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research", "url": "./court-litigation-bankruptcy.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Get civil and criminal case findings, bankruptcy records, dockets and court documents, with the courts, names and search period clearly defined.", "text": "Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER Civil, Criminal & Bankruptcy Court Research Get civil and criminal case findings, bankruptcy records, dockets and court documents, with the courts, names and search period clearly defined. Records Your Reviewing Team Can Use Bring civil litigation research, criminal court searches, bankruptcy records and identified court documents together through one point of contact. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Case findings Located case references for the agreed parties, courts or indexes, categories and search period. Dockets and documents The specified dockets, complaints, judgments, petitions, orders or other available records included in your request. Source and copy details The court or index, search date and requested plain or certified copy format, with any agreed page limits. Items needing follow-up Restricted, unavailable or outstanding records and source limitations identified with the returned material. Additional courts, case types, documents, certifications and copy charges are separately confirmed. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Define the Court Research Request Confirm the scope Possible deliverables County and state civil litigation Named courts, party names, case categories and period Located case references, status information, dockets and requested copies Federal district civil litigation Named districts or separately agreed federal index scope Case references, dockets, complaints, judgments or orders Federal district criminal searches Named federal districts or agreed federal index, subjects and search period Available case references, dockets, charges, dispositions and requested copies State criminal court searches Named state courts or separately confirmed statewide index, case categories and period Available criminal case findings and requested records, with source coverage identified County criminal court searches Named county-level court sources, subjects, case categories and period Available local case references, dockets, dispositions and requested copies Bankruptcy research Subjects, districts or index scope, period and case details Case references and requested petitions, schedules, claims or orders Court document retrieval Court, case number, docket entry and certification Specified plain or certified records, where available The Source Path Matters PACER distinguishes a national federal case index from the records maintained by each federal court. State and county research needs its own court sources. A case listing can identify where to look; the docket and underlying documents provide different information. Court names and responsibilities vary by state. Specify the actual sources requested and whether the search includes pending, closed or both categories of cases. Any additional case types are separately confirmed. Criminal Searches: Federal District, State and County For criminal searches, confirm felony, misdemeanor and criminal traffic coverage, as applicable, the search period, and whether pending cases and dispositions are required. Statewide indexes and county court searches have different coverage. Sealed, expunged, juvenile or otherwise restricted records may be unavailable. The returned work identifies the sources searched and any access limits. Prepare Your Request Exact names, entity type and additional names to search separately. Requested states, counties, courts or federal districts and search period. Known case numbers and any relevant docket-entry references. Case categories, pending/closed requirements and the records needed. For criminal searches of individuals, provide the full legal name and date of birth for each individual, plus aliases if known. Date of birth is not required for entities. Provide only information authorized for this request. Plain or certified copies, page limits, matter reference and deadline. Example Requests “Please quote civil litigation research for [exact names] in [named state/county courts] and [federal district], covering [period]. Include the located case references and current docket information, with copies of [specified documents] up to [page limit]. Separately confirm bankruptcy scope and any inaccessible records.” “Please quote criminal court searches for [full legal name, date of birth and known aliases for each individual] in [named federal districts], [named state courts or statewide index] and [named county court sources], covering [period]. Confirm felony, misdemeanor and criminal traffic coverage, pending-case and disposition requirements, and copies of [specified records].” Review and Delivery The agreed result identifies the subjects, courts or indexes, search criteria, period and located records. Returned documents are checked against the request, with unavailable items or source limits identified. Your legal team determines the appropriate scope and legal significance. Answers for Your Request Does a federal court search include state or county cases? No. Federal and state or county courts maintain different records. Identify the federal districts and the state or county court sources required. A single court result should not be treated as a search of every court. What should I provide for civil, criminal or bankruptcy court research? Provide the exact party names and known aliases, identify each subject as an individual or entity, and specify the jurisdictions or courts, case categories, search period and date needed. For criminal searches of individuals, include the full legal name, date of birth and any known aliases for each individual, and state whether felony, misdemeanor and criminal traffic cases are required. Include case numbers where known and specify the dockets, documents, certifications and copy limits required. Is a docket the same as the filed complaint or judgment? No. A docket records entries and activity in a case. Request the underlying complaint, judgment, petition or other document separately where needed, identifying the case and entry when available. What does a court search with no located cases mean? A no-record result means no responsive case was located within the identified names, sources, criteria and period. Your report identifies the coverage and relevant access limits. Official Sources PACER — finding federal court cases ↗ U.S. Courts — bankruptcy terminology ↗ Georgia Courts — trial-court types ↗ North Carolina Judicial Branch — obtaining court records ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Examples describe the named authority. Confirm current procedures, access, coverage and fees for the actual request. Criminal-Search References PACER — available records and access limits ↗ North Carolina Judicial Branch — county and statewide criminal-record search options ↗ Criminal-search references checked 11 November 2026. The North Carolina example describes that state; confirm the actual court sources, coverage and access for each request. Related Services and Guides SERVICE UCC, Lien & Litigation Research Connect court findings with the requested UCC and lien records. Explore → GLOSSARY Court Docket Understand the difference between a docket and the underlying documents. Explore → The Right Support for Your Next Request Get the research, filings and supporting documents your matter needs, with experienced people, human review and one point of contact. Discuss Your Request Place an Order →"}, {"title": "Abstract of Title", "url": "./glossary.html#term-abstract-of-title", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A summary of specified recorded instruments affecting a property or documented vessel. The period, records and supporting copies depend on the agreed abstract scope.", "text": "Abstract of Title A summary of specified recorded instruments affecting a property or documented vessel. The period, records and supporting copies depend on the agreed abstract scope."}, {"title": "Annual Report", "url": "./glossary.html#term-annual-report", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A periodic entity filing required by the applicable jurisdiction. Filing schedules, required information and the effect on entity status vary; confirm the actual obligation.", "text": "Annual Report A periodic entity filing required by the applicable jurisdiction. Filing schedules, required information and the effect on entity status vary; confirm the actual obligation."}, {"title": "Apostille", "url": "./glossary.html#term-apostille", "type": "term", "topics": ["international"], "summary": "A certificate authenticating the origin of an eligible public document for use under the Hague Apostille Convention. The issuing authority and destination determine the route; it does not validate the document’s contents.", "text": "Apostille A certificate authenticating the origin of an eligible public document for use under the Hague Apostille Convention. The issuing authority and destination determine the route; it does not validate the document’s contents."}, {"title": "Articles of Amendment", "url": "./glossary.html#term-articles-of-amendment", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A filed document changing specified information in an entity’s formation or registration record. The required approvals, form and permitted changes depend on the jurisdiction and entity type.", "text": "Articles of Amendment A filed document changing specified information in an entity’s formation or registration record. The required approvals, form and permitted changes depend on the jurisdiction and entity type."}, {"title": "Articles of Incorporation", "url": "./glossary.html#term-articles-of-incorporation", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The formation instrument filed to create a corporation under the applicable jurisdiction’s law. A copy of the articles is different from a current certificate of good standing.", "text": "Articles of Incorporation The formation instrument filed to create a corporation under the applicable jurisdiction’s law. A copy of the articles is different from a current certificate of good standing."}, {"title": "Articles of Organization", "url": "./glossary.html#term-articles-of-organization", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The formation instrument commonly used to create a limited liability company. Its name, required contents and filing process depend on the jurisdiction.", "text": "Articles of Organization The formation instrument commonly used to create a limited liability company. Its name, required contents and filing process depend on the jurisdiction."}, {"title": "Assignment Verification Report", "url": "./glossary.html#term-assignment-verification-report", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A scoped report identifying located mortgage assignments, related recorded instruments and apparent gaps for professional review.", "text": "Assignment Verification Report A scoped report identifying located mortgage assignments, related recorded instruments and apparent gaps for professional review."}, {"title": "Authentication", "url": "./glossary.html#term-authentication", "type": "term", "topics": ["international"], "summary": "Certification of the relevant official signature or seal through the required document-use process. Review of the document’s contents is separate.", "text": "Authentication Certification of the relevant official signature or seal through the required document-use process. Review of the document’s contents is separate."}, {"title": "Bankruptcy Court", "url": "./glossary.html#term-bankruptcy-court", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "The federal court handling a bankruptcy case and related proceedings. Identify the district and case number when requesting a docket or documents.", "text": "Bankruptcy Court The federal court handling a bankruptcy case and related proceedings. Identify the district and case number when requesting a docket or documents."}, {"title": "Bankruptcy Petition", "url": "./glossary.html#term-bankruptcy-petition", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "The filing that begins a bankruptcy case. A petition, case docket, schedules and later orders are different records and should be requested by name.", "text": "Bankruptcy Petition The filing that begins a bankruptcy case. A petition, case docket, schedules and later orders are different records and should be requested by name."}, {"title": "Bankruptcy Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-bankruptcy-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research for bankruptcy cases involving a named subject within the agreed sources and scope. A case listing does not by itself describe every filing, outcome or current obligation.", "text": "Bankruptcy Search Research for bankruptcy cases involving a named subject within the agreed sources and scope. A case listing does not by itself describe every filing, outcome or current obligation."}, {"title": "Beneficial Ownership", "url": "./glossary.html#term-beneficial-ownership", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Ownership or control information about the people behind an entity. Regulatory definitions and reporting obligations are context-specific; consult current FinCEN guidance for BOI reporting.", "text": "Beneficial Ownership Ownership or control information about the people behind an entity. Regulatory definitions and reporting obligations are context-specific; consult current FinCEN guidance for BOI reporting."}, {"title": "Borrower Diligence", "url": "./glossary.html#term-borrower-diligence", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "A defined set of factual record requests about a borrower and, where instructed, related collateral or parties. It may combine entity, lien, court and property research.", "text": "Borrower Diligence A defined set of factual record requests about a borrower and, where instructed, related collateral or parties. It may combine entity, lien, court and property research."}, {"title": "Business Entity Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-business-entity-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Research of an entity’s record at the responsible business-filing office, such as its name, file number, recorded status and filing history. An online result is not automatically an official status certificate.", "text": "Business Entity Search Research of an entity’s record at the responsible business-filing office, such as its name, file number, recorded status and filing history. An online result is not automatically an official status certificate."}, {"title": "Certificate of Good Standing", "url": "./glossary.html#term-certificate-of-good-standing", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "An official entity-status certificate whose meaning is defined by the issuing jurisdiction. Equivalent documents may be called certificates of existence or status. It does not certify compliance with every legal or tax obligation.", "text": "Certificate of Good Standing An official entity-status certificate whose meaning is defined by the issuing jurisdiction. Equivalent documents may be called certificates of existence or status. It does not certify compliance with every legal or tax obligation."}, {"title": "Certificate of Occupancy", "url": "./glossary.html#term-certificate-of-occupancy", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "An authority-issued record concerning occupancy approval for a specified building or space. Request the existing document and its identified scope; a copy does not independently approve a new use.", "text": "Certificate of Occupancy An authority-issued record concerning occupancy approval for a specified building or space. Request the existing document and its identified scope; a copy does not independently approve a new use."}, {"title": "Certified Copy", "url": "./glossary.html#term-certified-copy", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A reproduction of a specified filed or recorded document carrying the custodian’s certification. It is distinct from a plain copy and from a certificate addressing an entity’s status.", "text": "Certified Copy A reproduction of a specified filed or recorded document carrying the custodian’s certification. It is distinct from a plain copy and from a certificate addressing an entity’s status."}, {"title": "Civil Litigation Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-civil-litigation-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research of civil case records for the supplied party names, courts, period and case categories. Identify whether the deliverable requires an index result, docket, documents or certified copies.", "text": "Civil Litigation Search Research of civil case records for the supplied party names, courts, period and case categories. Identify whether the deliverable requires an index result, docket, documents or certified copies."}, {"title": "Code Violation", "url": "./glossary.html#term-code-violation", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A local authority’s record of an alleged or identified code issue. The record’s status, dates, balances and any resolution should be reported as supplied by the responsible office.", "text": "Code Violation A local authority’s record of an alleged or identified code issue. The record’s status, dates, balances and any resolution should be reported as supplied by the responsible office."}, {"title": "Collateral", "url": "./glossary.html#term-collateral", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Property or rights identified as security for an obligation. A records search can locate relevant filings; the client’s advisers determine attachment, perfection, priority and legal effect.", "text": "Collateral Property or rights identified as security for an obligation. A records search can locate relevant filings; the client’s advisers determine attachment, perfection, priority and legal effect."}, {"title": "Compliance Monitoring", "url": "./glossary.html#term-compliance-monitoring", "type": "term", "topics": ["monitoring"], "summary": "Periodic review of specified records or events, with agreed sources, frequency, recipients and follow-up responsibilities.", "text": "Compliance Monitoring Periodic review of specified records or events, with agreed sources, frequency, recipients and follow-up responsibilities."}, {"title": "Corporate Amendment", "url": "./glossary.html#term-corporate-amendment", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A filing changing specified information in an entity record. Identify the entity, jurisdiction, requested change and authorized instructions.", "text": "Corporate Amendment A filing changing specified information in an entity record. Identify the entity, jurisdiction, requested change and authorized instructions."}, {"title": "Corporate Compliance", "url": "./glossary.html#term-corporate-compliance", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The filing, status and operational obligations affecting an entity. AADS coordinates the specific filing and document tasks included in the agreed request.", "text": "Corporate Compliance The filing, status and operational obligations affecting an entity. AADS coordinates the specific filing and document tasks included in the agreed request."}, {"title": "Corporate Filing", "url": "./glossary.html#term-corporate-filing", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A submission to the responsible office concerning formation, qualification, maintenance, amendment, merger, conversion, reinstatement, withdrawal or dissolution of an entity. Acceptance and ongoing legal compliance are different questions.", "text": "Corporate Filing A submission to the responsible office concerning formation, qualification, maintenance, amendment, merger, conversion, reinstatement, withdrawal or dissolution of an entity. Acceptance and ongoing legal compliance are different questions."}, {"title": "Corporate Monitoring", "url": "./glossary.html#term-corporate-monitoring", "type": "term", "topics": ["monitoring"], "summary": "Recurring review of specified entity records or filing events. The office, frequency and report recipients define the coverage; monitoring availability must be separately confirmed.", "text": "Corporate Monitoring Recurring review of specified entity records or filing events. The office, frequency and report recipients define the coverage; monitoring availability must be separately confirmed."}, {"title": "Corporate Status", "url": "./glossary.html#term-corporate-status", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The condition shown for an entity by a particular jurisdiction at a given time. Status labels and their meanings vary, and a search result may differ from an official certificate.", "text": "Corporate Status The condition shown for an entity by a particular jurisdiction at a given time. Status labels and their meanings vary, and a search result may differ from an official certificate."}, {"title": "County Civil Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-county-civil-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research of the identified county-level civil court sources. A county can have more than one court or index, so the court types and search period should be stated.", "text": "County Civil Search Research of the identified county-level civil court sources. A county can have more than one court or index, so the court types and search period should be stated."}, {"title": "County Court at Law", "url": "./glossary.html#term-county-court-at-law", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "A court designation used in some jurisdictions, including Texas. Its records and jurisdiction should be identified separately from other county or district courts when defining a search.", "text": "County Court at Law A court designation used in some jurisdictions, including Texas. Its records and jurisdiction should be identified separately from other county or district courts when defining a search."}, {"title": "County Criminal Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-county-criminal-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research of specified county criminal records for the identified individual, period and permitted purpose, with source coverage and access restrictions stated.", "text": "County Criminal Search Research of specified county criminal records for the identified individual, period and permitted purpose, with source coverage and access restrictions stated."}, {"title": "County Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-county-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Research directed to named county sources such as land records, a court clerk or a tax office. The county name alone does not identify all offices or records included.", "text": "County Search Research directed to named county sources such as land records, a court clerk or a tax office. The county name alone does not identify all offices or records included."}, {"title": "Court Document Retrieval", "url": "./glossary.html#term-court-document-retrieval", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Obtaining identified records from a court case, such as a docket, complaint, judgment, petition or order. State whether plain or certified copies and any page limits are required.", "text": "Court Document Retrieval Obtaining identified records from a court case, such as a docket, complaint, judgment, petition or order. State whether plain or certified copies and any page limits are required."}, {"title": "Court Docket", "url": "./glossary.html#term-court-docket", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "The court’s list of entries and activity for a case. A docket entry and the underlying filed document are different deliverables.", "text": "Court Docket The court’s list of entries and activity for a case. A docket entry and the underlying filed document are different deliverables."}, {"title": "CTA / BOI", "url": "./glossary.html#term-cta-boi", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The Corporate Transparency Act and beneficial ownership information reporting framework. FinCEN’s current guidance exempts U.S. companies; certain foreign-country entities registered in the U.S. remain subject to reporting. Determine applicability from current official guidance.", "text": "CTA / BOI The Corporate Transparency Act and beneficial ownership information reporting framework. FinCEN’s current guidance exempts U.S. companies; certain foreign-country entities registered in the U.S. remain subject to reporting. Determine applicability from current official guidance."}, {"title": "Current Owner Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-current-owner-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Property research within an agreed current-owner scope. Identify the property, owner period, record categories, tax components and copies included rather than assuming a fixed nationwide package.", "text": "Current Owner Search Property research within an agreed current-owner scope. Identify the property, owner period, record categories, tax components and copies included rather than assuming a fixed nationwide package."}, {"title": "DBA", "url": "./glossary.html#term-dba", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Doing Business As: a trade or assumed name used by a person or entity. Registration, publication and renewal requirements vary by jurisdiction and business circumstances.", "text": "DBA Doing Business As: a trade or assumed name used by a person or entity. Registration, publication and renewal requirements vary by jurisdiction and business circumstances."}, {"title": "Debtor Name", "url": "./glossary.html#term-debtor-name", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The subject name used for a UCC filing or search. Exact wording, entity type and additional names matter; the client’s advisers determine the legally appropriate filing name and scope.", "text": "Debtor Name The subject name used for a UCC filing or search. Exact wording, entity type and additional names matter; the client’s advisers determine the legally appropriate filing name and scope."}, {"title": "Direct-Source Verification", "url": "./glossary.html#term-direct-source-verification", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Checking requested information against the responsible official record or custodian, with the source and any relevant limitations identified.", "text": "Direct-Source Verification Checking requested information against the responsible official record or custodian, with the source and any relevant limitations identified."}, {"title": "District Court Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-district-court-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research of a named district court’s records. Specify the state or federal system and location because the same court label can describe different jurisdictions.", "text": "District Court Search Research of a named district court’s records. Specify the state or federal system and location because the same court label can describe different jurisdictions."}, {"title": "Document Retrieval", "url": "./glossary.html#term-document-retrieval", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Obtaining a requested document from its custodian or filing source. Identify the record reference, certification, format and date requirements.", "text": "Document Retrieval Obtaining a requested document from its custodian or filing source. Identify the record reference, certification, format and date requirements."}, {"title": "Due Diligence", "url": "./glossary.html#term-due-diligence", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "A review process supporting a defined business or legal decision. Public-record research is one possible component, and the client’s reviewing professionals determine the full diligence scope.", "text": "Due Diligence A review process supporting a defined business or legal decision. Public-record research is one possible component, and the client’s reviewing professionals determine the full diligence scope."}, {"title": "Entity Status Research", "url": "./glossary.html#term-entity-status-research", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Checking the status and relevant filings shown for an entity in specified jurisdictions. Identify the source and date and request an official certificate separately when required.", "text": "Entity Status Research Checking the status and relevant filings shown for an entity in specified jurisdictions. Identify the source and date and request an official certificate separately when required."}, {"title": "Environmental / EPA Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-environmental-epa-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A lien associated with specified environmental response or liability provisions. Environmental lien research and environmental site assessments address different questions.", "text": "Environmental / EPA Lien A lien associated with specified environmental response or liability provisions. Environmental lien research and environmental site assessments address different questions."}, {"title": "ERISA Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-erisa-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A lien arising under applicable employee-benefit-plan provisions in specified circumstances. Some pension funding failures can give rise to a statutory lien; a general ERISA compliance review is a different task.", "text": "ERISA Lien A lien arising under applicable employee-benefit-plan provisions in specified circumstances. Some pension funding failures can give rise to a statutory lien; a general ERISA compliance review is a different task."}, {"title": "Estoppel Certificate", "url": "./glossary.html#term-estoppel-certificate", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A statement from an association or other relevant party concerning specified charges, balances or obligations. Identify the issuer, property, requested contents and effective date; do not assume every form covers the same matters.", "text": "Estoppel Certificate A statement from an association or other relevant party concerning specified charges, balances or obligations. Identify the issuer, property, requested contents and effective date; do not assume every form covers the same matters."}, {"title": "Federal District Court Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-federal-district-court-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research of specified federal district court records by subject or case reference. Identify districts, case categories, period and requested dockets or documents.", "text": "Federal District Court Search Research of specified federal district court records by subject or case reference. Identify districts, case categories, period and requested dockets or documents."}, {"title": "Federal Tax Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-federal-tax-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The federal government’s legal claim securing unpaid tax debt. Research identifies any related notices located within the agreed sources and scope.", "text": "Federal Tax Lien The federal government’s legal claim securing unpaid tax debt. Research identifies any related notices located within the agreed sources and scope."}, {"title": "Fictitious Name", "url": "./glossary.html#term-fictitious-name", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A business name different from the person’s or entity’s legal name, subject to the applicable assumed-name rules. A name filing is not the same as forming a separate entity or establishing trademark rights.", "text": "Fictitious Name A business name different from the person’s or entity’s legal name, subject to the applicable assumed-name rules. A name filing is not the same as forming a separate entity or establishing trademark rights."}, {"title": "Filing Date", "url": "./glossary.html#term-filing-date", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "The date a filing office records for receipt, filing or recording of an instrument. Report the office’s labels and timestamps accurately; submission, acceptance and indexing may occur at different times.", "text": "Filing Date The date a filing office records for receipt, filing or recording of an instrument. Report the office’s labels and timestamps accurately; submission, acceptance and indexing may occur at different times."}, {"title": "Fixture Filing", "url": "./glossary.html#term-fixture-filing", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A financing-statement filing concerning goods that are or become fixtures, with special filing requirements under applicable law. State UCC and real-property record sources must be separately identified where requested.", "text": "Fixture Filing A financing-statement filing concerning goods that are or become fixtures, with special filing requirements under applicable law. State UCC and real-property record sources must be separately identified where requested."}, {"title": "Foreign Qualification", "url": "./glossary.html#term-foreign-qualification", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "Registration of an existing entity to transact business in another jurisdiction. “Foreign” can refer to an entity formed in another U.S. state; it does not necessarily mean another country.", "text": "Foreign Qualification Registration of an existing entity to transact business in another jurisdiction. “Foreign” can refer to an entity formed in another U.S. state; it does not necessarily mean another country."}, {"title": "Foreclosure Information", "url": "./glossary.html#term-foreclosure-information", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Case and recorded-document information concerning an identified foreclosure matter, supplied for the reviewing professional’s assessment.", "text": "Foreclosure Information Case and recorded-document information concerning an identified foreclosure matter, supplied for the reviewing professional’s assessment."}, {"title": "Full Title Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-full-title-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Title research covering the agreed ownership chain or period and specified record categories. Confirm the search depth when ordering.", "text": "Full Title Search Title research covering the agreed ownership chain or period and specified record categories. Confirm the search depth when ordering."}, {"title": "Good Standing", "url": "./glossary.html#term-good-standing", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "An entity status or certification concept defined by the particular jurisdiction. Confirm the issuing office’s meaning and the receiving party’s exact document requirement.", "text": "Good Standing An entity status or certification concept defined by the particular jurisdiction. Confirm the issuing office’s meaning and the receiving party’s exact document requirement."}, {"title": "Human-Reviewed Research", "url": "./glossary.html#term-human-reviewed-research", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "Research checked by people against the agreed subjects, sources, records and deliverables, with outstanding items and source limitations identified.", "text": "Human-Reviewed Research Research checked by people against the agreed subjects, sources, records and deliverables, with outstanding items and source limitations identified."}, {"title": "Judgment", "url": "./glossary.html#term-judgment", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "A court’s decision or order resolving specified matters. The judgment and relevant later records provide the basis for professional review.", "text": "Judgment A court’s decision or order resolving specified matters. The judgment and relevant later records provide the basis for professional review."}, {"title": "Judgment Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-judgment-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A lien associated with a judgment under applicable law. Whether it exists, attaches or has priority requires jurisdiction-specific legal analysis; research identifies the requested recorded or court evidence.", "text": "Judgment Lien A lien associated with a judgment under applicable law. Whether it exists, attaches or has priority requires jurisdiction-specific legal analysis; research identifies the requested recorded or court evidence."}, {"title": "Judgment Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-judgment-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research for specified judgment records in named court or recording sources. A judgment search and a judgment-lien search can involve different records and should be scoped separately.", "text": "Judgment Search Research for specified judgment records in named court or recording sources. A judgment search and a judgment-lien search can involve different records and should be scoped separately."}, {"title": "Jurisdiction", "url": "./glossary.html#term-jurisdiction", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "The authority, court or geographic scope relevant to a record or request. Identify the specific state, county, district, municipality or agency rather than relying only on a broad regional label.", "text": "Jurisdiction The authority, court or geographic scope relevant to a record or request. Identify the specific state, county, district, municipality or agency rather than relying only on a broad regional label."}, {"title": "Legal and Vesting Deed Report", "url": "./glossary.html#term-legal-and-vesting-deed-report", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A focused report identifying the recorded deed’s owner names, vesting language, legal description and recording reference. Broader title and lien research is scoped separately.", "text": "Legal and Vesting Deed Report A focused report identifying the recorded deed’s owner names, vesting language, legal description and recording reference. Broader title and lien research is scoped separately."}, {"title": "Legalization", "url": "./glossary.html#term-legalization", "type": "term", "topics": ["international"], "summary": "The document-authentication route required for the receiving country or authority, often involving further consular or embassy steps. Confirm origin, destination and recipient instructions before choosing the route.", "text": "Legalization The document-authentication route required for the receiving country or authority, often involving further consular or embassy steps. Confirm origin, destination and recipient instructions before choosing the route."}, {"title": "Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A legal claim or charge against property or rights associated with an obligation. The type of lien, governing law and relevant records determine the research path and legal effect.", "text": "Lien A legal claim or charge against property or rights associated with an obligation. The type of lien, governing law and relevant records determine the research path and legal effect."}, {"title": "Lien Monitoring", "url": "./glossary.html#term-lien-monitoring", "type": "term", "topics": ["monitoring"], "summary": "Periodic review of defined lien-record sources for named subjects or properties, with agreed frequency, coverage and reporting.", "text": "Lien Monitoring Periodic review of defined lien-record sources for named subjects or properties, with agreed frequency, coverage and reporting."}, {"title": "Lien Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-lien-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Research for the categories of lien records specified in a request. List the lien types, subjects, jurisdictions, offices and period; one index should not be assumed to cover all liens.", "text": "Lien Search Research for the categories of lien records specified in a request. List the lien types, subjects, jurisdictions, offices and period; one index should not be assumed to cover all liens."}, {"title": "LienPulse", "url": "./glossary.html#term-lienpulse", "type": "term", "topics": ["monitoring"], "summary": "AADS’s developing concept for recurring public-record visibility. It is not a launched monitoring subscription or a promise of real-time coverage. Scope and availability require separate confirmation.", "text": "LienPulse AADS’s developing concept for recurring public-record visibility. It is not a launched monitoring subscription or a promise of real-time coverage. Scope and availability require separate confirmation."}, {"title": "Litigation Research", "url": "./glossary.html#term-litigation-research", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Factual research of case indexes, dockets and requested documents. Identify the courts, names, period and case categories; the client’s legal team assesses significance and legal exposure.", "text": "Litigation Research Factual research of case indexes, dockets and requested documents. Identify the courts, names, period and case categories; the client’s legal team assesses significance and legal exposure."}, {"title": "Mechanics Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-mechanics-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A construction-related lien associated with labor, services or materials under applicable law. Its requirements and effect vary; request the relevant property records and supporting instruments.", "text": "Mechanics Lien A construction-related lien associated with labor, services or materials under applicable law. Its requirements and effect vary; request the relevant property records and supporting instruments."}, {"title": "Municipal Lien Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-municipal-lien-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A scoped inquiry to responsible local departments or providers for specified debts, code issues, permits, utilities or assessments. Recorded land records and local departmental responses are separate research paths.", "text": "Municipal Lien Search A scoped inquiry to responsible local departments or providers for specified debts, code issues, permits, utilities or assessments. Recorded land records and local departmental responses are separate research paths."}, {"title": "Name Availability", "url": "./glossary.html#term-name-availability", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "An office’s assessment or search result concerning use of a proposed business name. Availability, reservation and formation acceptance are different steps, and name availability is not trademark clearance.", "text": "Name Availability An office’s assessment or search result concerning use of a proposed business name. Availability, reservation and formation acceptance are different steps, and name availability is not trademark clearance."}, {"title": "OFAC Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ofac-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "A search of agreed sanctions lists maintained by the Office of Foreign Assets Control for supplied names and identifiers. Potential matches require assessment; a no-match result is not complete sanctions clearance.", "text": "OFAC Search A search of agreed sanctions lists maintained by the Office of Foreign Assets Control for supplied names and identifiers. Potential matches require assessment; a no-match result is not complete sanctions clearance."}, {"title": "Open Mortgage Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-open-mortgage-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Research identifying located mortgage or deed-of-trust records and requested related assignments, modifications or releases within the agreed scope. Record findings do not independently establish a current loan payoff balance.", "text": "Open Mortgage Search Research identifying located mortgage or deed-of-trust records and requested related assignments, modifications or releases within the agreed scope. Record findings do not independently establish a current loan payoff balance."}, {"title": "Open Permit", "url": "./glossary.html#term-open-permit", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A permit shown as open or unresolved in the responsible authority’s records. Confirm the recorded status and any available inspections or closure information; an open status alone is not a complete code assessment.", "text": "Open Permit A permit shown as open or unresolved in the responsible authority’s records. Confirm the recorded status and any available inspections or closure information; an open status alone is not a complete code assessment."}, {"title": "Owner Verification", "url": "./glossary.html#term-owner-verification", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Research of the recorded owner names and requested property identification or deed details. The record source, legal description and date should be stated.", "text": "Owner Verification Research of the recorded owner names and requested property identification or deed details. The record source, legal description and date should be stated."}, {"title": "Ownership & Encumbrance Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ownership-encumbrance-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "An O&E report combining ownership research with the encumbrance categories defined in the request. Confirm the owner period, record sources, taxes and supporting copies included.", "text": "Ownership & Encumbrance Search An O&E report combining ownership research with the encumbrance categories defined in the request. Confirm the owner period, record sources, taxes and supporting copies included."}, {"title": "Owner/Mortgage Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-owner-mortgage-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A combined request for recorded ownership and specified mortgage-related records. Confirm the period, assignments, modifications, releases and copies required.", "text": "Owner/Mortgage Search A combined request for recorded ownership and specified mortgage-related records. Confirm the period, assignments, modifications, releases and copies required."}, {"title": "Patriot Act Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-patriot-act-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["ofac"], "summary": "An industry label often used for requested sanctions-list screening or related diligence. State the actual lists and work included; the label does not certify compliance with every Patriot Act obligation.", "text": "Patriot Act Search An industry label often used for requested sanctions-list screening or related diligence. State the actual lists and work included; the label does not certify compliance with every Patriot Act obligation."}, {"title": "Pending Suit Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-pending-suit-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Research for cases shown as pending or unresolved in the agreed court sources and period. Case status should be reported as the source shows it and distinguished from legal conclusions.", "text": "Pending Suit Search Research for cases shown as pending or unresolved in the agreed court sources and period. Case status should be reported as the source shows it and distinguished from legal conclusions."}, {"title": "Permit Research", "url": "./glossary.html#term-permit-research", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Research of requested building, trade or other permit records at the responsible authority. Identify the property, permit types, period, status information and copies needed.", "text": "Permit Research Research of requested building, trade or other permit records at the responsible authority. Identify the property, permit types, period, status information and copies needed."}, {"title": "Plain Copy", "url": "./glossary.html#term-plain-copy", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A reproduction of a record without the custodian’s certification. Confirm whether the recipient requires certification, an original, an electronic image or another format.", "text": "Plain Copy A reproduction of a record without the custodian’s certification. Confirm whether the recipient requires certification, an original, an electronic image or another format."}, {"title": "Plat Map Retrieval", "url": "./glossary.html#term-plat-map-retrieval", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Obtaining a specified recorded plat or related map from the responsible source. A map copy is not a new survey or a determination of boundaries on the ground.", "text": "Plat Map Retrieval Obtaining a specified recorded plat or related map from the responsible source. A map copy is not a new survey or a determination of boundaries on the ground."}, {"title": "Portfolio Monitoring", "url": "./glossary.html#term-portfolio-monitoring", "type": "term", "topics": ["monitoring"], "summary": "Recurring review of defined records for a group of entities, borrowers or properties. Subject lists, sources, frequency and escalation rules define the arrangement; AADS availability is not established by this glossary entry.", "text": "Portfolio Monitoring Recurring review of defined records for a group of entities, borrowers or properties. Subject lists, sources, frequency and escalation rules define the arrangement; AADS availability is not established by this glossary entry."}, {"title": "Proof of Claim", "url": "./glossary.html#term-proof-of-claim", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "A bankruptcy filing through which a creditor asserts a claim. Court records concerning allowance and payment are reviewed separately.", "text": "Proof of Claim A bankruptcy filing through which a creditor asserts a claim. Court records concerning allowance and payment are reviewed separately."}, {"title": "Public Record", "url": "./glossary.html#term-public-record", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "A record maintained by a public authority or official custodian. Public access can be limited by law, confidentiality, retention practices or source availability; not every record is online.", "text": "Public Record A record maintained by a public authority or official custodian. Public access can be limited by law, confidentiality, retention practices or source availability; not every record is online."}, {"title": "Public-Record Due Diligence", "url": "./glossary.html#term-public-record-due-diligence", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "The factual record-research component of an agreed diligence plan. It can combine corporate, UCC/lien, court, property and municipal sources without replacing professional legal, financial or technical review.", "text": "Public-Record Due Diligence The factual record-research component of an agreed diligence plan. It can combine corporate, UCC/lien, court, property and municipal sources without replacing professional legal, financial or technical review."}, {"title": "Registered Agent", "url": "./glossary.html#term-registered-agent", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The person or organization designated for an entity to receive specified legal notices and official communications in a jurisdiction. Appointment, acceptance and contact requirements vary.", "text": "Registered Agent The person or organization designated for an entity to receive specified legal notices and official communications in a jurisdiction. Appointment, acceptance and contact requirements vary."}, {"title": "Registered Agent Address", "url": "./glossary.html#term-registered-agent-address", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "The address designated for the agent’s statutory role. It should not automatically be treated as the entity’s principal office, general mailing address or a location for all business activities.", "text": "Registered Agent Address The address designated for the agent’s statutory role. It should not automatically be treated as the entity’s principal office, general mailing address or a location for all business activities."}, {"title": "Reinstatement", "url": "./glossary.html#term-reinstatement", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A process for restoring an entity’s status following specified administrative or filing issues, where allowed. Required filings, fees and resulting status depend on the jurisdiction and circumstances.", "text": "Reinstatement A process for restoring an entity’s status following specified administrative or filing issues, where allowed. Required filings, fees and resulting status depend on the jurisdiction and circumstances."}, {"title": "Rush Request", "url": "./glossary.html#term-rush-request", "type": "term", "topics": ["general"], "summary": "A request identifying an expedited deadline. Availability and any additional fees must be confirmed against the actual source, processing route and scope.", "text": "Rush Request A request identifying an expedited deadline. Availability and any additional fees must be confirmed against the actual source, processing route and scope."}, {"title": "Search-to-Reflect", "url": "./glossary.html#term-search-to-reflect", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A post-filing search checking whether an identified UCC filing appears in the filing office’s searchable records through the reported date, for review alongside the filing acknowledgment.", "text": "Search-to-Reflect A post-filing search checking whether an identified UCC filing appears in the filing office’s searchable records through the reported date, for review alongside the filing acknowledgment."}, {"title": "Secretary of State", "url": "./glossary.html#term-secretary-of-state", "type": "term", "topics": ["corporate"], "summary": "A state office that commonly maintains entity records and, in many states, central UCC records. Responsibilities vary: Washington UCC requests go to its Department of Licensing, for example.", "text": "Secretary of State A state office that commonly maintains entity records and, in many states, central UCC records. Responsibilities vary: Washington UCC requests go to its Department of Licensing, for example."}, {"title": "Secured Party", "url": "./glossary.html#term-secured-party", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A person or organization holding a security interest or another role defined by applicable secured-transactions law. A financing statement’s listed secured party is record information requiring context.", "text": "Secured Party A person or organization holding a security interest or another role defined by applicable secured-transactions law. A financing statement’s listed secured party is record information requiring context."}, {"title": "Service of Process", "url": "./glossary.html#term-service-of-process", "type": "term", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Delivery of specified legal papers under the applicable procedural rules. Receiving papers as a registered agent and arranging delivery to another party are different services.", "text": "Service of Process Delivery of specified legal papers under the applicable procedural rules. Receiving papers as a registered agent and arranging delivery to another party are different services."}, {"title": "Special Assessment", "url": "./glossary.html#term-special-assessment", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A charge imposed for a specified public improvement, district or other authorized purpose. Identify the responsible authority, property, assessment period and balances requested.", "text": "Special Assessment A charge imposed for a specified public improvement, district or other authorized purpose. Identify the responsible authority, property, assessment period and balances requested."}, {"title": "State Tax Lien", "url": "./glossary.html#term-state-tax-lien", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A lien associated with unpaid state tax obligations under the applicable state’s law. Identify the responsible tax and recording sources and the notices or releases required.", "text": "State Tax Lien A lien associated with unpaid state tax obligations under the applicable state’s law. Identify the responsible tax and recording sources and the notices or releases required."}, {"title": "Tax Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-tax-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "For property research, an inquiry into specified tax accounts, years, balances or collection records. A property-tax search is different from a search for tax liens against a named person or entity.", "text": "Tax Search For property research, an inquiry into specified tax accounts, years, balances or collection records. A property-tax search is different from a search for tax liens against a named person or entity."}, {"title": "Title Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-title-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Research of the defined recorded ownership chain and other agreed property record categories. State the depth, sources and copies required; AADS supplies factual research rather than a title opinion or insurance.", "text": "Title Search Research of the defined recorded ownership chain and other agreed property record categories. State the depth, sources and copies required; AADS supplies factual research rather than a title opinion or insurance."}, {"title": "Two Owner Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-two-owner-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "Property research covering the specified current and prior owner periods. Confirm the owners, dates, record categories and copies included rather than relying on the label alone.", "text": "Two Owner Search Property research covering the specified current and prior owner periods. Confirm the owners, dates, record categories and copies included rather than relying on the label alone."}, {"title": "UCC", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The Uniform Commercial Code, as enacted in the applicable jurisdiction. Article 9 addresses secured transactions, including many financing-statement filing and search concepts.", "text": "UCC The Uniform Commercial Code, as enacted in the applicable jurisdiction. Article 9 addresses secured transactions, including many financing-statement filing and search concepts."}, {"title": "UCC-1", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-1", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The commonly used initial financing-statement form. Filing a UCC-1 and creating or perfecting an enforceable security interest are different questions requiring the appropriate legal analysis.", "text": "UCC-1 The commonly used initial financing-statement form. Filing a UCC-1 and creating or perfecting an enforceable security interest are different questions requiring the appropriate legal analysis."}, {"title": "UCC-3", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-3", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "The commonly used form for specified changes to an existing financing statement, including amendment, assignment, continuation or termination. Identify the filing action and original record.", "text": "UCC-3 The commonly used form for specified changes to an existing financing statement, including amendment, assignment, continuation or termination. Identify the filing action and original record."}, {"title": "UCC Continuation", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-continuation", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A filing intended to continue a financing statement’s effectiveness under applicable rules. Authorization and timing matter; the client’s advisers determine the legally appropriate action.", "text": "UCC Continuation A filing intended to continue a financing statement’s effectiveness under applicable rules. Authorization and timing matter; the client’s advisers determine the legally appropriate action."}, {"title": "UCC Filing", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-filing", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Submission of a financing statement or related record to the responsible filing office. Acceptance, indexing and legal effect should be distinguished.", "text": "UCC Filing Submission of a financing statement or related record to the responsible filing office. Acceptance, indexing and legal effect should be distinguished."}, {"title": "UCC Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Research of specified UCC records under the supplied search criteria at the identified office. Confirm exact names, search logic or certification, through date, and copies required.", "text": "UCC Search Research of specified UCC records under the supplied search criteria at the identified office. Confirm exact names, search logic or certification, through date, and copies required.", "aliases": ["UCC11", "UCC-11", "information request"]}, {"title": "UCC Termination", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-termination", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A filed termination statement relating to an identified financing statement. Its authorization and legal effect require context; it does not by itself prove the underlying debt was paid.", "text": "UCC Termination A filed termination statement relating to an identified financing statement. Its authorization and legal effect require context; it does not by itself prove the underlying debt was paid."}, {"title": "Utility Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-utility-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "An inquiry to specified utility providers about requested account or property balances and related obligations. Municipal and independent providers may require separate requests and authorization.", "text": "Utility Search An inquiry to specified utility providers about requested account or property balances and related obligations. Municipal and independent providers may require separate requests and authorization."}, {"title": "Vessel Search", "url": "./glossary.html#term-vessel-search", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A scoped request for vessel ownership and encumbrance records, which may include a Coast Guard Abstract of Title for an eligible documented vessel. Identify the vessel and source; availability must be confirmed.", "text": "Vessel Search A scoped request for vessel ownership and encumbrance records, which may include a Coast Guard Abstract of Title for an eligible documented vessel. Identify the vessel and source; availability must be confirmed."}, {"title": "Vesting", "url": "./glossary.html#term-vesting", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "The manner in which ownership is described in a deed or related record. Report the recorded language accurately and refer questions about its legal effect to the reviewing professional.", "text": "Vesting The manner in which ownership is described in a deed or related record. Report the recorded language accurately and refer questions about its legal effect to the reviewing professional."}, {"title": "Zoning Verification", "url": "./glossary.html#term-zoning-verification", "type": "term", "topics": ["property"], "summary": "A request for zoning classification, use or other specified land-use information from the responsible authority. Identify intended use and questions; a general letter may not resolve every development or compliance issue.", "text": "Zoning Verification A request for zoning classification, use or other specified land-use information from the responsible authority. Identify intended use and questions; a general letter may not resolve every development or compliance issue."}, {"title": "Does a federal court search include state or county cases?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-29", "type": "faq", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "No. Federal and state or county courts maintain different records. Identify the federal districts and the state or county court sources required. A single court result should not be treated as a search of every court.", "text": "Does a federal court search include state or county cases? No. Federal and state or county courts maintain different records. Identify the federal districts and the state or county court sources required. A single court result should not be treated as a search of every court."}, {"title": "What should I provide for civil, criminal or bankruptcy court research?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-30", "type": "faq", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "Provide the exact party names and known aliases, identify each subject as an individual or entity, and specify the jurisdictions or courts, case categories, search period and date needed. For criminal searches of individuals, include the full legal name, date of birth and any known aliases for each individual, and state whether felony, misdemeanor and criminal traffic cases are required. Include case numbers where known and specify the dockets, documents, certifications and copy limits required.", "text": "What should I provide for civil, criminal or bankruptcy court research? Provide the exact party names and known aliases, identify each subject as an individual or entity, and specify the jurisdictions or courts, case categories, search period and date needed. For criminal searches of individuals, include the full legal name, date of birth and any known aliases for each individual, and state whether felony, misdemeanor and criminal traffic cases are required. Include case numbers where known and specify the dockets, documents, certifications and copy limits required."}, {"title": "Is a docket the same as the filed complaint or judgment?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-31", "type": "faq", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "No. A docket records entries and activity in a case. Request the underlying complaint, judgment, petition or other document separately where needed, identifying the case and entry when available.", "text": "Is a docket the same as the filed complaint or judgment? No. A docket records entries and activity in a case. Request the underlying complaint, judgment, petition or other document separately where needed, identifying the case and entry when available."}, {"title": "What does a court search with no located cases mean?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-32", "type": "faq", "topics": ["courts"], "summary": "A no-record result means no responsive case was located within the identified names, sources, criteria and period. Your report identifies the coverage and relevant access limits.", "text": "What does a court search with no located cases mean? A no-record result means no responsive case was located within the identified names, sources, criteria and period. Your report identifies the coverage and relevant access limits."}, {"title": "UCC Filing & Continuation Services", "url": "./ucc-filing-continuations.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Get your state UCC filings and county fixture filings coordinated, with filing evidence and clear follow-through for your matter.", "text": "UCC Filing & Continuation Services | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER UCC Filing & Continuation Services Get your state UCC filings and county fixture filings coordinated, with filing evidence and clear follow-through for your matter. Keep the Filing Work Moving. Coordinate your state UCC filings and county fixture filings, where applicable, through one point of contact. Keep authorized instructions, office responses and supporting evidence connected to your matter. Discuss Your Request Place an Order State and County Filing Support STATE LEVEL Central UCC Filings Initial financing statements and related UCC changes submitted to the responsible state filing office under authorized client instructions. COUNTY LEVEL · WHERE APPLICABLE Fixture Filings and Recording County or real-property filing work with the property, office, instrument and recording requirements identified for the request. Choose the Filing Action Initial UCC financing statement. Amendment or assignment tied to an identified filing. Continuation under confirmed timing and authorized instructions. Termination under authorized client instructions. County fixture filing or related recording, where applicable. A separately requested search to reflect the filing. What You Receive Your confirmed scope identifies the deliverables. Depending on the request, these can include: Submission response The responsible office’s filing acknowledgment, acceptance, rejection or pending response. Filing identifiers Available filing numbers, dates, office details and returned filed copies. Requested follow-up Any separately agreed search to reflect, supporting copies or response to a rejected submission. Open items Missing instructions, outstanding office responses or other items requiring follow-up. Office acceptance, searchable indexing and legal effect answer different questions. Your legal team determines the required filing and legal effect. Prepare Your Filing Request Exact debtor and secured-party details, the requested action and approved filing document or authorized preparation instructions. State filing office; county and property information for fixture or recording work. Original filing number and available history for changes to an existing financing statement. Requested evidence, copies, matter reference and deadline. Keep Continuation Dates in View Need help planning ongoing filing maintenance? Discuss the filings to track, relevant dates, authorized recipients and required follow-through before a tracking arrangement begins. Discuss Continuation Tracking → Start Your Next Filing Request Bring the filing action, jurisdiction, supporting documents and date needed. AADS confirms scope, fees and anticipated timing. Discuss Your Request Place an Order Answers for Your Next Request Can I request both state and county UCC filing work? Yes. Identify the state filing office and any county or real-property fixture filing separately, with the property and recording requirements where applicable. The correct office and requested action are confirmed for the matter. What do I need for an amendment, assignment, continuation or termination? Provide the initial financing-statement number, filing office, available filing history, requested action, authorized instructions and deadline. Include approved documents or details needed to prepare the submission. Does filing automatically include a search to reflect? Confirm the search separately. The office’s acknowledgment and a later search of its indexed records are different deliverables. Specify the debtor name, office, search through date and copy requirements. What is a transmitting utility UCC filing, and does it need continuations? Purpose: A UCC financing statement provides public notice of a claimed security interest in collateral. Transmitting utility is a special debtor classification for certain transportation, communications, pipeline and utility businesses. Where filed: A transmitting utility’s UCC financing statement is generally filed with the state’s designated central UCC filing office. Under the applicable transmitting-utility rule, that filing also serves as a fixture filing for the fixtures it identifies. Confirm the governing state’s requirements and any separate real-property recording needs. Duration: When the debtor qualifies and the filed initial financing statement indicates transmitting-utility status, the special duration rule keeps the financing statement effective until a termination statement is filed. It does not automatically lapse after five years under that rule. Continuations: Routine five-year continuation statements are not required for a financing statement governed by this continuing-effectiveness rule. Termination and review: An authorized termination statement ends the filing’s effectiveness. Confirm the debtor’s classification, the initial designation, governing-state rules and any later amendments or terminations with your legal team. Read the glossary definition → · Florida duration rule · North Carolina duration rule · Washington duration rule · Florida filing-office rule · North Carolina Article 9 — section 25-9-501 · Washington filing-office rule Official Filing References Washington provides one jurisdiction example. Confirm the applicable office and timing rules for each request. Washington RCW 62A.9A-501 — filing offices ↗ Washington RCW 62A.9A-515 — duration and continuation timing ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Confirm current requirements for the actual filing."}, {"title": "UCC & Lien Monitoring", "url": "./ucc-lien-monitoring.html", "type": "service", "topics": ["ucc-liens", "monitoring"], "summary": "Discuss continuation-date tracking and recurring record checks, with defined coverage, reporting and follow-through for your team.", "text": "UCC & Lien Monitoring | AADS Skip to main content AADS · PUBLIC RECORDS INTELLIGENCE CENTER UCC & Lien Monitoring Discuss continuation-date tracking and recurring record checks, with defined coverage, reporting and follow-through for your team. Keep Important Filing Dates and Record Changes in View. Discuss continuation-date tracking and recurring checks of specified UCC and lien records, with the coverage and follow-through defined around your portfolio. Discuss Monitoring Requirements Explore Filing Services Scope and availability are confirmed before enrollment. This website does not currently operate a live monitoring subscription or automated alert service. Two Distinct Monitoring Needs YOUR EXISTING FILINGS UCC Continuation Tracking Define the enrolled filings, verified lapse dates and continuation windows, reminder schedule, responsible contacts and escalation steps. Identify the initial filing and available filing history. Confirm dates and applicable timing rules. Agree on reminders, backup recipients and filing responsibility. CHANGES IN SPECIFIED RECORDS UCC & Lien Record Monitoring Define recurring checks for named subjects or property in the agreed sources, so new filings and specified record changes reach your reviewing team. Agree on subjects, jurisdictions and record categories. Confirm search frequency and source availability. Identify the evidence and updates your team needs. Agree on the Coverage Before It Begins Subjects and sources Exact names, filings or properties; state, county and other specifically agreed indexes. Dates and frequency Verified baseline records, filing dates, continuation windows and the schedule for recurring checks. Updates and evidence Dated findings, located references, requested copies and source limitations. Responsibility and follow-through Primary and backup recipients, escalation, client instructions and separately authorized filing work. Records for Your Team’s Lien-Position Review Defined record checks identify changes for your legal or credit team to assess against your lien position, with coverage and reporting frequency agreed in advance. Prepare for a Monitoring Discussion A list of borrowers, entities, properties or filings to consider. Available filing acknowledgments, filing numbers and history for continuation tracking. Requested jurisdictions, sources, record categories and search frequency. The people who should receive updates and act on unanswered reminders. Portfolio size, matter references and the reporting format your team needs. Discuss a Monitoring Arrangement Tell us what your team needs to keep in view. Confirm a workable scope and responsibilities before any recurring arrangement starts. Discuss Monitoring Requirements Explore Filing Services Answers for Your Next Request Is continuation tracking the same as filing a continuation? No. Tracking and reminders identify dates and required follow-up. Filing requires authorized instructions and confirmation of the office’s response. The agreed arrangement must identify who is responsible for each step. Does monitoring establish or guarantee lien priority? No. Defined record checks can identify filings or other changes for review. Your legal or credit team determines their relevance and effect on lien position. Source coverage, timing and indexing limits remain part of the scope. Does discussing monitoring enroll my filings or start alerts? No. Scope, source availability, frequency, recipients, fees and responsibility must be confirmed before enrollment. This website does not currently operate a live monitoring subscription or automated alert service. Official Filing References Washington provides one jurisdiction example. Confirm the applicable office and timing rules for each request. Washington RCW 62A.9A-501 — filing offices ↗ Washington RCW 62A.9A-515 — duration and continuation timing ↗ Sources checked 10 November 2026. Confirm current requirements for the actual filing."}, {"title": "UCC-11 (UCC11)", "url": "./glossary.html#term-ucc-11-ucc11", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A form submitted, where accepted, to the appropriate UCC filing office to request an official search of its UCC records and/or specified copies. The office processes the request through its staff or system. The completed form is the request; the returned search response is the result. Confirm scope, certification, copies and the current submission process.", "text": "UCC-11 (UCC11) A form submitted, where accepted, to the appropriate UCC filing office to request an official search of its UCC records and/or specified copies. The office processes the request through its staff or system. The completed form is the request; the returned search response is the result. Confirm scope, certification, copies and the current submission process."}, {"title": "Transmitting Utility (UCC)", "url": "./glossary.html#term-transmitting-utility-ucc", "type": "term", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "A special UCC debtor classification for certain transportation, communications, pipeline and utility businesses. It can affect filing location and financing-statement duration. Washington, for example, provides that an initial financing statement identifying a qualifying transmitting utility remains effective until a termination statement is filed. Confirm the classification and governing-state rules.", "text": "Transmitting Utility (UCC) A special UCC debtor classification for certain transportation, communications, pipeline and utility businesses. It can affect filing location and financing-statement duration. Washington, for example, provides that an initial financing statement identifying a qualifying transmitting utility remains effective until a termination statement is filed. Confirm the classification and governing-state rules."}, {"title": "What is UCC11 (UCC-11), and what should I request?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-33", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "UCC11 (UCC-11) is a formal information request submitted, where accepted, to the appropriate UCC filing office for an official search of that office’s records and/or specified copies. The office conducts or processes the search through its staff or official system. The returned response and requested copies are the deliverables. Provide the exact debtor name, filing office, required scope, certification, copies and search-date requirements. The responsible office may be the Secretary of State or another agency; confirm current submission procedures.", "text": "What is UCC11 (UCC-11), and what should I request? UCC11 (UCC-11) is a formal information request submitted, where accepted, to the appropriate UCC filing office for an official search of that office’s records and/or specified copies. The office conducts or processes the search through its staff or official system. The returned response and requested copies are the deliverables. Provide the exact debtor name, filing office, required scope, certification, copies and search-date requirements. The responsible office may be the Secretary of State or another agency; confirm current submission procedures."}, {"title": "What is a transmitting utility UCC filing, and does it need continuations?", "url": "./faq.html#faq-34", "type": "faq", "topics": ["ucc-liens"], "summary": "Purpose: A UCC financing statement provides public notice of a claimed security interest in collateral. Transmitting utility is a special debtor classification for certain transportation, communications, pipeline and utility businesses. Where filed: A transmitting utility’s UCC financing statement is generally filed with the state’s designated central UCC filing office. Under the applicable transmitting-utility rule, that filing also serves as a fixture filing for the fixtures it identifies. Confirm the governing state’s requirements and any separate real-property recording needs. Duration: When the debtor qualifies and the filed initial financing statement indicates transmitting-utility status, the special duration rule keeps the financing statement effective until a termination statement is filed. It does not automatically lapse after five years under that rule. Continuations: Routine five-year continuation statements are not required for a financing statement governed by this continuing-effectiveness rule. Termination and review: An authorized termination statement ends the filing’s effectiveness. Confirm the debtor’s classification, the initial designation, governing-state rules and any later amendments or terminations with your legal team.", "text": "What is a transmitting utility UCC filing, and does it need continuations? Purpose: A UCC financing statement provides public notice of a claimed security interest in collateral. Transmitting utility is a special debtor classification for certain transportation, communications, pipeline and utility businesses. Where filed: A transmitting utility’s UCC financing statement is generally filed with the state’s designated central UCC filing office. Under the applicable transmitting-utility rule, that filing also serves as a fixture filing for the fixtures it identifies. Confirm the governing state’s requirements and any separate real-property recording needs. Duration: When the debtor qualifies and the filed initial financing statement indicates transmitting-utility status, the special duration rule keeps the financing statement effective until a termination statement is filed. It does not automatically lapse after five years under that rule. Continuations: Routine five-year continuation statements are not required for a financing statement governed by this continuing-effectiveness rule. Termination and review: An authorized termination statement ends the filing’s effectiveness. Confirm the debtor’s classification, the initial designation, governing-state rules and any later amendments or terminations with your legal team."}, {"title": "50-State Research and Filing Guides", "url": "./state-guides.html", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "general"], "summary": "AADS coordinates research, filings and document retrieval across all 50 states. Explore guidance for your jurisdiction or discuss the scope your matter requires.", "text": "AADS coordinates research, filings and document retrieval across all 50 states. Explore guidance for your jurisdiction or discuss the scope your matter requires. Alabama AL Alaska AK Arizona AZ Arkansas AR California CA Colorado CO Connecticut CT Delaware DE Florida FL Georgia GA Hawaii HI Idaho ID Illinois IL Indiana IN Iowa IA Kansas KS Kentucky KY Louisiana LA Maine ME Maryland MD Massachusetts MA Michigan MI Minnesota MN Mississippi MS Missouri MO Montana MT Nebraska NE Nevada NV New Hampshire NH New Jersey NJ New Mexico NM New York NY North Carolina NC North Dakota ND Ohio OH Oklahoma OK Oregon OR Pennsylvania PA Rhode Island RI South Carolina SC South Dakota SD Tennessee TN Texas TX Utah UT Vermont VT Virginia VA Washington WA West Virginia WV Wisconsin WI Wyoming WY"}, {"title": "Alabama Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-alabama", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Alabama’s Secretary of State describes both an online advanced search and an office search requested on UCC-11. Its UCC guidance also addresses copies of individual filings and amendments tied to an initial filing number.", "text": "Alabama AL Alabama Secretary of State Alabama Secretary of State, Business Services Alabama’s Secretary of State describes both an online advanced search and an office search requested on UCC-11. Its UCC guidance also addresses copies of individual filings and amendments tied to an initial filing number. Specify whether an office search response, individual filing copies or all copies under the requested debtor name are needed."}, {"title": "Alaska Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-alaska", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Alaska’s UCC Central File is administered by Natural Resources, while corporate records are handled by Commerce. The UCC resource separates certified searches, online filing and copy requests.", "text": "Alaska AK Alaska Department of Natural Resources — UCC Central File Alaska Department of Commerce, Community, and Economic Development, Division of Corporations, Business and Professional Licensing Alaska’s UCC Central File is administered by Natural Resources, while corporate records are handled by Commerce. The UCC resource separates certified searches, online filing and copy requests. Identify the statewide UCC component separately from the recording district and any historical property-record research."}, {"title": "Arizona Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-arizona", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Arizona separates UCC work at the Secretary of State from corporation and LLC records at the Corporation Commission. The UCC office’s guidance distinguishes public, uncertified results from a certified search request.", "text": "Arizona AZ Arizona Secretary of State Arizona Corporation Commission Arizona separates UCC work at the Secretary of State from corporation and LLC records at the Corporation Commission. The UCC office’s guidance distinguishes public, uncertified results from a certified search request. Specify the exact debtor name, the required certification and whether you need a listing, supporting copies or both."}, {"title": "Arkansas Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-arkansas", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Arkansas’s Secretary of State directs UCC services to its Business and Commercial Services portal. Its filing tips emphasize entering debtor and secured-party names as presented and identifying the action requested on an amendment.", "text": "Arkansas AR Arkansas Secretary of State Arkansas Secretary of State, Business Services Arkansas’s Secretary of State directs UCC services to its Business and Commercial Services portal. Its filing tips emphasize entering debtor and secured-party names as presented and identifying the action requested on an amendment. Provide the exact debtor names and initial filing references, and identify the requested search response and supporting copies."}, {"title": "California Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-california", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "California’s Secretary of State identifies UCC Connect for inquiries, filings, copies and debtor search certificates. Its guidance also identifies additional lien categories maintained by the office.", "text": "California CA California Secretary of State California Secretary of State, Business Services California’s Secretary of State identifies UCC Connect for inquiries, filings, copies and debtor search certificates. Its guidance also identifies additional lien categories maintained by the office. Specify the UCC and other lien categories requested, whether a debtor search certificate is needed, and which filed documents to retrieve."}, {"title": "Colorado Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-colorado", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Colorado’s UCC resource separates debtor-name searching, electronic filing and validation of a search report or certified document. Its filing page states that UCC forms must be submitted online.", "text": "Colorado CO Colorado Secretary of State Colorado Secretary of State, Business Services Colorado’s UCC resource separates debtor-name searching, electronic filing and validation of a search report or certified document. Its filing page states that UCC forms must be submitted online. Identify the exact debtor search and certificate needed, retain report-validation details, and supply the instructions for any separate filing."}, {"title": "Connecticut Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-connecticut", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Connecticut’s business portal distinguishes searching UCC lien records, requesting information and obtaining certified lien copies. The Secretary of the State’s guidance routes online transactions through a Business.CT.gov account.", "text": "Connecticut CT Connecticut Secretary of the State Connecticut Secretary of the State Connecticut’s business portal distinguishes searching UCC lien records, requesting information and obtaining certified lien copies. The Secretary of the State’s guidance routes online transactions through a Business.CT.gov account. Specify a search response versus certified copies, and provide the town separately for any land-record component."}, {"title": "Delaware Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-delaware", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Delaware’s Division of Corporations directs UCC filings to electronic channels and identifies authorized filers for paper-originated submissions. Its guidance separates filing services, UCC searches and entity-status services.", "text": "Delaware DE Delaware Secretary of State Delaware Department of State, Division of Corporations Delaware’s Division of Corporations directs UCC filings to electronic channels and identifies authorized filers for paper-originated submissions. Its guidance separates filing services, UCC searches and entity-status services. List the debtor names and required search evidence, and supply accepted filing instructions separately from a corporate-status request."}, {"title": "Hawaii Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-hawaii", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Hawaii’s Bureau of Conveyances handles UCC requests as well as its statewide land-record systems. Its guidance distinguishes Regular System, Land Court and documents recorded in both systems.", "text": "Hawaii HI Hawaii Department of Land and Natural Resources — Bureau of Conveyances Hawaii Department of Commerce and Consumer Affairs, Business Registration Division Hawaii’s Bureau of Conveyances handles UCC requests as well as its statewide land-record systems. Its guidance distinguishes Regular System, Land Court and documents recorded in both systems. Provide the property and recording-system references when known, and distinguish a UCC information request from a deed, mortgage or Land Court document request."}, {"title": "Idaho Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-idaho", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Idaho’s Secretary of State identifies SOSBiz for UCC/lien searches and filing services, with a separate copy-request route for certified searches. Its guidance also distinguishes UCC, agricultural and other lien resources.", "text": "Idaho ID Idaho Secretary of State Idaho Secretary of State, Business Services Idaho’s Secretary of State identifies SOSBiz for UCC/lien searches and filing services, with a separate copy-request route for certified searches. Its guidance also distinguishes UCC, agricultural and other lien resources. Specify the lien categories and certification needed, and identify whether farm-product or other specialized records are part of the request."}, {"title": "Illinois Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-illinois", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Illinois offers a Secretary of State index of UCC and federal tax lien filings. The official search page explains that filing images are not displayed online and directs requests for certified results with images to the UCC-11 process.", "text": "Illinois IL Illinois Secretary of State Illinois Secretary of State, Business Services Illinois offers a Secretary of State index of UCC and federal tax lien filings. The official search page explains that filing images are not displayed online and directs requests for certified results with images to the UCC-11 process. State whether your team needs an index result or a certified search with filing images; identify each debtor name and requested filing numbers."}, {"title": "Indiana Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-indiana", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Indiana’s INBiz distinguishes a free UCC browse from purchasing an official search certificate and images. The guidance warns that a search for one name does not automatically return nicknames, misspellings or assumed names.", "text": "Indiana IN Indiana Secretary of State Indiana Secretary of State, Business Services Indiana’s INBiz distinguishes a free UCC browse from purchasing an official search certificate and images. The guidance warns that a search for one name does not automatically return nicknames, misspellings or assumed names. List each additional debtor name separately and distinguish preliminary browsing from the official search certificate and images required."}, {"title": "Iowa Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-iowa", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Iowa’s official certified UCC/federal lien search identifies its search logic and displayed through date. Its UCC search options distinguish unlapsed filings from the option to include filings that lapsed within the past year.", "text": "Iowa IA Iowa Secretary of State Iowa Secretary of State, Business Services Iowa’s official certified UCC/federal lien search identifies its search logic and displayed through date. Its UCC search options distinguish unlapsed filings from the option to include filings that lapsed within the past year. Specify UCC and federal lien components separately, whether the available lapsed-record option is required, and which supporting copies to obtain."}, {"title": "Kansas Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-kansas", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Kansas publishes separate online and paper UCC information-request procedures and identifies its UCCII debtor search and supporting-image options. Its guidance treats filing, search requests and copies as separate instructions.", "text": "Kansas KS Kansas Secretary of State Kansas Secretary of State, Business Services Kansas publishes separate online and paper UCC information-request procedures and identifies its UCCII debtor search and supporting-image options. Its guidance treats filing, search requests and copies as separate instructions. Identify each debtor search, the required collateral images and any authorized UCC-1 or UCC-3 filing work."}, {"title": "Kentucky Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-kentucky", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Kentucky’s Secretary of State describes its UCC division as the state repository and provides UCC searches, standardized forms and online UCC-1/UCC-3 filing services. Its guidance addresses acknowledgment delivery for paper submissions.", "text": "Kentucky KY Kentucky Secretary of State Kentucky Secretary of State, Business Services Kentucky’s Secretary of State describes its UCC division as the state repository and provides UCC searches, standardized forms and online UCC-1/UCC-3 filing services. Its guidance addresses acknowledgment delivery for paper submissions. Specify the search findings and supporting copies needed, and provide acknowledgment-delivery details for any filing request."}, {"title": "Louisiana Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-louisiana", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Louisiana’s Secretary of State explains that UCC filings go through parish clerks rather than being filed directly with the Secretary of State. Initial statements may be filed through a Louisiana parish clerk; amendments are directed to the original filing office.", "text": "Louisiana LA Louisiana parish clerks — filings; Secretary of State — statewide UCC guidance Louisiana Secretary of State Louisiana’s Secretary of State explains that UCC filings go through parish clerks rather than being filed directly with the Secretary of State. Initial statements may be filed through a Louisiana parish clerk; amendments are directed to the original filing office. Provide the original filing number and parish filing office for amendments. Identify parish property records and court research as separate components."}, {"title": "Maine Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-maine", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Maine distinguishes its official debtor search from an unofficial search used to identify name variations. The official services page separates searches, filing services and requests for forms or rules.", "text": "Maine ME Maine Secretary of State Maine Secretary of State Maine distinguishes its official debtor search from an unofficial search used to identify name variations. The official services page separates searches, filing services and requests for forms or rules. Identify whether you need an official search, certification and copies, and list additional name variations as separate instructions."}, {"title": "Maryland Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-maryland", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Maryland UCC services are administered through SDAT rather than the Secretary of State. The official portal distinguishes a search response listing associated filings from a document request supplying the filed copies.", "text": "Maryland MD Maryland State Department of Assessments and Taxation Maryland State Department of Assessments and Taxation (SDAT) Maryland UCC services are administered through SDAT rather than the Secretary of State. The official portal distinguishes a search response listing associated filings from a document request supplying the filed copies. Request the response and supporting documents as separate deliverables, and specify certification rather than treating an entity-status lookup as a UCC search."}, {"title": "Massachusetts Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-massachusetts", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Massachusetts UCC search rules address the debtor name, the report date and through date, and the related filings and copies requested. The Secretary of the Commonwealth publishes separate UCC information-request and registry-of-deeds resources.", "text": "Massachusetts MA Massachusetts Secretary of the Commonwealth Massachusetts Secretary of the Commonwealth Massachusetts UCC search rules address the debtor name, the report date and through date, and the related filings and copies requested. The Secretary of the Commonwealth publishes separate UCC information-request and registry-of-deeds resources. Supply the exact debtor name, certification and copy requirements. For property research, identify the registry district serving the city or town."}, {"title": "Michigan Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-michigan", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Michigan’s Secretary of State distinguishes a debtor quick search from a UCC-11 search, with options for all available or unlapsed records. Its guidance distinguishes certification from an additional official seal; entity records are handled by LARA.", "text": "Michigan MI Michigan Department of State — Secretary of State Michigan Department of Licensing and Regulatory Affairs (LARA) Michigan’s Secretary of State distinguishes a debtor quick search from a UCC-11 search, with options for all available or unlapsed records. Its guidance distinguishes certification from an additional official seal; entity records are handled by LARA. Specify the record-status scope, copies and seal requirement, and route corporate records separately from the UCC request."}, {"title": "Minnesota Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-minnesota", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Minnesota’s Secretary of State publishes separate guidance for UCC, tax lien and CNS farm-product records, including standard and limited search requests. Those categories should be named in the order scope.", "text": "Minnesota MN Minnesota Secretary of State Minnesota Secretary of State Minnesota’s Secretary of State publishes separate guidance for UCC, tax lien and CNS farm-product records, including standard and limited search requests. Those categories should be named in the order scope. Identify the indexes and search type needed and specify supporting copies. State any farm-product records as a separate component."}, {"title": "Mississippi Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-mississippi", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Mississippi’s public UCC search provides basic creditor and debtor information. The Secretary of State’s guidance distinguishes that lookup from its subscription search for more detailed research and viewing filed documents.", "text": "Mississippi MS Mississippi Secretary of State Mississippi Secretary of State Mississippi’s public UCC search provides basic creditor and debtor information. The Secretary of State’s guidance distinguishes that lookup from its subscription search for more detailed research and viewing filed documents. Specify whether the request needs a basic listing, document images or an official response and certification."}, {"title": "Missouri Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-missouri", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Missouri’s Secretary of State distinguishes central UCC records from real-estate-related filings handled by county recorders of deeds. Its guidance separately discusses fixture, timber and as-extracted collateral filings.", "text": "Missouri MO Missouri Secretary of State Missouri Secretary of State Missouri’s Secretary of State distinguishes central UCC records from real-estate-related filings handled by county recorders of deeds. Its guidance separately discusses fixture, timber and as-extracted collateral filings. Name the central and county record components required and identify the property, county and supporting copies for local research."}, {"title": "Montana Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-montana", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Montana’s lien guidance addresses exact debtor names, original filing numbers for amendments and online access to filing chains, debtor search certificates and lien images. It separates UCC and farm-product/EFS information.", "text": "Montana MT Montana Secretary of State Montana Secretary of State Montana’s lien guidance addresses exact debtor names, original filing numbers for amendments and online access to filing chains, debtor search certificates and lien images. It separates UCC and farm-product/EFS information. Provide the exact debtor name and initial filing references; specify the certificate, filing chain and images required."}, {"title": "Nebraska Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-nebraska", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Nebraska’s Secretary of State directs UCC, EFS, searches and document-image services to its business portal. It publishes a UCC-11 standard-search form and separate EFS resources.", "text": "Nebraska NE Nebraska Secretary of State Nebraska Secretary of State Nebraska’s Secretary of State directs UCC, EFS, searches and document-image services to its business portal. It publishes a UCC-11 standard-search form and separate EFS resources. Identify the UCC or EFS component, requested search response, images and copy requirements instead of using one general lien label."}, {"title": "Nevada Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-nevada", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Nevada’s Secretary of State publishes UCC forms and information-request options and directs online transactions to ORION. Its guidance lists searching, copying and certification as distinct services.", "text": "Nevada NV Nevada Secretary of State Nevada Secretary of State Nevada’s Secretary of State publishes UCC forms and information-request options and directs online transactions to ORION. Its guidance lists searching, copying and certification as distinct services. Identify the requested UCC-11 response, documents and certification, and confirm the submission channel for the actual request."}, {"title": "New Hampshire Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-new-hampshire", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "New Hampshire publishes a state UCC 11-U information request and distinguishes search responses with or without copies. Its filing guidance also identifies the acknowledgment-return information needed on a submission.", "text": "New Hampshire NH New Hampshire Secretary of State New Hampshire Secretary of State New Hampshire publishes a state UCC 11-U information request and distinguishes search responses with or without copies. Its filing guidance also identifies the acknowledgment-return information needed on a submission. Specify the search response, copy scope and any service-level request; provide the correct acknowledgment or delivery details."}, {"title": "New Jersey Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-new-jersey", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "New Jersey UCC services are provided through the Treasury’s Division of Revenue and Enterprise Services. Its portal distinguishes certified and non-certified searches, document downloads, new filings and amendments.", "text": "New Jersey NJ New Jersey Treasury — Division of Revenue and Enterprise Services New Jersey Department of the Treasury, Division of Revenue and Enterprise Services New Jersey UCC services are provided through the Treasury’s Division of Revenue and Enterprise Services. Its portal distinguishes certified and non-certified searches, document downloads, new filings and amendments. Specify the certified or non-certified response required, the debtor names and documents, and any separate entity-certificate request."}, {"title": "New Mexico Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-new-mexico", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "New Mexico’s Secretary of State separates commercial UCC and agricultural lien resources from its business-entity services. Its UCC guidance page links the UCC portal and identifies bulk-filing guides and archived materials.", "text": "New Mexico NM New Mexico Secretary of State New Mexico Secretary of State New Mexico’s Secretary of State separates commercial UCC and agricultural lien resources from its business-entity services. Its UCC guidance page links the UCC portal and identifies bulk-filing guides and archived materials. State whether the request concerns UCC records, agricultural liens, corporate records or a combination, and identify the required result and copies."}, {"title": "New York Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-new-york", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "New York’s Department of State explains UCC information requests and requests for individual filing copies. Its guidance states that a copy request needs the UCC filing number and distinguishes special financing-statement categories.", "text": "New York NY New York Department of State New York Department of State New York’s Department of State explains UCC information requests and requests for individual filing copies. Its guidance states that a copy request needs the UCC filing number and distinguishes special financing-statement categories. Provide filing numbers for specific copies, identify the required search response and separate county property and court components."}, {"title": "North Dakota Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-north-dakota", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "North Dakota’s Central Indexing System distinguishes public search, UCC search, UCC-11 and secured-party search options. It maintains separate indexes for UCC, farm products, statutory liens, state tax liens and federal liens.", "text": "North Dakota ND North Dakota Secretary of State North Dakota Secretary of State North Dakota’s Central Indexing System distinguishes public search, UCC search, UCC-11 and secured-party search options. It maintains separate indexes for UCC, farm products, statutory liens, state tax liens and federal liens. Name the indexes required and the search type; a public lookup should not be substituted for a requested certified response."}, {"title": "Ohio Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-ohio", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Ohio’s Secretary of State provides UCC filing, database searching and official search-certificate services through its UCC online resources. It also publishes the administrative rules for filing and searching.", "text": "Ohio OH Ohio Secretary of State Ohio Secretary of State Ohio’s Secretary of State provides UCC filing, database searching and official search-certificate services through its UCC online resources. It also publishes the administrative rules for filing and searching. Specify the debtor names, required official search certificate and supporting copies; describe filing work as a separate authorized instruction."}, {"title": "Oklahoma Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-oklahoma", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Oklahoma County Clerk identifies its office as the central location for most Oklahoma UCC filings, while transmitting utilities and EFS filings go to the Secretary of State. Its guidance separately addresses real-estate fixture filings.", "text": "Oklahoma OK Oklahoma County Clerk — central UCC filing office; special categories use other offices Oklahoma Secretary of State Oklahoma County Clerk identifies its office as the central location for most Oklahoma UCC filings, while transmitting utilities and EFS filings go to the Secretary of State. Its guidance separately addresses real-estate fixture filings. Identify the filing category first, retain the original office and filing number, and separately define any county fixture or property records."}, {"title": "Oregon Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-oregon", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Oregon’s Secretary of State separates UCC record searches, copy requests, filing resources and farm-product information. It publishes received/processed information so source timing can be distinguished from the date of the request.", "text": "Oregon OR Oregon Secretary of State Oregon Secretary of State Oregon’s Secretary of State separates UCC record searches, copy requests, filing resources and farm-product information. It publishes received/processed information so source timing can be distinguished from the date of the request. Specify the requested record search, copies and any farm-product component, and ask that the source through date be identified."}, {"title": "Pennsylvania Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-pennsylvania", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Pennsylvania’s Department of State publishes UCC filing, search and copy procedures. Its guidance addresses attachments and debtor indexing and explains that debtor information should appear in the proper form fields.", "text": "Pennsylvania PA Pennsylvania Department of State Pennsylvania Department of State Pennsylvania’s Department of State publishes UCC filing, search and copy procedures. Its guidance addresses attachments and debtor indexing and explains that debtor information should appear in the proper form fields. Provide complete debtor information and attachments, and specify the search response, supporting copies and certification required."}, {"title": "Rhode Island Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-rhode-island", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Rhode Island’s Department of State distinguishes database searches by Article 9, begins-with or exact-match options from a certified UCC-11 search request. Land evidence records are maintained separately by the responsible city or town.", "text": "Rhode Island RI Rhode Island Secretary of State Rhode Island Secretary of State Rhode Island’s Department of State distinguishes database searches by Article 9, begins-with or exact-match options from a certified UCC-11 search request. Land evidence records are maintained separately by the responsible city or town. Specify the search type and certification; identify the municipality for land evidence research rather than treating a county name as sufficient."}, {"title": "South Carolina Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-south-carolina", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "South Carolina’s Secretary of State identifies its UCC Electronic Filing, Search and Retrieval system and publishes a UCC-11 office-search request. Its guidance also provides a filing-submission form for an emailed acknowledgment.", "text": "South Carolina SC South Carolina Secretary of State South Carolina Secretary of State South Carolina’s Secretary of State identifies its UCC Electronic Filing, Search and Retrieval system and publishes a UCC-11 office-search request. Its guidance also provides a filing-submission form for an emailed acknowledgment. Identify debtor or secured-party search instructions, required copies and the return acknowledgment for any filing work."}, {"title": "South Dakota Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-south-dakota", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "South Dakota publishes UCC and EFS filing tools and a UCC2 certified search/copy request, rather than labeling every information request UCC-11. Its portal separates subscriber transactions from forms prepared for mailing.", "text": "South Dakota SD South Dakota Secretary of State South Dakota Secretary of State South Dakota publishes UCC and EFS filing tools and a UCC2 certified search/copy request, rather than labeling every information request UCC-11. Its portal separates subscriber transactions from forms prepared for mailing. Specify the certified-search and copy deliverables, identify UCC versus EFS records and confirm the request channel."}, {"title": "Tennessee Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-tennessee", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Tennessee’s Secretary of State publishes UCC search and filing guidance, including filing-number requirements, rejection reasons and indebtedness-tax instructions for applicable filings.", "text": "Tennessee TN Tennessee Secretary of State Tennessee Secretary of State Tennessee’s Secretary of State publishes UCC search and filing guidance, including filing-number requirements, rejection reasons and indebtedness-tax instructions for applicable filings. Provide the initial filing number for amendments and the filing instructions and tax-related information required for the particular submission."}, {"title": "Texas Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-texas", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Texas’s Secretary of State directs UCC filings and access to accepted records through SOSPortal. Its guidance states that paper UCC filings are no longer accepted and distinguishes filing history from records requested for review.", "text": "Texas TX Texas Secretary of State Texas Secretary of State Texas’s Secretary of State directs UCC filings and access to accepted records through SOSPortal. Its guidance states that paper UCC filings are no longer accepted and distinguishes filing history from records requested for review. Provide electronic filing instructions and attachments, and identify the search, copies or certificates required separately from an acknowledgment."}, {"title": "Utah Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-utah", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Utah’s Division of Corporations and Commercial Code directs UCC/CFS filing and searches to its online service. Its guidance distinguishes displayed searchable filing status from the filing history and directs information requests through UCC-11.", "text": "Utah UT Utah Division of Corporations and Commercial Code Utah Division of Corporations and Commercial Code Utah’s Division of Corporations and Commercial Code directs UCC/CFS filing and searches to its online service. Its guidance distinguishes displayed searchable filing status from the filing history and directs information requests through UCC-11. Request the filing history and copies needed to assess amendments or terminations, rather than relying on an active-status label alone."}, {"title": "Vermont Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-vermont", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Vermont distinguishes uncertified database searches from certified UCC-11 reports, with or without images. Its guidance also separates standard search logic from non-standard name and filing-number searches.", "text": "Vermont VT Vermont Secretary of State Vermont Secretary of State Vermont distinguishes uncertified database searches from certified UCC-11 reports, with or without images. Its guidance also separates standard search logic from non-standard name and filing-number searches. Specify the report type, certification and images needed, and identify the town for a separate land-record request."}, {"title": "Virginia Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-virginia", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Virginia’s State Corporation Commission directs UCC filings to the Clerk’s Information System and provides UCC/federal tax lien searches. Circuit court land records and litigation records are separate sources.", "text": "Virginia VA Virginia State Corporation Commission Virginia State Corporation Commission Virginia’s State Corporation Commission directs UCC filings to the Clerk’s Information System and provides UCC/federal tax lien searches. Circuit court land records and litigation records are separate sources. Separate SCC UCC work from the relevant circuit court or other court, and identify the city or county and documents needed."}, {"title": "West Virginia Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-west-virginia", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "West Virginia’s online UCC system separates certified and non-certified searches, filing-document downloads, new filings and amendments. It also provides validation of certified searches using a code on the certificate.", "text": "West Virginia WV West Virginia Secretary of State West Virginia Secretary of State West Virginia’s online UCC system separates certified and non-certified searches, filing-document downloads, new filings and amendments. It also provides validation of certified searches using a code on the certificate. Specify the response and copies required, retain certificate-validation details, and identify any filing work separately."}, {"title": "Wisconsin Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-wisconsin", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Wisconsin’s Department of Financial Institutions maintains the statewide UCC lien system through its Division of Corporate and Consumer Services. Its guidance provides separate online filing, searching and data-service resources.", "text": "Wisconsin WI Wisconsin Department of Financial Institutions Wisconsin Department of Financial Institutions Wisconsin’s Department of Financial Institutions maintains the statewide UCC lien system through its Division of Corporate and Consumer Services. Its guidance provides separate online filing, searching and data-service resources. Specify the DFI UCC search and copies, any corporate records and separate county or court research components."}, {"title": "Wyoming Research and Filing Guide", "url": "./state-guides.html#state-wyoming", "type": "guide", "topics": ["states", "ucc-liens", "corporate", "courts", "property"], "summary": "Wyoming’s Secretary of State separates UCC/EFS filing resources, search-logic guidance and buyer-registration materials from its business-entity and certificate services.", "text": "Wyoming WY Wyoming Secretary of State Wyoming Secretary of State Wyoming’s Secretary of State separates UCC/EFS filing resources, search-logic guidance and buyer-registration materials from its business-entity and certificate services. Specify whether UCC, EFS or other records are needed, identify the exact debtor names, and request entity certificates separately."}];
